window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"readme": "# 🎯 NISM Series X-A: Investment Adviser (Level 1) — Complete Study Guide\n\n> **Everything you need to clear the exam — explained from first principles, chapter by chapter.**\n> Built to the official NISM-Series-X-A syllabus and test objectives (6 modules · 20 chapters · 150 marks).\n\n---\n\n## 📋 What this exam is actually about (read this first!)\n\nAn **Investment Adviser** is a financial doctor. A doctor examines your body and prescribes medicine. An Investment Adviser examines your **money situation** — what you earn, what you owe, what you're saving for (a house, a child's education, retirement), and how much loss you can stomach — and then prescribes the right financial \"medicines.\"\n\n**Level 1 (Series X-A)** is the *foundation* exam. It tests whether you can:\n\n1. **Plan** — build a personal financial plan, do the maths of money (time value, EMIs, ratios).\n2. **Know the market** — how India's financial system and securities markets are wired, and who regulates whom.\n3. **Know the products** — equity, bonds, derivatives, mutual funds, PMS, AIFs.\n4. **Build a portfolio** — asset allocation, Modern Portfolio Theory, and measuring whether the portfolio actually performed.\n5. **Operate legally and ethically** — KYC and onboarding, the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, ethics, and how a client complains about you.\n\n> **Level 1 vs Level 2 in one line:** Level 1 (X-A) is *markets, products, portfolios and rules*. Level 2 (X-B) is *insurance, retirement, tax and estate planning*. You must pass **both** to be a registered Investment Adviser under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Exam Structure (know this cold — it changes how you study)\n\n| Part | What | Marks |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **Independent MCQs** | 90 questions × 1 mark | **90** |\n| **Caselets (1-mark)** | 6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark | **30** |\n| **Caselets (2-mark)** | 3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks | **30** |\n| **TOTAL** | **105 questions** | **150** |\n\n- ⏱️ **Time:** 3 hours\n- ✅ **Passing score:** 60% — i.e. **90 out of 150 marks**\n- ⚠️ **Negative marking:** 25% of the marks for that question. A wrong 1-mark MCQ costs **−0.25**; a wrong 2-mark caselet answer costs **−0.5**.\n- 💰 **Fee:** ₹3,000 (check nism.ac.in for the current fee)\n- 🧮 **Calculator:** the test machine has a spreadsheet — practise `=PV()`, `=FV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()` until they're automatic.\n\n### 🎯 Where the marks actually live\n\n| Module | Chapters | Marks | Share |\n|--------|----------|-------|-------|\n| 1 · Personal Financial Planning | 1–4 | **37** | 24.7% |\n| 2 · Indian Financial Markets | 5–6 | **10** | 6.7% |\n| 3 · Investment Products | 7–10 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio | 11–13 | **23** | 15.3% |\n| 5 · Portfolio Construction, Monitoring & Evaluation | 14–16 | **20** | 13.3% |\n| 6 · Operations, Regulation, Compliance & Ethics | 17–20 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| | | **150** | 100% |\n\n> **Read that table again.** Module 1 alone is **37 marks** — more than any other — and it is almost entirely *numerical* (time value of money, EMIs, financial ratios). Module 6 is another **30 marks** and is almost entirely *recall* (regulations, ethics, grievance routes). Those two modules together are **45% of the paper**, and they are the two easiest to score in — one with a calculator, the other with flashcards.\n>\n> Module 2 is only **10 marks** — don't over-invest a week in it.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 How to use this study guide\n\nEach chapter folder contains **three files**:\n\n| File | What it is | How to use it |\n|------|-----------|---------------|\n| `notes.md` | **Short Notes** — the whole chapter explained plainly, with every formula *and the logic behind it* | Read first, 2–3 times |\n| `flashcards.md` | **Flashcards** — question on one side, answer on the other | Revise daily; cover the answer and test yourself |\n| `questions.md` | **Question Bank — 100 MCQs**, each with a full explanation (the formula used, *and* why every wrong option is wrong) + a worked caselet | Attempt *after* reading the notes |\n\nThat's **~2,000 practice MCQs + 20 worked caselets** across the guide. Each `questions.md` is organised into three difficulty tiers so you build up gradually:\n\n- 🟢 **Tier 1 — Easy (Recall):** definitions and facts (≈Q1–35)\n- 🟡 **Tier 2 — Medium (Application):** \"which rule applies here\", comparisons (≈Q36–70)\n- 🔴 **Tier 3 — Hard (Numerical & Scenario):** multi-step calculations and exam-style scenarios (≈Q71–100)\n\nEvery answer sits inside a collapsible *\"Answer & Explanation\"* block — attempt first, then expand to see **why the right option is right, why the others are wrong, and the exact calculation behind it.**\n\n**Plus, across the whole guide:**\n\n- 📑 **10 full-length mock papers** — each built to the exact 150-mark blueprint (90 MCQs + 6 one-mark caselets + 3 two-mark caselets), auto-scored with real negative marking and a 3-hour timer.\n- 🔢 **Financial Maths Primer** — every formula in the syllabus, derived and worked.\n- 📊 **Excel/Calc TVM guide** — the spreadsheet functions you'll actually use in the exam hall.\n\n**Recommended cycle per chapter:**\n> Read `notes.md` → drill `flashcards.md` → attempt `questions.md` (Easy → Medium → Hard) → re-read the notes for anything you got wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Chapter Map\n\n### Module 1: Personal Financial Planning — *37 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 1 | Introduction to Personal Financial Planning | [`chapter-01`](chapter-01/) |\n| 2 | Time Value of Money | [`chapter-02`](chapter-02/) |\n| 3 | Cash Flow Management and Budgeting | [`chapter-03`](chapter-03/) |\n| 4 | Debt Management and Loans | [`chapter-04`](chapter-04/) |\n\n### Module 2: Indian Financial Markets — *10 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 5 | Introduction to Indian Financial Markets | [`chapter-05`](chapter-05/) |\n| 6 | Securities Market Segments | [`chapter-06`](chapter-06/) |\n\n### Module 3: Investment Products — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 7 | Introduction to Investments | [`chapter-07`](chapter-07/) |\n| 8 | Investing in Stocks | [`chapter-08`](chapter-08/) |\n| 9 | Investing in Fixed Income Securities | [`chapter-09`](chapter-09/) |\n| 10 | Understanding Derivatives | [`chapter-10`](chapter-10/) |\n\n### Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio — *23 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 11 | Mutual Funds | [`chapter-11`](chapter-11/) |\n| 12 | Portfolio Manager | [`chapter-12`](chapter-12/) |\n| 13 | Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) | [`chapter-13`](chapter-13/) |\n\n### Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation — *20 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 14 | Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory | [`chapter-14`](chapter-14/) |\n| 15 | Portfolio Construction Process | [`chapter-15`](chapter-15/) |\n| 16 | Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation | [`chapter-16`](chapter-16/) |\n\n### Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 17 | Operational Aspects of Investment Management | [`chapter-17`](chapter-17/) |\n| 18 | Key Regulations | [`chapter-18`](chapter-18/) |\n| 19 | Ethical Issues | [`chapter-19`](chapter-19/) |\n| 20 | Grievance Redress Mechanism | [`chapter-20`](chapter-20/) |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ 10-Day Study Plan\n\n| Day | Cover | Why |\n|-----|-------|-----|\n| 1 | Ch 1 + Financial Maths Primer | Sets the vocabulary for everything else |\n| 2 | Ch 2 (TVM) + Excel guide | **The single highest-value day.** TVM shows up in Ch 4, 9, 14, 16 and every caselet |\n| 3 | Ch 3–4 (Budgeting, Ratios, Loans) | Finishes the 37-mark module; heavily numerical |\n| 4 | Ch 5–6 (Markets, Segments) | Only 10 marks — read once, flashcard it, move on |\n| 5 | Ch 7–8 (Investments, Stocks) | Valuation ratios are exam favourites |\n| 6 | Ch 9–10 (Bonds, Derivatives) | Bond pricing & duration are the hardest maths in the paper |\n| 7 | Ch 11–13 (MF, PMS, AIF) | Product features + the MF/PMS/AIF comparison table |\n| 8 | Ch 14–16 (MPT, Construction, Performance) | Sharpe/Treynor/Beta/Alpha — pure formula marks |\n| 9 | Ch 17–18 (Operations, Regulations) | Big recall load — start flashcards early |\n| 10 | Ch 19–20 (Ethics, Grievances) + mock papers | Easy marks; then simulate the full 3-hour paper |\n\n> Then: **one full mock paper a day** until the exam, reviewing every wrong answer.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The 12 Facts You Must Know Cold\n\nBurn these into memory — they recur across chapters and caselets:\n\n1. **Exam:** 150 marks · 105 questions · 3 hours · pass **90/150 (60%)** · **25% negative marking**.\n2. **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ** — and PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ. Everything else in Module 1 is a variation of this.\n3. **Effective annual rate** with *m* compoundings: **(1 + r/m)^m − 1**. Monthly compounding always beats annual.\n4. **Real return** = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 — *not* simply nominal minus inflation.\n5. **Annuity due > ordinary annuity** by exactly one period's interest: **FV(due) = FV(ordinary) × (1 + r)**.\n6. **Solvency ratio** = Net worth ÷ Total assets. **Liquidity ratio** = Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses. **Savings ratio** = Savings ÷ Income.\n7. **EMI** = P × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1], where r is the *monthly* rate and n the number of months.\n8. **Regulator map:** SEBI → securities; RBI → banking, money & forex; IRDAI → insurance; PFRDA → pensions; MCA/RoC → companies.\n9. **Bond price and yield move in opposite directions.** Longer duration = bigger price swing.\n10. **Sharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σ** (total risk). **Treynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ β** (market risk). **Alpha** = actual return − CAPM-expected return.\n11. **IA net worth:** ₹5 lakh (individual) / ₹50 lakh (non-individual); an IA must do **risk profiling** and ensure **suitability**, and must **segregate advice from distribution**.\n12. **Complaint escalation:** the intermediary first → then **SCORES** (SEBI) / **RBI Ombudsman** / **Insurance Ombudsman** / **PFRDA** → then the **Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)**.\n\n*(Each is explained in full inside the relevant chapter — these are just your quick-recall anchors.)*\n\n---\n\n*Made with care. Good luck — you've got this! 💪*\n", "chapters": [{"num": "01", "title": "Introduction to Personal Financial Planning", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks — the biggest module in the paper).**\n> This chapter is the *map*. It doesn't have much maths, but it names every concept the rest of the syllabus expands on. Learn the vocabulary here and the other 19 chapters get easier.\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is Financial Planning?\n\n**Financial planning is the process of meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances.**\n\nUnpack that sentence — every word is doing work:\n\n- **Life goals** — buying a house, funding a child's education, retiring comfortably, leaving money to heirs. Not \"getting rich\"; *specific, dated, costed* objectives.\n- **Process** — it's ongoing, not a one-time event. You plan, act, review, adjust.\n- **Management of finances** — income, expenses, savings, investments, insurance, loans and taxes are all levers.\n\n> 🧠 **The core idea:** money has no meaning on its own. ₹50 lakh is neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" until you attach it to a goal (a flat in Pune? 25 years of retirement?). Financial planning is the discipline of connecting **money you will have** to **money you will need**.\n\n### Investment vs. Financial Planning\n\n| | Investment advice | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Question asked** | \"Which fund should I buy?\" | \"What are you trying to achieve, and what set of decisions gets you there?\" |\n| **Scope** | One product decision | Cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| **Time horizon** | Often short | Whole lifetime |\n| **Success measured by** | Product return | Whether the goal was met |\n\nA product can beat its benchmark and *still* fail you — if it's illiquid when you need the money. That gap is exactly why financial planning exists.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Why is Financial Planning Needed?\n\nFive forces make planning necessary:\n\n1. **Goals cost money and arrive on dates you don't control.** College fees arrive when the child turns 18, not when markets are up.\n2. **Inflation quietly destroys purchasing power.** At 6% inflation, ₹1 lakh of expenses today becomes about ₹3.2 lakh in 20 years. Money kept \"safe\" in a low-interest account *loses* real value.\n3. **Income is finite and lumpy.** Most people earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years. The earning years must fund the non-earning years.\n4. **Life is uncertain.** Death, disability, illness and job loss can wipe out a plan that had no insurance or emergency fund.\n5. **Products are complex and conflicted.** Investors face hundreds of schemes and a sales industry paid to push them.\n\n### 1.2.1 Role of the Financial Planner\n\nThe planner:\n\n- **Collects** data on income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance and taxes;\n- **Quantifies** goals — converting \"a good retirement\" into \"₹4.2 crore by 2050\";\n- **Assesses risk** — both the *ability* and the *willingness* to take risk;\n- **Recommends** a coherent strategy across products (never a product in isolation);\n- **Implements** or helps implement;\n- **Reviews and rebalances** as life and markets change.\n\nCritically, the planner brings **objectivity and discipline**. Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms; a planner is the circuit-breaker between emotion and the \"confirm\" button.\n\n### 1.2.2 Financial Planning vs. a Typical Financial Advisory Service\n\n| Aspect | Typical advisory / distribution | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Trigger** | A product is available to sell | A client goal needs funding |\n| **Focus** | Product features and returns | The client's whole balance sheet and cash flow |\n| **Coverage** | One need at a time | Integrated across all needs |\n| **Compensation** | Often commission from the manufacturer | Often a fee from the client |\n| **Duty** | May be a *suitability* standard only | **Fiduciary** — client's interest first |\n| **Relationship** | Transaction | Long-term engagement |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam favourite.** A **fiduciary** must put the client's interest *ahead of their own*. This is the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser (see Chapters 18–19).\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Scope of Financial Planning\n\nFinancial planning has **eight** components. Expect questions that give you a client situation and ask which component applies.\n\n### 1.3.1 Personal Financial Analysis\n\nThe diagnosis stage: where does the client stand *today*?\n\n- Build the **personal balance sheet** (assets, liabilities, net worth) and the **cash flow statement** (income, expenses, surplus).\n- Compute the ratios (Chapter 3) — savings, solvency, liquidity, debt-to-income.\n- Then **set goals**.\n\n**Goal-setting rules:**\n\n- **Prioritise goals.** Not everything can be funded at once. Retirement and a child's education generally outrank a car upgrade — because you can borrow for education but *nobody lends for retirement*.\n- **Ensure adequate focus on important goals.** Long-dated, expensive goals (retirement) get ignored because they feel far away. Yet they benefit most from compounding, so starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n- **Stagger the timing of goals.** If three big goals land in the same year, the plan breaks. Move a discretionary goal (a foreign holiday, a car) forward or back so the funding requirement smooths out.\n\nA good goal is **specific, quantified in future rupees (i.e. inflation-adjusted), and dated**. \"Save for my daughter's college\" is not a goal. \"₹42 lakh in March 2038 for a 4-year engineering degree\" is.\n\n### 1.3.2 Cash Flow Management and Budgeting\nTracking inflows and outflows, generating a **surplus**, and building an **emergency/contingency fund** (commonly 3–6 months of expenses). Without a surplus there is nothing to invest — this is the engine of the whole plan. *(Chapter 3.)*\n\n### 1.3.3 Insurance Planning\nProtecting the plan against catastrophic events — death, disability, illness, property loss. Insurance is about **risk transfer**, not returns. If the earning member dies uninsured, every other goal collapses.\n\n### 1.3.4 Debt Management and Counselling\nKeeping borrowing productive and serviceable — the right *purpose*, *cost*, and *maturity* of debt; repayment strategies; and rehabilitation when a borrower is over-leveraged. *(Chapter 4.)*\n\n### 1.3.5 Investment Planning and Asset Allocation\nChoosing the mix of asset classes (equity, debt, gold, real estate) that matches each goal's horizon and the client's risk profile — then selecting products within that mix. **Asset allocation, not stock selection, drives most of a portfolio's outcome.** *(Chapters 14–15.)*\n\n### 1.3.6 Tax Planning\nArranging affairs to legally minimise tax — using available exemptions, deductions and the tax treatment of different products. Note: tax efficiency is a *modifier*, never the primary reason to buy a product.\n\n### 1.3.7 Retirement Planning\nEstimating the corpus needed to fund post-retirement expenses (inflated to the retirement date) and building it through the earning years. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n### 1.3.8 Estate Planning\nEnsuring assets pass to intended beneficiaries smoothly — wills, nominations, trusts, joint holdings. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Assets, Liabilities and Net Worth\n\nThe **personal balance sheet** is a snapshot at a point in time.\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### Classifying assets\n\n| Type | What it is | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liquid assets** | Convertible to cash quickly with little loss of value | Cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs |\n| **Financial assets** | Claims on someone else | Deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, insurance cash values, PF/PPF |\n| **Physical / real assets** | Tangible property | House, land, gold, jewellery, art |\n| **Personal-use assets** | Held for use, not returns | Car, appliances, the home you live in |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap:** *personal-use assets* (car, furniture) generally **depreciate** and produce no income. Counting them heavily inflates net worth but not the ability to fund goals — which is exactly why the **financial assets ratio** exists (Chapter 3).\n\n### Classifying liabilities\n\n- **Short-term:** credit card outstanding, overdrafts, personal loans, dues within a year.\n- **Long-term:** home loan, education loan, vehicle loan.\n\nRecord liabilities at the **outstanding principal** on the statement date — not the original loan amount and not the total of remaining EMIs (that would include future interest).\n\n### Reading net worth\n\n- **Positive and growing** = the plan is working.\n- **Negative** = liabilities exceed assets — technically insolvent; debt management becomes the first priority.\n- Net worth grows in exactly two ways: (a) **saving** out of income, and (b) **appreciation** of assets held.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 The Financial Planning Process\n\nThe six steps — a favourite for sequence questions. Learn the **order**.\n\n| Step | Name | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Establish the **client–planner relationship** | Define scope, services, duration, compensation, and both parties' responsibilities — **in writing** |\n| **2** | Gather data on the **client's financial situation** | Quantitative (income, assets, debts, insurance, taxes) *and* qualitative (attitudes, experience, family) |\n| **3** | Define **client needs and financial goals** | Convert wishes into specific, dated, costed goals; prioritise them |\n| **4** | **Risk profiling** and asset allocation | Assess capacity + willingness to bear risk; derive the allocation |\n| **5** | **Portfolio construction** | Recommend and implement a specific plan/product set |\n| **6** | **Reviewing and rebalancing** | Monitor, report, and correct drift as life and markets change |\n\n### 1.5.1 Client–Planner Relationship\nSets the terms of engagement. The adviser must disclose **what services are provided, how they are paid, and any conflicts of interest**. Getting this in writing protects both sides — and for a registered IA it is a regulatory requirement.\n\n### 1.5.2 Client's Financial Situation\nIncomplete data produces a wrong plan. Collect income, expenses, existing investments, loans, insurance covers, tax status, employer benefits — plus family structure and dependants.\n\n### 1.5.3 Client Needs and Financial Goals\nTranslate \"I want to be comfortable\" into numbers. Apply the goal rules from 1.3.1: prioritise, focus, stagger.\n\n### 1.5.4 Risk Profiling\nTwo distinct things, often confused:\n\n- **Risk capacity (ability)** — *objective*. Driven by horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants and how essential the goal is. A 28-year-old with a stable job and no dependants has high capacity.\n- **Risk tolerance/attitude (willingness)** — *subjective*. Psychological comfort with volatility.\n\n> 🧠 **Rule:** where they conflict, plan to the **lower** of the two. High capacity but low tolerance means a client who panic-sells at the bottom, converting a paper loss into a permanent one. Also note a **risk requirement**: the return the goal actually needs.\n\n### 1.5.5 Portfolio Construction\nAsset allocation first, product selection second. Products must fit the goal's **horizon** (don't fund a 1-year goal with equity) and the client's **liquidity** needs.\n\n### 1.5.6 Reviewing and Rebalancing\nTwo separate triggers:\n\n- **Market drift** — a 60:40 equity:debt mix becomes 70:30 after a rally. Rebalancing sells the winner and buys the laggard, restoring the risk level (and enforcing \"sell high, buy low\").\n- **Life events** — marriage, a child, job change, inheritance, illness. These change the *goals*, not just the weights.\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Financial Advisory and Execution\n\nTwo distinct activities:\n\n- **Advice** — recommending what the client should do. Value comes from the recommendation itself.\n- **Execution / distribution** — actually placing the transaction. Value comes from operational convenience.\n\nThe **conflict**: if the adviser earns commission on what they sell, the recommendation may drift toward whatever pays best. SEBI's answer is the **segregation of advice and distribution** — a registered Investment Adviser must keep advisory separate from any distribution activity, and an individual IA cannot do both. *(Chapter 18 covers the exact provisions.)*\n\n### The IA's value proposition\n\n| Adviser adds value by | Because |\n|---|---|\n| Setting the asset allocation | It drives most of the outcome |\n| Preventing behavioural mistakes | Panic-selling and return-chasing destroy more wealth than bad products |\n| Choosing **direct plans** | Lower expense ratio compounds over decades |\n| Tax-aware structuring | Post-tax return is what the client actually spends |\n| Regular review | Keeps the plan aligned with a changing life |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Financial planning | Meeting life goals through proper management of finances |\n| Net worth | Total assets − total liabilities |\n| Steps in the process | Relationship → Data → Goals → Risk profiling → Construction → Review |\n| Scope (8 areas) | Analysis, cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| Risk capacity | **Ability** to take risk — objective (horizon, income, net worth) |\n| Risk tolerance | **Willingness** to take risk — subjective (psychology) |\n| Which one binds? | Plan to the **lower** of capacity and tolerance |\n| Fiduciary | Must place the client's interest **ahead of** their own |\n| Goal quality | Specific + quantified in future rupees + dated + prioritised |\n| Why stagger goals | So funding requirements don't collide in one year |\n| Two ways net worth grows | Saving out of income; appreciation of assets |\n| Rebalancing triggers | Market drift and life events |\n| Advice vs execution | Recommending vs transacting — SEBI requires them segregated |\n\n> **Exam tip:** this chapter is mostly definitional, so the questions are *precise*. Watch the difference between **capacity and tolerance**, between **assets and net worth**, and the **order** of the six process steps.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Define financial planning in one sentence.\n> **A:** The process of **meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances** — connecting money you will have to money you will need.\n\n**Q2.** What are the three things that make a goal a *proper* financial goal?\n> **A:** It is **specific**, **quantified in future (inflation-adjusted) rupees**, and **dated**. \"Save for college\" is not a goal; \"₹42 lakh in March 2038\" is.\n\n**Q3.** What is the formula for net worth?\n> **A:** **Net Worth = Total Assets − Total Liabilities.**\n\n**Q4.** Name the six steps of the financial planning process, in order.\n> **A:** 1) Establish the **client–planner relationship** → 2) Gather the **client's financial situation** → 3) Define **needs and goals** → 4) **Risk profiling** → 5) **Portfolio construction** → 6) **Review and rebalance**.\n\n**Q5.** List the eight components in the scope of financial planning.\n> **A:** Personal financial **analysis**, **cash flow**/budgeting, **insurance**, **debt** management, **investment** planning & asset allocation, **tax**, **retirement**, **estate** planning.\n\n**Q6.** What is the difference between risk *capacity* and risk *tolerance*?\n> **A:** **Capacity = ability**, and it is **objective** (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants). **Tolerance = willingness**, and it is **subjective** (psychological comfort with volatility).\n\n**Q7.** If a client has high risk capacity but low risk tolerance, what do you plan to?\n> **A:** The **lower** of the two. A client who panics and sells at the bottom converts a paper loss into a permanent one, regardless of how much risk they could theoretically afford.\n\n**Q8.** What does \"fiduciary\" mean?\n> **A:** The adviser must place the **client's interest ahead of their own** — the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser.\n\n**Q9.** Why can't you simply borrow your way through retirement?\n> **A:** You can borrow for a house or education, but **nobody lends for retirement**. That is why retirement typically outranks discretionary goals in prioritisation.\n\n**Q10.** What does \"staggering goals\" mean and why do it?\n> **A:** Deliberately spacing goals out in time so several large funding requirements **don't land in the same year** and break the plan. Discretionary goals get moved, not essential ones.\n\n**Q11.** Name the four classes of assets on a personal balance sheet.\n> **A:** **Liquid** assets, **financial** assets, **physical/real** assets, and **personal-use** assets.\n\n**Q12.** Why are personal-use assets treated carefully in a balance sheet?\n> **A:** They generally **depreciate and produce no income**, so counting them inflates net worth without improving the ability to fund goals.\n\n**Q13.** At what value do you record a home loan on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** on the statement date — *not* the original loan amount, and *not* the sum of remaining EMIs (which would include future interest).\n\n**Q14.** What are the only two ways net worth grows?\n> **A:** **Saving** out of income, and **appreciation** of assets already held.\n\n**Q15.** What does a negative net worth mean?\n> **A:** Liabilities exceed assets — the person is **technically insolvent**, and debt management becomes the first priority.\n\n**Q16.** Give three differences between financial planning and typical product-led advisory.\n> **A:** Planning is triggered by a **client goal** (not a saleable product); it is **integrated** across all needs (not one need at a time); and it usually carries a **fiduciary** duty with fee-based compensation.\n\n**Q17.** What are the two triggers for reviewing and rebalancing a portfolio?\n> **A:** **Market drift** (a rally turns 60:40 into 70:30) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\n\n**Q18.** How does rebalancing enforce good behaviour?\n> **A:** It mechanically **sells the asset that has risen and buys the one that lagged** — \"sell high, buy low\" — while restoring the intended risk level.\n\n**Q19.** What is the difference between advice and execution?\n> **A:** **Advice** is recommending what the client should do; **execution/distribution** is placing the transaction. SEBI requires an IA to **segregate** the two.\n\n**Q20.** Why does inflation make financial planning necessary?\n> **A:** It destroys purchasing power — at 6% inflation ₹1 lakh of expenses becomes roughly **₹3.2 lakh in 20 years**, so \"safe\" low-interest money loses real value.\n\n**Q21.** Why is the mismatch between earning years and living years a planning problem?\n> **A:** People typically earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years, so the earning years must **fund the non-earning years**.\n\n**Q22.** What is a contingency (emergency) fund and how large is it usually?\n> **A:** A pool of liquid money for income shocks and emergencies, commonly **3–6 months of expenses**.\n\n**Q23.** Which drives portfolio outcomes more — asset allocation or security selection?\n> **A:** **Asset allocation.** It explains most of the variation in portfolio returns, which is why it comes before product selection.\n\n**Q24.** Why should long-dated goals get deliberate focus?\n> **A:** They *feel* far away and get neglected, yet they benefit most from **compounding** — starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n\n**Q25.** What must be disclosed when establishing the client–planner relationship?\n> **A:** The **scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities of both parties, and any conflicts of interest** — in writing.\n\n**Q26.** What two kinds of data are gathered in step 2?\n> **A:** **Quantitative** (income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance, tax status) and **qualitative** (attitudes, risk experience, family structure, dependants).\n\n**Q27.** What is \"risk requirement\"?\n> **A:** The **return the goal actually needs** — distinct from risk capacity (ability) and risk tolerance (willingness).\n\n**Q28.** Why is tax planning never the *primary* reason to buy a product?\n> **A:** Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of return, not a substitute for suitability. A tax-efficient product that doesn't fit the goal's horizon or liquidity need is still the wrong product.\n\n**Q29.** What is the core conflict of interest in commission-based distribution?\n> **A:** The recommendation can drift toward **whatever pays the adviser most**, rather than what suits the client best.\n\n**Q30.** Name five ways an Investment Adviser adds measurable value.\n> **A:** Setting **asset allocation**; preventing **behavioural mistakes**; using **direct plans** (lower expense ratio); **tax-aware** structuring; and **regular review**.\n\n**Q31.** Can a financial plan succeed if the product beats its benchmark?\n> **A:** Not necessarily. A product can outperform and still fail the client — for example if it is **illiquid exactly when the money is needed**. Goal achievement, not product return, is the measure of success.\n\n**Q32.** Insurance planning is about what — returns or protection?\n> **A:** **Risk transfer / protection.** Insurance shields the plan from catastrophic events; it is not bought for returns.\n\n**Q33.** What is the role of the planner as a \"circuit breaker\"?\n> **A:** Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms. The planner sits **between emotion and the confirm button**, supplying objectivity and discipline.\n\n**Q34.** Why is ₹50 lakh neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" on its own?\n> **A:** Because money has meaning only **relative to a goal**. Enough for a flat in a small town, nowhere near enough for 25 years of retirement.\n\n**Q35.** What four things should debt management get right?\n> **A:** The **purpose**, the **cost**, the **maturity** of the debt — and rehabilitation (counselling/rescheduling) when a borrower is over-leveraged.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam has **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of the marks — guess only when you can eliminate at least two options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Financial planning is best defined as the process of:\nA) Maximising investment returns  B) Meeting life goals through the proper management of finances  C) Selecting the best-performing mutual fund  D) Minimising the tax payable each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard definition ties money to **life goals**: financial planning is meeting one's life goals through the proper management of one's finances.\nA) Maximising returns is not the objective; a high-return product that is illiquid when the goal arrives still fails the client. C) Product selection is one small step inside planning, not the definition. D) Tax minimisation is one of the eight components of scope, not the whole discipline.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Net worth is calculated as:\nA) Total income − total expenses  B) Total assets − total liabilities  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Total assets + total liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth is a **balance sheet** concept: everything you own minus everything you owe.\nA) Income minus expenses is the **surplus/savings**, a cash-flow concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Adding liabilities to assets is meaningless — liabilities are a claim *against* assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is the FIRST step in the financial planning process?\nA) Risk profiling of the client  B) Constructing the portfolio  C) Establishing the client–planner relationship  D) Gathering the client's financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The process begins by **defining the engagement**: scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts, preferably in writing.\nD) Data gathering is step 2 — you cannot ethically collect sensitive financial data before the terms of engagement are agreed. A) Risk profiling is step 4. B) Portfolio construction is step 5.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Risk capacity refers to:\nA) The client's psychological comfort with volatility  B) The client's objective ability to bear risk  C) The return the goal requires  D) The volatility of the recommended portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** is objective and is driven by time horizon, income stability, net worth and number of dependants.\nA) That is risk **tolerance** (willingness) — subjective. C) That is the risk **requirement**. D) That is portfolio risk, an output, not a client attribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of these is NOT one of the components in the scope of financial planning?\nA) Insurance planning  B) Estate planning  C) Corporate merger advisory  D) Retirement planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Corporate merger advisory is **investment banking**, not personal financial planning.\nA), B) and D) are three of the eight components — the others being personal financial analysis, cash flow/budgeting, debt management, investment planning and tax planning.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A fiduciary is required to:\nA) Guarantee the client a minimum return  B) Place the client's interest ahead of their own  C) Disclose returns only on request  D) Recommend only products from a single manufacturer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fiduciary standard requires the adviser to put the **client's interest first**, above the adviser's own commercial interest.\nA) No adviser may guarantee returns. C) Disclosure must be proactive, not on-request. D) Restricting to a single manufacturer is a conflict of interest, the very thing a fiduciary duty guards against.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is a liquid asset?\nA) A residential plot of land  B) Balance in a savings bank account  C) The family car  D) Unlisted equity shares of a private company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A liquid asset can be converted into cash **quickly and without material loss of value** — cash, savings balances, liquid funds, sweep-in deposits.\nA) Land takes months to sell. C) A car is a personal-use asset that depreciates. D) Unlisted shares have no ready market.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The personal balance sheet shows a client's position:\nA) Over a financial year  B) At a specific point in time  C) Only after retirement  D) Averaged over three years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot** on a stated date. The cash-flow (income and expenditure) statement is the one that covers a *period*.\nA) and D) describe flow statements or averages. C) It is prepared at any life stage.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A fixed deposit  B) An equity mutual fund  C) The car used by the family  D) A rented-out second flat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Personal-use assets are held for use rather than for return, and typically **depreciate** — cars, appliances, furniture.\nA) and B) are financial assets. D) A rented-out flat generates income, so it is an investment (physical/real) asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** How should an outstanding home loan be recorded on the personal balance sheet?\nA) The original sanctioned loan amount  B) The total of all remaining EMIs  C) The outstanding principal on the statement date  D) The market value of the house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is a liability today.\nA) The original amount ignores repayments already made. B) The sum of remaining EMIs includes **future interest**, which is not yet owed. D) The house value is an *asset*, recorded on the other side of the balance sheet.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Risk tolerance is best described as:\nA) Objective and measurable from income and horizon  B) Subjective — the client's psychological willingness to bear volatility  C) The same as risk capacity  D) Determined solely by the client's age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tolerance is **attitudinal**: how comfortable the client actually feels when the portfolio falls.\nA) Describes risk capacity. C) The two are distinct and often conflict. D) Age influences capacity, but tolerance is psychological and varies between people of the same age.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A contingency or emergency fund is typically sized at:\nA) 1 month of expenses  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 3–6 months of income  D) 2 years of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The common benchmark is **3–6 months of expenses**, held in liquid form.\nA) One month is too thin to absorb a job loss. C) The standard is measured against *expenses*, not income, because expenses are what must be met. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return liquid assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which statement about advice and execution is correct?\nA) They are the same activity  B) Advice is recommending; execution is transacting  C) Execution must always precede advice  D) Only execution requires SEBI registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Advice** = recommending what the client should do. **Execution/distribution** = actually placing the transaction. SEBI requires an Investment Adviser to segregate the two.\nA) They are distinct and have different conflicts. C) Advice logically precedes execution. D) Investment *advice* is precisely what triggers IA registration.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following increases a client's net worth?\nA) Transferring money from a savings account to a fixed deposit  B) Saving a part of income  C) Taking a personal loan  D) Repaying the interest portion of an EMI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth grows in only two ways: **saving out of income** and **appreciation of assets held**.\nA) Moving money between assets changes composition, not total net worth. C) A loan raises assets *and* liabilities equally — net worth is unchanged. D) Interest is an expense; only the *principal* portion of an EMI reduces liabilities.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** \"Staggering goals\" means:\nA) Postponing all goals indefinitely  B) Spacing goals in time so funding requirements don't collide  C) Funding only the cheapest goals  D) Investing in staggered maturities of bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If several expensive goals land in the same year the plan breaks, so **discretionary** goals are shifted earlier or later.\nA) Postponing everything is avoidance, not planning. C) Cost is not the prioritisation criterion — importance is. D) That is a bond ladder, a portfolio technique.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which pair correctly matches the concept to its statement?\nA) Surplus — balance sheet  B) Net worth — cash flow statement  C) Net worth — balance sheet  D) Assets — income statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Net worth** belongs to the **balance sheet** (a stock concept).\nA) Surplus (income − expenses) belongs to the cash flow statement. B) and D) reverse the two statements. Remember: balance sheet = *stocks*, cash flow = *flows*.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Inflation matters to financial planning primarily because it:\nA) Increases interest rates on deposits  B) Erodes the purchasing power of money over time  C) Reduces the tax payable on gains  D) Guarantees higher equity returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation means the same rupees buy less later, so goals must be costed in **future** rupees.\nA) Rates may rise with inflation but that is a side effect, not the planning reason. C) Inflation does not reduce tax. D) No return is guaranteed by inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which of the following is a qualitative input in data gathering?\nA) The client's annual salary  B) The outstanding home loan balance  C) The client's past experience of market losses  D) The premium on a term policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Qualitative** data covers attitudes, prior experience, family structure and preferences.\nA), B) and D) are all **quantitative** — hard numbers from documents.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Asset allocation is primarily the decision about:\nA) Which individual stocks to buy  B) The mix of asset classes in the portfolio  C) Which broker to use  D) When to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Asset allocation is the **mix across asset classes** (equity, debt, gold, real estate).\nA) That is security selection, a later and less impactful decision. C) and D) are operational/compliance matters, not allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which is a long-term liability?\nA) A credit card outstanding due this month  B) A 20-year home loan  C) An overdraft repayable on demand  D) An electricity bill payable next week\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Long-term liabilities run beyond a year — home, education and vehicle loans.\nA), C) and D) are all short-term liabilities due within a year.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The main purpose of insurance planning within a financial plan is:\nA) To generate the highest possible return  B) To transfer the financial impact of catastrophic risks  C) To reduce the client's tax liability  D) To increase the client's net worth quickly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance is about **risk transfer** — protecting the plan from death, disability, illness or property loss.\nA) Insurance is not a return product. C) Tax relief is incidental. D) Insurance protects net worth; it does not build it.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of the following describes the review-and-rebalance step?\nA) It happens only once, at the start  B) It is triggered by market drift and life events  C) It is required only when markets fall  D) It applies only to clients above age 60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two triggers: **market drift** (allocation moves away from target) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\nA) Review is ongoing, not one-off. C) A rally distorts allocation just as a fall does. D) It applies to all clients.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following is TRUE of a goal-based approach?\nA) Goals should be expressed in today's rupees only  B) All goals should be funded simultaneously and equally  C) Goals should be specific, quantified in future rupees and dated  D) Goals need not be prioritised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A usable goal is specific, costed in **inflation-adjusted future rupees**, and has a date.\nA) Today's rupees understate the requirement. B) Resources are finite, so goals must be prioritised. D) Prioritisation is explicitly part of goal-setting.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The financial planning process step that immediately follows \"defining client needs and goals\" is:\nA) Establishing the relationship  B) Risk profiling and asset allocation  C) Reviewing and rebalancing  D) Gathering financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Order: relationship → data → goals → **risk profiling** → construction → review.\nA) and D) come earlier. C) Review is the last step.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which of these is an example of a physical/real asset?\nA) A corporate bond  B) A unit-linked insurance plan  C) Gold jewellery  D) A recurring deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Physical/real assets are **tangible** — property, land, gold, art.\nA), B) and D) are all financial assets, i.e. claims on another party.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A client with a negative net worth is:\nA) In an ideal position for aggressive equity investing  B) Technically insolvent, with debt management as the first priority  C) Required to buy more insurance immediately  D) Unaffected, since net worth is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liabilities exceed assets, which is **technical insolvency**; reducing debt comes before building an investment portfolio.\nA) Aggressive equity while insolvent compounds the problem. C) Insurance matters but is not the first fix. D) Net worth is the central measure of financial health.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of the following best distinguishes financial planning from product distribution?\nA) Planning always uses direct plans  B) Planning is triggered by a client's goal, distribution by a saleable product  C) Distribution is illegal in India  D) Planning is only for high-net-worth clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Distribution starts from **what can be sold**; planning starts from **what the client needs**.\nA) Direct plans are typical of advisory but not the defining difference. C) Distribution is legal and regulated. D) Planning applies at every income level.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which of these is a short-term liability?\nA) A 15-year home loan  B) A 7-year education loan  C) A credit card outstanding  D) A 5-year car loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Credit card dues are payable within the month — a classic short-term liability.\nA), B) and D) all extend well beyond one year.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following is the correct relationship?\nA) Risk requirement = the client's psychological comfort  B) Risk requirement = the return the goal needs  C) Risk requirement = total assets ÷ liabilities  D) Risk requirement = the same as risk capacity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **risk requirement** is the return the goal actually demands, given the amount and the time available.\nA) That is tolerance. C) That is a leverage-type ratio. D) Capacity is the *ability* to bear risk, a different concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The client–planner relationship step should ideally document:\nA) Only the adviser's fee  B) Scope, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts  C) The expected portfolio return  D) The client's PAN alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All the engagement terms are documented, in writing, so both sides know the boundaries.\nA) Fee alone is incomplete. C) Returns cannot be promised. D) PAN is collected in onboarding, not the engagement terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which of these best explains why retirement usually outranks a car upgrade in priority?\nA) Cars are always cheaper  B) You can borrow for most goals, but nobody lends for retirement  C) Retirement goals are always nearer in time  D) Regulations require it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loans exist for houses, cars and education; there is **no loan for retirement**, so it must be self-funded.\nA) Cost is not the criterion. C) Retirement is usually the *farthest* goal, which is exactly why it gets neglected. D) No regulation prescribes goal priority.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In the balance sheet, the value of an equity mutual fund holding should be taken at:\nA) The amount originally invested  B) Its current market/NAV-based value  C) The amount net of exit load only  D) Zero, until redeemed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets are recorded at **current market value** so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Loads are transaction costs, not the valuation basis. D) Unrealised holdings are still assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of the following is TRUE about tax planning within financial planning?\nA) It is the primary reason to select any product  B) It modifies returns but does not override suitability  C) It applies only to salaried clients  D) It replaces the need for asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of post-tax return; a tax-friendly product that doesn't fit the horizon or liquidity need is still wrong.\nA) Suitability comes first. C) It applies to all taxpayers. D) Allocation is a separate and more fundamental decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The main behavioural value an adviser adds is:\nA) Predicting market tops and bottoms  B) Preventing panic-selling and return-chasing  C) Guaranteeing outperformance  D) Eliminating all portfolio risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors destroy returns by selling in crashes and buying after rallies; the adviser is the **circuit breaker**.\nA) Market timing is not reliably possible. C) No one can guarantee outperformance. D) Risk can be managed, never eliminated.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which one of these is an output of the financial planning process rather than an input?\nA) The client's monthly income  B) The client's existing insurance covers  C) The recommended asset allocation  D) The client's outstanding loans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **recommended asset allocation** is produced by the process.\nA), B) and D) are all data *collected* in step 2 as inputs.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Rahul, aged 27, has a stable government job, no dependants and a 30-year horizon, but he sold all his equity funds in the last 12% market correction. What should the adviser plan to?\nA) His high risk capacity, since he is young  B) His low demonstrated risk tolerance  C) The average of the two  D) The highest equity allocation available\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where capacity and tolerance conflict, plan to the **lower**. Rahul's *capacity* is high (age, stability, no dependants) but his behaviour reveals **low tolerance**. Allocating aggressively guarantees another panic-sale, converting a temporary paper loss into a permanent one.\nA) Ignores demonstrated behaviour. C) Averaging is not the rule; the binding constraint is the lower of the two. D) The worst possible fit for a proven panic-seller. The adviser can raise equity gradually as Rahul's experience grows.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client owns: savings balance ₹2,00,000; equity funds ₹8,00,000; a house worth ₹60,00,000; a car worth ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan outstanding ₹35,00,000; car loan ₹2,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹39,00,000  B) ₹76,00,000  C) ₹33,00,000  D) ₹41,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = Total assets − Total liabilities. Assets = 2,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 60,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹76,00,000**. Liabilities = 35,00,000 + 2,00,000 = **₹37,00,000**. Net worth = 76,00,000 − 37,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\nB) ₹76,00,000 is total assets only, ignoring all debt. C) ₹33,00,000 wrongly excludes the car from assets. D) ₹41,00,000 omits the car loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client takes a ₹5,00,000 personal loan and deposits it in a fixed deposit. The immediate effect on net worth is:\nA) An increase of ₹5,00,000  B) A decrease of ₹5,00,000  C) No change  D) An increase equal to the FD interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Borrowing raises **assets by ₹5,00,000** (the FD) and **liabilities by ₹5,00,000** (the loan) simultaneously. Assets − liabilities is therefore unchanged.\nA) Ignores the new liability. B) Ignores the new asset. D) Interest accrues *later*, and in practice the loan rate will exceed the FD rate, so net worth will actually *fall* over time.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client says: \"I want my son to study abroad.\" Which is the properly framed goal?\nA) \"Save aggressively for my son\"  B) \"Accumulate ₹75 lakh by June 2035 for a 2-year master's programme\"  C) \"Invest ₹20,000 a month in equity funds\"  D) \"Get the highest return possible before 2035\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is **specific** (2-year master's), **quantified in future rupees** (₹75 lakh), and **dated** (June 2035).\nA) is a vague intention. C) describes a *means*, not a goal — you cannot tell whether ₹20,000 a month is enough without the target. D) has a date but no amount, so success cannot be measured.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An adviser earns a trail commission from a fund house and also charges the client a fee for the same advice. Under SEBI's framework this is problematic because:\nA) Trail commissions are illegal in all cases  B) Advice and distribution must be segregated  C) Fees may not exceed ₹1,000  D) Only banks may earn commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI requires a registered Investment Adviser to **segregate advisory from distribution**; an individual IA cannot provide advice and distribution to the same client, precisely to remove this conflict.\nA) Commissions are legal for distributors, who are not acting as IAs. C) There is a fee cap framework but that is not the issue here. D) Many entities may distribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client most urgently needs a contingency fund before starting equity SIPs?\nA) A tenured professor with a 25-year horizon and 6 months of expenses in a liquid fund  B) A freelance designer with irregular income and no liquid savings  C) A salaried employee with 8 months of expenses in a sweep-in FD  D) A retiree with a large annuity and 12 months of liquid assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Irregular income plus **zero liquid savings** is the dangerous combination: any income gap forces redemption of long-term investments, possibly at a loss.\nA), C) and D) all already hold 6 months or more of liquid cover, so they can begin or continue long-term investing.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A 45-year-old client wants to fund a goal that is 9 months away. The most appropriate consideration is:\nA) Maximise equity exposure for the highest return  B) Match the product to the short horizon — capital protection and liquidity  C) Use the client's risk tolerance alone  D) Choose whichever product is most tax-efficient\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **9-month** goal, horizon dominates: the money must be there, in full, on the date. Equity's short-run volatility can easily leave a shortfall.\nA) Nine months is far too short for equity. C) Even a high-tolerance client cannot change the fact that the goal is near. D) Tax efficiency never overrides the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of the following pairs a life event to the correct planning response?\nA) Birth of a child → reduce life insurance  B) Job loss → increase equity SIP amount  C) Inheritance received → review goals and rebalance allocation  D) Marriage → cancel the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An inheritance changes the client's **capacity and resources**, so goals and allocation should be revisited.\nA) A new dependant *increases* the insurance need. B) During job loss the emergency fund is drawn on and SIPs may be paused, not increased. D) Marriage adds responsibilities; the emergency fund becomes more important.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A portfolio set at 60% equity : 40% debt has drifted to 72% : 28% after a strong rally. Rebalancing to target requires:\nA) Buying more equity  B) Selling equity and buying debt  C) Selling debt and buying equity  D) Doing nothing, since equity is performing well\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity has grown beyond target, so you **sell equity and buy debt** to restore 60:40. This both resets the risk level and mechanically enforces \"sell high, buy low\".\nA) and C) push the drift further. D) Leaving it means the client is now carrying materially more risk than the plan intended — precisely when valuations are highest.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which is the strongest argument for recommending direct plans?\nA) Direct plans always outperform in a single year  B) The lower expense ratio compounds over long horizons  C) Direct plans are guaranteed by SEBI  D) Direct plans have no market risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct plans carry **no distribution commission**, so the expense ratio is lower; over decades that cost saving compounds into a meaningfully larger corpus.\nA) Single-year outperformance is not guaranteed and is not the argument. C) SEBI regulates but guarantees nothing. D) Market risk is identical — only the cost differs.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client's balance sheet is dominated by the self-occupied house (85% of assets). The key planning concern is:\nA) Net worth is too high  B) The asset base is illiquid and cannot fund goals  C) The house should be sold immediately  D) There is no concern; property is the safest asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A self-occupied house produces **no income and cannot be partly liquidated**. High net worth on paper coexists with an inability to fund goals — which is why the *financial assets ratio* is tracked.\nA) High net worth is not a problem in itself. C) Selling the home the family lives in is rarely the answer; building financial assets alongside is. D) Property carries market, liquidity and concentration risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which sequence correctly describes goal prioritisation for a 32-year-old with limited surplus?\nA) Foreign holiday → retirement → emergency fund  B) Emergency fund and insurance → retirement and child's education → discretionary goals  C) Retirement → foreign holiday → emergency fund  D) Discretionary goals first, since they are cheapest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Protect first** (emergency fund, insurance), then fund **essential long-term goals** (retirement, education), then **discretionary** wants.\nA) and C) place discretionary spending or long-term goals ahead of basic protection, leaving the plan exposed to a single shock. D) Cost is not the criterion — importance and the consequence of failure are.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An adviser gathers only the client's income and existing mutual fund holdings, then recommends a portfolio. The main deficiency is:\nA) Too much data was collected  B) Liabilities, insurance and goals were not captured, so the plan may be unsuitable  C) Mutual funds should never be recommended  D) Income is irrelevant to planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without **liabilities, insurance cover, tax status and goals**, the adviser cannot know the client's capacity, protection gaps or horizons. Incomplete data produces an unsuitable plan.\nA) The problem is too *little* data. C) Mutual funds are perfectly legitimate. D) Income is essential — it is just not sufficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which of these is the best example of the planner acting as a \"circuit breaker\"?\nA) Switching the client's portfolio to cash during every correction  B) Persuading a client not to redeem their entire equity SIP after a 15% fall  C) Recommending the fund with the highest one-year return  D) Increasing equity allocation after a rally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stopping an emotional, plan-destroying decision at the worst moment is precisely the behavioural value the adviser adds.\nA) Systematically selling into every fall *is* the mistake. C) Chasing recent winners is return-chasing. D) Raising equity after a rally increases risk exactly when valuations are richest.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client aged 55 with 5 years to retirement and a modest corpus has a high risk *requirement* but low risk *capacity*. The appropriate response is:\nA) Take maximum equity risk to close the gap  B) Reset the goal — retire later, save more, or reduce the target corpus  C) Ignore the shortfall  D) Borrow to invest in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the return a goal *needs* exceeds what the client can *safely* pursue, the honest answer is to change the **goal**, not to gamble: extend the working years, raise the savings rate, or lower the target.\nA) Chasing a high required return with 5 years left risks a catastrophic shortfall precisely when there is no time to recover. C) Ignoring it guarantees failure. D) Leveraging into equity near retirement is reckless.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following correctly describes the difference between the balance sheet and cash flow statement?\nA) Both cover a period of time  B) The balance sheet is a snapshot; the cash flow statement covers a period  C) The cash flow statement shows net worth  D) The balance sheet shows monthly surplus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance sheet = **stock** at a point in time; cash flow statement = **flow** over a period.\nA) Only the cash flow statement covers a period. C) Net worth comes from the balance sheet. D) Surplus comes from the cash flow statement.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client insists on investing an emergency fund in a small-cap equity fund \"for better returns\". The adviser should explain that:\nA) Small-cap funds are the ideal emergency vehicle  B) An emergency fund's job is availability of full value on demand, not return  C) Emergency funds are unnecessary  D) The fund will guarantee capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An emergency fund's defining requirement is **liquidity and certainty of value** at the moment of need — which is often exactly when markets are falling and jobs are being lost.\nA) Small-caps are the most volatile and least liquid segment. C) Emergency funds are foundational. D) No equity fund guarantees capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client's goals are: retirement in 25 years, a car in 2 years, and a home down-payment in 4 years. Which allocation logic is correct?\nA) All three funded from the same aggressive equity portfolio  B) Longer-horizon goals can carry more equity; nearer goals need debt/liquid products  C) All three in fixed deposits  D) Nearer goals should carry the most equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon drives allocation.** The 25-year retirement goal can absorb equity volatility; the 2-year and 4-year goals need capital stability.\nA) A single aggressive pot exposes near-term goals to a badly timed drawdown. C) All-FD would likely fail the 25-year goal against inflation. D) Reverses the correct logic entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is a conflict of interest an Investment Adviser must disclose?\nA) The adviser has passed the NISM examination  B) The adviser's family member owns the fund house being recommended  C) The adviser uses a spreadsheet for calculations  D) The adviser has more than 50 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **financial or family interest in a recommended product provider** is a material conflict and must be disclosed.\nA) Certification is a qualification, not a conflict. C) and D) are operational facts with no bearing on the adviser's incentives.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary reason that a product beating its benchmark can still fail a client is:\nA) Benchmarks are always wrong  B) The product may not match the goal's horizon or liquidity need  C) Outperformance is taxed at a higher rate  D) SEBI prohibits benchmark comparison\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Success is measured by whether the **goal** was met. A great fund that is locked in, or volatile just when the money is needed, still fails the client.\nA) Benchmarks are imperfect but that is not the point. C) Tax does not depend on beating a benchmark. D) Benchmark comparison is required, not prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which statement about the eight components of financial planning scope is correct?\nA) They are applied strictly one at a time, in isolation  B) They are interlinked — a decision in one affects the others  C) Only investment planning matters for young clients  D) Estate planning applies only to the very wealthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The components are **integrated**: taking a large home loan changes cash flow, insurance need, tax position and investible surplus at once.\nA) Treating them in isolation is exactly the weakness of product-led advice. C) Young clients still need protection and cash-flow discipline. D) Anyone with assets and heirs benefits from at least a will and correct nominations.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client earns ₹1,20,000 a month and spends ₹1,15,000. The most immediate planning issue is:\nA) The portfolio is wrongly allocated  B) There is almost no surplus to fund any goal  C) The client needs more insurance riders  D) The client should buy a house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A surplus of only ₹5,000 (about **4% of income**) means the *engine* of the plan is barely running. Cash-flow management must come before any allocation discussion.\nA) Allocation is irrelevant when there is nothing meaningful to allocate. C) Insurance matters but is not the binding constraint. D) Adding an EMI to an already stretched budget makes it worse.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which best describes why long-dated goals are frequently under-funded?\nA) They are cheaper than short-term goals  B) They feel distant, so they lose out to immediate wants — despite benefiting most from compounding  C) Regulations discourage long-term saving  D) Compounding does not apply beyond 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Psychological discounting of the distant future means retirement loses to today's spending, yet the long horizon is exactly what makes compounding powerful.\nA) They are usually the *most* expensive. C) Tax rules generally *encourage* long-term saving. D) Compounding grows stronger with time, not weaker.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An adviser recommends the same model portfolio to every client regardless of circumstances. This violates:\nA) Nothing — model portfolios are standard practice  B) The suitability principle, since recommendations must fit the individual client  C) Only the record-keeping rules  D) The requirement to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Suitability** requires the recommendation to reflect the client's goals, horizon, risk profile and circumstances.\nA) Model portfolios may be a starting point but cannot replace client-specific assessment. C) Record-keeping is separate. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client's assets are ₹40 lakh and liabilities ₹52 lakh. Which statement is correct?\nA) Net worth is ₹92 lakh  B) Net worth is −₹12 lakh and debt reduction is the priority  C) Net worth is ₹12 lakh  D) Net worth cannot be negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth = 40 − 52 = **−₹12 lakh**. The client is technically insolvent, so **debt management comes first**.\nA) Adds instead of subtracting. C) Ignores the sign — the answer is negative. D) Net worth certainly can be negative, which is the whole warning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these correctly ranks the *impact* on long-run portfolio outcomes?\nA) Security selection > asset allocation > market timing  B) Asset allocation has the largest impact  C) Broker choice > asset allocation  D) Timing the market reliably beats all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Empirical work consistently finds **asset allocation** explains most of the variation in portfolio returns over time.\nA) Reverses the order. C) Broker choice affects costs marginally. D) Consistent market timing is not reliably achievable.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A 60-year-old retiree needs ₹40,000 a month from a ₹90 lakh corpus. The adviser's first check should be:\nA) Whether the corpus can sustain the withdrawal after inflation  B) Which small-cap fund has the best 3-year record  C) Whether the client wants a new car  D) The client's credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The core question is **sustainability**: ₹4.8 lakh a year is a 5.3% withdrawal rate on ₹90 lakh, and that requirement will *rise* with inflation while the corpus must last decades.\nB) Chasing small-cap returns with a drawdown portfolio is dangerous. C) A discretionary want, not the first check. D) Credit score matters for borrowing, not for withdrawal sustainability.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which action best demonstrates fiduciary conduct?\nA) Recommending the product with the highest commission because it also performs adequately  B) Recommending a lower-cost direct plan that pays the adviser nothing extra  C) Not disclosing a family stake in a recommended product  D) Guaranteeing 12% returns to retain a client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choosing the option that is **best for the client even though it earns the adviser less** is fiduciary conduct in its purest form.\nA) Letting commission drive the recommendation is the conflict itself. C) Non-disclosure of a material interest is a breach. D) Guaranteeing returns is both false and prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client wants to fund a wedding in 18 months and retirement in 22 years from one lump sum. The correct approach is:\nA) Invest the whole sum in equity  B) Split by goal — short-horizon money in liquid/debt, long-horizon money in growth assets  C) Invest the whole sum in a savings account  D) Fund the wedding by borrowing and invest everything in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal-bucketing**: each goal gets an allocation matched to *its own* horizon.\nA) Risks an 18-month shortfall. C) Wastes 22 years of compounding on the retirement portion. D) Adds unnecessary interest cost and leverage risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of the following is an example of the \"risk requirement\" driving a plan revision?\nA) A client's income rises, so capacity rises  B) A client feels uneasy about volatility  C) The corpus needed implies a 16% annual return, which is unrealistic, so the goal is reset  D) The adviser changes broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the **return the goal demands** is implausible, the plan (not the portfolio) must change — save more, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.\nA) That is capacity. B) That is tolerance. D) Operational, not a planning trigger.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A client holds ₹15 lakh in a savings account \"to be safe\" while paying 11% on a ₹10 lakh personal loan. The adviser should point out:\nA) The savings balance is earning more than the loan costs  B) Using idle low-yield cash to repay high-cost debt gives a certain, tax-free 11% return  C) The loan should be increased  D) Nothing — the arrangement is optimal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying an 11% loan with cash earning ~3% is a **guaranteed 8% improvement**, with no market risk. Paying off debt is a risk-free, tax-free \"return\" equal to the interest saved.\nA) Savings rates are far below 11%. C) More debt worsens it. D) It is clearly sub-optimal — though an emergency buffer should still be retained.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these best reflects the integrated nature of financial planning?\nA) Buying a term policy has no effect on the investment plan  B) A large home loan EMI reduces investible surplus, raises the insurance need and changes the tax position  C) Tax planning is entirely separate from investment planning  D) Estate planning affects nothing else\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — One decision ripples across **cash flow, protection and tax** simultaneously — the essence of integration.\nA) The premium consumes surplus and the cover protects the plan. C) Product tax treatment directly affects post-tax returns. D) Estate decisions interact with ownership, nomination and tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An adviser tells a client \"equity always gives 15% over the long term\". This is inappropriate because:\nA) Equity returns are lower than debt  B) Past returns are not guaranteed and stating them as certain misleads the client  C) Equity is prohibited for retail investors  D) 15% is below inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Presenting a historical average as a **guarantee** is misleading. Returns vary by period, and the client may make decisions on a false premise.\nA) Equity has historically outperformed debt over long periods, but not with certainty. C) Equity is freely available to retail investors. D) 15% would comfortably exceed typical Indian inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** In which situation is rebalancing MOST clearly required?\nA) Equity has moved from 60% to 61% of the portfolio  B) A 60:40 portfolio has become 78:22 after a two-year rally  C) The client received a routine account statement  D) A fund changed its logo\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **18-percentage-point drift** means the client is carrying materially more risk than the plan intended.\nA) A 1-point drift is inside any reasonable tolerance band and rebalancing would only incur cost and tax. C) and D) are not portfolio events at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client asks the adviser to simply \"pick the best fund\". The most professional response is:\nA) Name the top-performing fund of the last year  B) Explain that the right fund depends on the goal, horizon and risk profile, and begin the planning process  C) Refuse to help  D) Recommend the fund paying the highest commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is no context-free \"best fund\". Redirecting to **goal, horizon and risk profile** is exactly what distinguishes planning from product-pushing.\nA) Chasing last year's winner is return-chasing. C) Refusal helps no one. D) A direct conflict of interest.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client has assets of ₹1,10,00,000 and liabilities of ₹42,00,000. During the year she saves ₹6,00,000 and her assets appreciate by ₹9,00,000, while she repays ₹3,00,000 of loan principal. What is her closing net worth?\nA) ₹68,00,000  B) ₹83,00,000  C) ₹86,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Work both sides of the balance sheet.\n\n**Opening net worth** = 1,10,00,000 − 42,00,000 = **₹68,00,000**.\n\n**Closing assets** = 1,10,00,000 + 6,00,000 (saved) + 9,00,000 (appreciation) − 3,00,000 (cash used to repay principal) = **₹1,22,00,000**.\n**Closing liabilities** = 42,00,000 − 3,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\n**Closing net worth** = 1,22,00,000 − 39,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nShortcut: net worth grows only by **savings + appreciation** = 6,00,000 + 9,00,000 = ₹15,00,000, so 68,00,000 + 15,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nA) ₹68,00,000 is the *opening* figure, ignoring the year's growth. C) ₹86,00,000 wrongly adds the ₹3,00,000 repayment as an increase. D) ₹80,00,000 omits part of the appreciation. **Key principle: repaying principal is net-worth neutral** — it swaps ₹3,00,000 of assets for ₹3,00,000 less liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client's goal needs ₹50,00,000 in today's terms, 15 years away, with inflation at 6% p.a. What is the goal value in future rupees (nearest lakh)?\nA) ₹95,00,000  B) ₹1,19,80,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹50,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁵. (1.06)¹⁵ ≈ **2.3966**. So ₹50,00,000 × 2.3966 ≈ **₹1,19,83,000 ≈ ₹1.20 crore**.\nA) ₹95,00,000 roughly applies simple interest (50 × 1.9). C) ₹80,00,000 badly understates the compounding. D) ₹1,50,00,000 assumes a 3× multiple, too high for 6% over 15 years. **This is why goals must be stated in future rupees — the real requirement is 2.4× the sticker price today.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client's monthly income is ₹1,50,000 and monthly expenses ₹1,05,000. What is the savings ratio?\nA) 70%  B) 30%  C) 43%  D) 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Income. Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,05,000 = **₹45,000**. Ratio = 45,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **0.30 = 30%**.\nA) 70% is the *expenses* ratio (1,05,000 ÷ 1,50,000). C) 43% wrongly divides savings by expenses (45,000 ÷ 1,05,000). D) 15% halves the correct answer. Note savings and expenses ratios must sum to 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A client holds: cash ₹50,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; equity funds ₹12,00,000; PPF ₹8,00,000; house ₹75,00,000; car ₹8,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹75,000. What is the liquidity ratio (in months)?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 3.3 months  C) 0.67 months  D) 28.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquidity ratio = **Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses**. Liquid assets = cash ₹50,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹3,00,000**. Ratio = 3,00,000 ÷ 75,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 3.3 months uses only ₹2,50,000. C) 0.67 uses cash alone. D) 28 months wrongly counts equity and PPF as liquid — equity is volatile and PPF is locked in, so neither qualifies. The client has 4 months of cover: acceptable, though the low end of the 3–6 month norm.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's data, what is the client's total asset figure and the financial assets ratio?\nA) ₹1,06,00,000 and 21.7%  B) ₹98,00,000 and 23.5%  C) ₹1,06,00,000 and 78.3%  D) ₹1,06,00,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total assets = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 75,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹1,06,00,000**. Financial assets = cash + liquid fund + equity + PPF = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹23,00,000**. Ratio = 23,00,000 ÷ 1,06,00,000 = **21.7%**.\nB) Miscounts total assets. C) 78.3% is the *physical* asset share (house + car). D) 11.3% counts only equity. **Interpretation: with 78% of wealth in a house and a car, this client is asset-rich but cannot fund goals — the classic illiquidity trap.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client's goal requires ₹1,00,00,000 in 12 years. He can invest a lump sum of ₹35,00,000 today. What annual return does the goal require?\nA) 6.0%  B) 9.15%  C) 12.5%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **risk requirement**. FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ, so r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,00,00,000 ÷ 35,00,000)^(1/12) − 1 = (2.857)^(0.08333) − 1. ln(2.857) ≈ 1.0498; ÷ 12 = 0.08748; e^0.08748 ≈ 1.0914. So r ≈ **9.15% p.a.**\nA) 6% would give only ₹70.4 lakh. C) 12.5% would overshoot to about ₹1.44 crore. D) 8% gives about ₹88 lakh — short of the goal. In Excel: `=RATE(12,0,-3500000,10000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A 30-year-old plans to retire at 60. His current annual expenses are ₹6,00,000. At 6% inflation, what will his annual expenses be in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹34,46,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years to retirement = 60 − 30 = **30**. Future expenses = ₹6,00,000 × (1.06)³⁰. (1.06)³⁰ ≈ **5.7435**. So ₹6,00,000 × 5.7435 ≈ **₹34,46,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes only a 3× rise. C) ₹24,00,000 assumes 4×. D) ₹12,00,000 assumes expenses merely double. **The lesson: over 30 years at 6%, costs rise nearly 5.75×** — which is why retirement corpora look shockingly large.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client has a home loan of ₹30,00,000 at 9%, a car loan of ₹5,00,000 at 11%, and a credit card balance of ₹1,50,000 at 42%. She has ₹2,00,000 spare. Under the *avalanche* method she should repay:\nA) The car loan, as it is mid-sized  B) The credit card first, as it has the highest rate  C) The home loan, as it is largest  D) Split equally across all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **avalanche** method attacks the **highest interest rate first**, which minimises total interest paid. At 42%, the card is by far the costliest.\nA) and C) ignore the rate. D) Splitting dilutes the benefit. *(Contrast: the **snowball** method repays the smallest balance first for psychological momentum. Here both happen to point at the card, since it is both costliest and smallest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client wants ₹20,00,000 in 6 years. She invests ₹10,00,000 today at 9% p.a. Will she reach the goal, and what is the gap?\nA) Yes, with a surplus of ₹2.4 lakh  B) No — she reaches about ₹16.77 lakh, a shortfall of about ₹3.23 lakh  C) No — she reaches ₹12 lakh  D) Yes, exactly ₹20 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = 10,00,000 × (1.09)⁶. (1.09)⁶ ≈ **1.6771**. FV ≈ **₹16,77,100**. Shortfall = 20,00,000 − 16,77,100 = **₹3,22,900**.\nA) Overstates growth. C) ₹12 lakh implies simple interest of about 3.3%. D) Would need roughly 12.2% a year. **The adviser's options: invest more now, add a monthly SIP, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's portfolio target is 50% equity, 30% debt, 20% gold on a ₹40,00,000 portfolio. Current values: equity ₹24,00,000, debt ₹10,00,000, gold ₹6,00,000. What trade rebalances it?\nA) Sell ₹4,00,000 equity; buy ₹2,00,000 debt and ₹2,00,000 gold  B) Buy ₹4,00,000 equity  C) Sell ₹2,00,000 gold  D) No action needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = ₹40,00,000. Targets: equity 50% = **₹20,00,000**; debt 30% = **₹12,00,000**; gold 20% = **₹8,00,000**. Current vs target: equity 24 vs 20 → **sell ₹4,00,000**; debt 10 vs 12 → **buy ₹2,00,000**; gold 6 vs 8 → **buy ₹2,00,000**.\nB) Increases the existing overweight. C) Gold is *under*weight, not over. D) Equity is 60% against a 50% target — a 10-point drift that clearly warrants action.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client aged 40 will need ₹80,00,000 in 20 years. Assuming 10% p.a., what lump sum is needed today (nearest ₹1,000)?\nA) ₹11,89,000  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹18,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ = 80,00,000 ÷ (1.10)²⁰. (1.10)²⁰ ≈ **6.7275**. PV = 80,00,000 ÷ 6.7275 ≈ **₹11,89,150 ≈ ₹11,89,000**.\nB) ₹40,00,000 halves the target arbitrarily. C) ₹8,00,000 divides by 10. D) ₹18,50,000 corresponds to roughly 7.6%. In Excel: `=PV(10%,20,0,-8000000)`. **Note how powerful 20 years is: under ₹12 lakh becomes ₹80 lakh.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Two clients, both aged 35 with identical incomes: A has 3 dependants and an unstable job; B is single with a government job. Which statement is correct?\nA) They must have identical portfolios since age and income match  B) B has higher risk capacity because of income stability and no dependants  C) A has higher risk capacity because of more dependants  D) Risk capacity depends only on age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** depends on horizon, **income stability**, net worth and **dependants**. B's secure income and absence of dependants both raise capacity.\nA) Identical age and income do not make identical circumstances. C) More dependants *reduce* capacity — more claims on the same income. D) Age is only one of several drivers.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A ₹25,00,000 portfolio generated a 14% return in a year in which inflation was 6%. What is the approximate real return?\nA) 8.00%  B) 7.55%  C) 20.00%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.14 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.07547 − 1 = **7.55%**.\nA) 8% is the crude subtraction (14 − 6), which always slightly *overstates* the real return. C) 20% adds them. D) 6% is the inflation rate itself. **Use the exact formula — the exam tests precisely this distinction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has monthly income ₹2,00,000, total EMIs ₹70,000, total assets ₹1,50,00,000 and total liabilities ₹60,00,000. What are the debt-to-income ratio and the solvency ratio?\nA) 35% and 1.50  B) 35% and 0.60  C) 35% and 0.40  D) 40% and 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt-to-income = EMIs ÷ Income = 70,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **35%**. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = (1,50,00,000 − 60,00,000) ÷ 1,50,00,000 = 90,00,000 ÷ 1,50,00,000 = **0.60**.\nA) 1.50 divides assets by liabilities — not the solvency ratio. C) 0.40 is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency. D) Miscomputes the EMI share (uses ₹80,000). **Note leverage + solvency = 1**, a useful self-check: 0.40 + 0.60 = 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client can save ₹25,000 a month for 10 years at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹54,80,000  C) ₹43,00,000  D) ₹66,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary annuity FV = PMT × [((1+i)ⁿ − 1) ÷ i], with i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667** and n = 120. (1.0091667)¹²⁰ ≈ **2.9853**. So FV = 25,000 × [(2.9853 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667] = 25,000 × (1.9853 ÷ 0.0091667) = 25,000 × **216.58** ≈ **₹54,14,500 ≈ ₹54.8 lakh** (small rounding differences).\nA) ₹30,00,000 is just the total invested (₹25,000 × 120), ignoring all returns. C) ₹43,00,000 understates the compounding. D) ₹66,00,000 overstates it. In Excel: `=FV(11%/12,120,-25000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: how much of that corpus is the client's own contribution, and how much is return?\nA) ₹30,00,000 contributed; about ₹24,80,000 return  B) ₹25,00,000 contributed; ₹29,80,000 return  C) ₹54,80,000 contributed; nil return  D) ₹12,00,000 contributed; ₹42,80,000 return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total contributed = ₹25,000 × 120 months = **₹30,00,000**. Corpus ≈ ₹54,80,000, so the return component ≈ 54,80,000 − 30,00,000 = **₹24,80,000**.\nB) Uses one month's contribution. C) Ignores returns entirely. D) Understates contributions. **Teaching point: over 10 years roughly 45% of the corpus came from returns — and that share rises steeply with longer horizons.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An adviser is paid a fee by the client *and* a commission by a product manufacturer for the same recommendation, without disclosure. This is:\nA) Acceptable if returns are good  B) A breach of the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations  C) Required by SEBI  D) Acceptable if the client does not ask\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Undisclosed dual compensation creates a **material conflict of interest** and breaches both the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations; SEBI additionally requires segregation of advisory and distribution.\nA) Good outcomes do not cure a conflict — the process was compromised. C) SEBI requires the opposite. D) Disclosure is proactive; silence is not consent.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client aged 50 has a ₹60,00,000 corpus and needs ₹1,50,00,000 at 60. What return is required, and what should the adviser conclude?\nA) 9.6% — comfortably achievable with a debt portfolio  B) About 9.6% — demanding but feasible with a growth-tilted portfolio, else the goal must be reset  C) 25% — easily achievable  D) 4% — very conservative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (1,50,00,000 ÷ 60,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = (2.5)^(0.1) − 1. ln(2.5) ≈ 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 ≈ 1.0959. So r ≈ **9.6% p.a.**\nA) A pure debt portfolio is unlikely to deliver 9.6% consistently. C) The required return is nowhere near 25%. D) 4% would reach only about ₹88.8 lakh. **The professional answer: 9.6% over 10 years needs real equity exposure — but the client is 50, so if capacity or tolerance won't support it, reset the goal (save more, retire later, or lower the target) rather than over-reach.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's expenses are ₹90,000 a month and he holds ₹1,80,000 in liquid assets. His job is contract-based, renewed annually. What should the adviser advise?\nA) Nothing — 2 months of cover is fine  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months, given the income insecurity  C) Invest the ₹1,80,000 in mid-cap equity  D) Reduce insurance cover to save money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity ratio = 1,80,000 ÷ 90,000 = **2 months**, below the 3–6 month norm. With **contract-based, annually renewed income**, the risk of a gap is high, so the target should sit at the *upper* end — about **₹5,40,000**.\nA) Two months is thin for insecure income. C) Moving the emergency fund into mid-caps defeats its purpose. D) Insecure income makes protection *more* important.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A client compares two options for a 3-year goal: Option A returns 12% but can fall 25% in a bad year; Option B returns 7% with negligible volatility. Which is more appropriate and why?\nA) Option A — higher return always wins  B) Option B — a 3-year horizon cannot absorb a 25% drawdown near the goal date  C) Option A, because 3 years is long term  D) Neither; the client should not invest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With only **3 years**, there is no time to recover from a large drawdown. If the fall happens in year 3, the goal simply fails.\nA) Return cannot be assessed without the horizon and the consequence of failure. C) Three years is *not* long term for volatile assets — equity generally needs 5–7 years or more. D) Not investing guarantees inflation erosion; Option B is the sound middle path.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client's total assets are ₹80,00,000, of which ₹64,00,000 is a self-occupied house. Liabilities are ₹20,00,000. Compute the solvency ratio and comment.\nA) 0.75 — strong on paper, but wealth is concentrated and illiquid  B) 0.25 — insolvent  C) 1.25 — impossible  D) 0.80 — fully liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 80,00,000 − 20,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Solvency ratio = 60,00,000 ÷ 80,00,000 = **0.75**, which is healthy.\nB) 0.25 is the leverage ratio (20 ÷ 80). C) Solvency cannot exceed 1 unless liabilities are negative. D) The portfolio is anything but liquid. **The nuance the exam wants: a strong solvency ratio can coexist with poor goal-funding ability when 80% of assets sit in a self-occupied house.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination correctly describes a client who should NOT be advised into a 100% equity portfolio?\nA) Age 25, 30-year horizon, stable income, high tolerance  B) Age 58, retiring in 2 years, sole corpus, low tolerance  C) Age 30, 20-year goal, secure job  D) Age 35, 25-year retirement goal, comfortable with volatility\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short horizon (**2 years**), the corpus is the **only** one, and **low tolerance** — every factor argues against full equity. A market fall just before retirement would be unrecoverable.\nA), C) and D) all combine long horizons with adequate capacity, so meaningful equity is defensible.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client will receive a ₹40,00,000 inheritance. Goals: clear a ₹12,00,000 loan at 13%, build a ₹4,50,000 emergency fund, and invest the rest for retirement in 20 years. What is the most sensible sequence?\nA) Invest all ₹40,00,000 in equity, keep paying the loan  B) Repay the 13% loan, fund the emergency corpus, invest the remaining ₹23,50,000 for retirement  C) Keep it all in a savings account  D) Repay the loan only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying a **13%** loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 13% return** — very hard to beat with certainty. Then secure the **₹4,50,000** emergency buffer. The remaining ₹40,00,000 − 12,00,000 − 4,50,000 = **₹23,50,000** goes to the 20-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) Carrying 13% debt while chasing uncertain returns is poor risk-adjusted logic. C) Guarantees inflation erosion over 20 years. D) Leaves the client without a buffer and wastes the compounding opportunity.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A goal needs ₹30,00,000 in 8 years. The client invests ₹18,000 a month at 10% p.a. (monthly compounding). Does she reach it?\nA) Yes, with a large surplus  B) She reaches about ₹26.4 lakh — a shortfall of roughly ₹3.6 lakh  C) She reaches exactly ₹30,00,000  D) She reaches about ₹17.3 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — i = 10% ÷ 12 = **0.008333**, n = 96. (1.008333)⁹⁶ ≈ **2.2182**. FV = 18,000 × [(2.2182 − 1) ÷ 0.008333] = 18,000 × (1.2182 ÷ 0.008333) = 18,000 × **146.19** ≈ **₹26,31,000**. Shortfall ≈ **₹3.69 lakh**.\nA) There is no surplus. C) She would need about ₹20,500 a month. D) ₹17.3 lakh is roughly the un-compounded total (₹18,000 × 96 = ₹17.28 lakh) — which is exactly the *contribution*, not the corpus. In Excel: `=FV(10%/12,96,-18000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Continuing Q94: what monthly investment would exactly meet the ₹30,00,000 goal?\nA) ₹18,000  B) About ₹20,520  C) About ₹25,000  D) About ₹31,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMT = FV ÷ annuity factor = 30,00,000 ÷ **146.19** ≈ **₹20,522**.\nA) ₹18,000 is the shortfall case from Q94. C) ₹25,000 would overshoot to roughly ₹36.5 lakh. D) ₹31,250 is simply ₹30,00,000 ÷ 96, ignoring all returns. In Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,96,0,3000000)`. **A ₹2,500 a month increase closes the entire gap — the kind of concrete, actionable answer a planner is paid for.**\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An adviser prepares a plan without asking about the client's existing life insurance. Six months later the sole earner dies with no cover, and the family's goals collapse. The principal failure was in which step?\nA) Step 1 — establishing the relationship  B) Step 2 — gathering the client's financial situation  C) Step 5 — portfolio construction  D) Step 6 — review and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Data gathering** must capture existing insurance cover; without it the protection gap is invisible and the plan is built on incomplete facts.\nA) The engagement terms were not the failure. C) The portfolio may have been fine — the *protection* was missing. D) Review comes later and cannot repair a foundational data gap. **Insurance planning is one of the eight components precisely because an uninsured death destroys every other goal.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client's portfolio target is 70:30 equity:debt with a ±5 percentage-point tolerance band. Current allocation is 73:27. The correct action is:\nA) Rebalance immediately  B) No action — the drift is inside the band  C) Move to 100% debt  D) Increase equity to 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The drift is **3 percentage points**, inside the ±5-point band. Rebalancing anyway would incur **transaction costs and capital gains tax** for no meaningful change in risk.\nA) Premature — bands exist exactly to prevent over-trading. C) An extreme move unrelated to the plan. D) Deliberately pushes the drift beyond the band.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client earns ₹18,00,000 a year, spends ₹13,50,000 and pays ₹2,40,000 in EMIs (included in expenses). What is the savings ratio, and is the debt burden reasonable?\nA) 25% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — both healthy  B) 33% savings; debt-to-income 20% — both healthy  C) 25% savings; debt-to-income 40% — debt is excessive  D) 15% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — savings too low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 18,00,000 − 13,50,000 = **₹4,50,000**; savings ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **25%**. Debt-to-income = 2,40,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **13.3%**, comfortably under the ~35–40% guideline.\nB) Miscomputes both. C) Overstates the EMI burden. D) Understates the savings ratio. **A 25% savings rate with a 13% debt burden is a genuinely strong position — the client can afford to invest toward long-term goals.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client insists on a goal requiring a 22% annual return over 7 years. The adviser's most professional response is:\nA) Promise to deliver 22% using derivatives  B) Explain that 22% sustained is unrealistic, and reset the goal by saving more, extending the horizon, or lowering the target  C) Recommend the riskiest available fund and hope  D) Tell the client the goal is impossible and end the engagement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the **risk requirement** is implausible, the honest professional response is to change the **plan variables** the client controls: contribution, time, or target.\nA) Promising returns is prohibited and false. C) Hoping is not a strategy, and the downside falls entirely on the client. D) Correct diagnosis but poor advice — the planner's job is to offer workable alternatives, not to walk away.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Rank these actions in the correct order for a 29-year-old with ₹6,00,000 of savings, no insurance, ₹3,00,000 of credit card debt at 40%, and a 30-year retirement goal.\nA) Start equity SIP → buy insurance → repay card  B) Repay the 40% card debt → build emergency fund and buy term cover → start the retirement SIP  C) Buy insurance → start SIP → repay card  D) Build emergency fund → start SIP → ignore the card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Clear the 40% debt first** — no investment reliably beats a certain 40% cost. Then **protect** (emergency fund and term cover) so a single shock cannot derail the plan. Only then **invest** the balance for the 30-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) and C) leave 40% interest compounding against the client while chasing ~12% returns. D) Ignoring 40% debt is the single most expensive mistake available here. **The universal sequence: expensive debt → protection → investment.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Read the scenario, then attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** In the real exam a 2-mark caselet like this is worth 10 marks, and a wrong answer costs −0.5.\n\n### Case: The Sharma Household\n\nAnil Sharma, aged 38, is a marketing manager earning **₹2,40,000 a month** (net). His wife Meera, 35, is a homemaker. They have one daughter, Aditi, aged 6.\n\n**Monthly expenses:** ₹1,55,000 (this includes a home loan EMI of ₹42,000 and a car loan EMI of ₹13,000).\n\n**Assets:** savings account ₹3,20,000; liquid fund ₹1,80,000; equity mutual funds ₹14,00,000; EPF ₹11,00,000; self-occupied house (market value) ₹95,00,000; car ₹7,00,000; gold jewellery ₹6,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan outstanding ₹48,00,000; car loan outstanding ₹3,50,000; credit card outstanding ₹1,20,000 (interest 38% p.a.).\n\n**Insurance:** an employer group life cover of ₹25,00,000 only. No individual term cover, no health cover beyond the employer policy.\n\n**Goals:** (i) Aditi's undergraduate education in 12 years, costing **₹25,00,000 in today's terms**; (ii) retirement at 60.\n\nAssume **inflation of 6% p.a.** unless stated otherwise.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Sharma household's net worth?\nA) ₹1,38,00,000  B) ₹85,30,000  C) ₹52,70,000  D) ₹1,25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,20,000 + 1,80,000 + 14,00,000 + 11,00,000 + 95,00,000 + 7,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,38,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 48,00,000 + 3,50,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹52,70,000**. **Net worth = 1,38,00,000 − 52,70,000 = ₹85,30,000.**\nA) ₹1,38,00,000 is total assets only. C) ₹52,70,000 is total liabilities. D) ₹1,25,00,000 does not correspond to any correct combination. *(Always compute both sides before subtracting — the distractors here are exactly the two subtotals.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the household's liquidity ratio, and how does it compare with the norm?\nA) 3.23 months — adequate  B) 2.06 months — below the 3–6 month norm  C) 12.26 months — excessive  D) 1.16 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹3,20,000 + liquid fund ₹1,80,000 = **₹5,00,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,55,000**. Liquidity ratio = 5,00,000 ÷ 1,55,000 = **3.23 months**, which just meets the lower bound of the 3–6 month norm.\nB) 2.06 wrongly uses ₹3,20,000 alone. C) 12.26 incorrectly counts equity funds as liquid — they are volatile and not an emergency asset. D) 1.16 uses ₹1,80,000 alone. **Verdict: adequate but thin.** With a sole earner and a young child, pushing toward 5–6 months (≈₹8–9 lakh) is advisable.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What will Aditi's education cost in future rupees, 12 years from now?\nA) ₹41,00,000  B) ₹50,29,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹25,00,000 × (1.06)¹². (1.06)¹² ≈ **2.01220**. So ₹25,00,000 × 2.0122 = **₹50,30,500 ≈ ₹50.29 lakh**.\nA) ₹41,00,000 understates the compounding (roughly 1.64×). C) ₹30,00,000 applies a token 20% uplift. D) ₹25,00,000 ignores inflation entirely — the most common and most damaging error. **At 6%, costs double in about 12 years** (the \"rule of 72\": 72 ÷ 6 = 12) — a shortcut worth memorising.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Ignoring existing investments, what monthly SIP at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding) is needed to accumulate the QC3 amount in 12 years?\nA) About ₹17,300  B) About ₹34,900  C) About ₹28,000  D) About ₹42,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target **₹50,30,500**; i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**; n = **144**. (1.0091667)¹⁴⁴ ≈ **3.7275**. Annuity factor = (3.7275 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 2.7275 ÷ 0.0091667 ≈ **297.55**. PMT = 50,30,500 ÷ 297.55 ≈ **₹16,907 ≈ ₹17,300** (allowing for rounding).\nB) ₹34,900 roughly doubles the requirement. C) ₹28,000 assumes a far lower return. D) ₹42,000 is close to 50,30,500 ÷ 120, ignoring returns. In Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,144,0,5030500)`. **Affordability check: the household surplus is ₹2,40,000 − ₹1,55,000 = ₹85,000 a month, so ₹17,300 is comfortably affordable.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the single most urgent gap in the Sharma household's financial plan?\nA) The equity allocation is too low  B) The absence of individual term life and health cover, with a sole earner and a dependent child  C) The gold holding is too small  D) The car loan should be prepaid first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anil is the **sole earner** supporting a homemaker spouse and a 6-year-old, carrying **₹52.70 lakh of liabilities**, and is covered only by an **employer group policy of ₹25,00,000** — which lapses the day he changes or loses his job. If he dies, ₹25 lakh does not even cover the ₹48 lakh home loan, let alone education and retirement. Every other goal in the plan is contingent on his survival.\nA) Allocation matters, but it is a return question, not a survival one. C) Gold weighting is a minor optimisation. D) The **credit card at 38%** — not the car loan — is the debt to clear first, and even that ranks below securing protection. **Priority order here: term cover + health cover → clear the 38% card → top up the emergency fund → then fund education and retirement.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "02", "title": "Time Value of Money", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **This is the single most important chapter in the syllabus.** Its formulas reappear in Chapter 4 (EMIs), Chapter 9 (bond pricing), Chapter 14 (expected returns), Chapter 16 (CAGR, annualising) and in almost every caselet. Master this and you have effectively pre-answered a large slice of the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 2.1 The Core Idea\n\n**A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow.**\n\nThree reasons:\n\n1. **Opportunity cost** — money in hand can be invested and earn a return.\n2. **Inflation** — future rupees buy less.\n3. **Risk/uncertainty** — a promised future rupee might never arrive.\n\nBecause of this, you can never simply add or compare cash flows that occur at different dates. You must first move them all to a **common point in time**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one mental model:** think of a **time line**. Money moved *forward* in time is **compounded** (multiplied by (1+r)ⁿ). Money moved *backward* is **discounted** (divided by (1+r)ⁿ). That's the entire chapter.\n\n```\n        discount ←────────────────────── compound\n   |--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n   0        1        2        3        4        5   (periods)\n   PV                                            FV\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 The Five Variables\n\nEvery TVM problem has **five** variables. Give me any four and I can solve for the fifth.\n\n| Symbol | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PV** | Present Value | Value today |\n| **FV** | Future Value | Value at a future date |\n| **r** (or `RATE`) | Rate per period | Interest/return **per period** |\n| **n** (or `NPER`) | Number of periods | Total compounding periods |\n| **PMT** | Payment | The equal cash flow each period |\n\n> ⚠️ **The #1 exam error:** mixing an *annual* rate with a *monthly* number of periods. **r and n must always use the same period.** For monthly problems: `r = annual rate ÷ 12` and `n = years × 12`.\n\n### Sign convention (matters in Excel)\nMoney you **pay out** is **negative**; money you **receive** is **positive**. If a spreadsheet returns `#NUM!`, it is almost always because every value was entered with the same sign.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.1 & 2.2.2 Future Value and Present Value\n\n### Future Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times (1 + r)^n}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹1,00,000 invested for 10 years at 9%.\nFV = 1,00,000 × (1.09)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × 2.3674 = **₹2,36,740**.\n\n### Present Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{FV}{(1 + r)^n}}$$\n\n**Example:** how much do I need today to have ₹50,00,000 in 15 years at 10%?\nPV = 50,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹⁵ = 50,00,000 ÷ 4.1772 = **₹11,96,970**.\n\n> These two are the **same equation rearranged**. Learn one and you know both.\n\n### Simple vs compound interest\n\n| | Simple interest | Compound interest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interest earned on** | Principal only | Principal **+ accumulated interest** |\n| **Formula** | FV = PV × (1 + r×n) | FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| **Growth shape** | Straight line | Accelerating curve |\n\n**₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years:** simple → ₹3,00,000; compound → **₹6,72,750**. The gap *is* the value of compounding.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.3 Rate of Return\n\nRearranging FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ:\n\n$$\\boxed{r = \\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1}$$\n\nThis is the **CAGR** (Compound Annual Growth Rate) — the single smoothed annual rate that takes PV to FV.\n\n**Example:** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years.\nr = (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = (2.5)^0.125 − 1 = 1.1214 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n> Excel: `=RATE(8,0,-400000,1000000)`.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.5 Number of Periods\n\n$$\\boxed{n = \\frac{\\ln(FV / PV)}{\\ln(1 + r)}}$$\n\n**Example:** how long for ₹5,00,000 to become ₹15,00,000 at 11%?\nn = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.11) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.10436 = **10.53 years**.\n\n### 🔑 The Rule of 72 (memorise this)\n\n$$\\textbf{Years to double} \\approx \\frac{72}{\\text{annual rate (\\%)}}$$\n\n| Rate | Doubling time |\n|---|---|\n| 6% | 12 years |\n| 8% | 9 years |\n| 9% | 8 years |\n| 12% | 6 years |\n\nUse it in reverse too: if money doubled in 6 years, the rate was ≈ 72 ÷ 6 = **12%**. It's an approximation, but exam-fast and usually within a few months.\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Compounding Frequency\n\nInterest may be credited more than once a year. More frequent compounding = **more interest**, because interest starts earning interest sooner.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m \\times n}}$$\n\nwhere **m** = compoundings per year (annual 1, half-yearly 2, quarterly 4, monthly 12, daily 365).\n\n### Effective Annual Rate (EAR)\n\nThe rate that, compounded **once a year**, gives the same result:\n\n$$\\boxed{EAR = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1}$$\n\n**₹1,00,000 at a nominal 12% for 1 year:**\n\n| Frequency | m | EAR | Value after 1 year |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Annual | 1 | 12.000% | ₹1,12,000 |\n| Half-yearly | 2 | 12.360% | ₹1,12,360 |\n| Quarterly | 4 | 12.551% | ₹1,12,551 |\n| Monthly | 12 | 12.683% | ₹1,12,683 |\n| Daily | 365 | 12.747% | ₹1,12,747 |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the **nominal** (stated) rate is the same 12% throughout — only the **effective** rate changes. Always compare products on **EAR**, never on the headline rate.\n>\n> Note the gains shrink as m rises: annual→monthly adds 0.68 percentage points; monthly→daily adds only 0.06. The limit (continuous compounding) is e^r − 1 = 12.75%.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.6 Annuities\n\nAn **annuity** is a series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n### Ordinary annuity (payment at the END of each period)\n\nThis is the default. Most EMIs and SIPs are treated this way.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}}$$\n\n**Example (FV):** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. compounded monthly.\nr = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 15 × 12 = **180**.\n(1.01)¹⁸⁰ = **5.9958**.\nFV = 10,000 × [(5.9958 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 10,000 × **499.58** = **₹49,95,800**.\n\n*(Total invested = ₹18,00,000. Returns contributed ₹31,95,800 — nearly two-thirds of the corpus.)*\n\n### Annuity due (payment at the BEGINNING of each period)\n\nEach payment sits invested for **one extra period**, so simply multiply by (1+r):\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{due}} = FV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{due}} = PV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n\nUsing the example above: FV_due = 49,95,800 × 1.01 = **₹50,45,758** — about ₹50,000 more, purely from timing.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** \"**Due** means you pay **first**\" → money works longer → the value is **always higher**. In Excel this is the `type` argument: **0 = ordinary (end)**, **1 = due (beginning)**.\n\n### 2.2.4 Solving for PMT\n\nRearranging the FV annuity formula:\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{FV \\times r}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n**Example:** to accumulate ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years at 11% p.a. (monthly):\nr = 0.0091667, n = 240, (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**.\nPMT = (1,00,00,000 × 0.0091667) ÷ (8.9006 − 1) = 91,667 ÷ 7.9006 = **₹11,602 a month**.\n\nAnd from the **PV** side (this is the **EMI** formula — see Chapter 4):\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{PV \\times r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}} = PV \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.7 Perpetuity\n\nA perpetuity is an annuity that **never ends**.\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{perpetuity}} = \\frac{PMT}{r}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 a year forever, discount rate 8% → PV = 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n### Growing perpetuity\n\nIf the payment grows at a constant rate **g** (and g < r):\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r - g}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 next year growing 4% a year forever, discounted at 8%:\nPV = 60,000 ÷ (0.08 − 0.04) = **₹15,00,000**.\n\n> ⚠️ If **g ≥ r** the formula breaks (the value is infinite) — a favourite trick question.\n\n### Growing annuity (finite)\n\n$$PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r-g}\\left[1 - \\left(\\frac{1+g}{1+r}\\right)^n\\right]$$\n\nUseful for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Real vs Nominal Return\n\nThe **nominal** return is what the statement shows. The **real** return is what your money will actually buy.\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Real return} = \\frac{1 + \\text{nominal}}{1 + \\text{inflation}} - 1}$$\n\n**Example:** nominal 9%, inflation 6%.\nReal = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.02830 − 1 = **2.83%**.\n\n> ⚠️ The rough answer (9 − 6 = 3%) **overstates** it. The exam frequently offers the crude subtraction as a distractor — use the exact formula.\n\n**Why it matters:** an FD at 7% with inflation at 6% delivers a real return of only (1.07 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **0.94%**. Before tax. After 30% tax the nominal becomes 4.9%, and the real return is (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" investment is quietly *losing* purchasing power.\n\n### Inflating a goal\n\n$$\\text{Future cost} = \\text{Today's cost} \\times (1 + \\text{inflation})^n$$\n\nAlways cost goals in **future rupees** before computing what to invest.\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Putting It Together — the standard goal calculation\n\nEvery goal caselet follows the same four steps:\n\n1. **Inflate the goal** to its future cost: `Cost × (1 + i)ⁿ`\n2. **Grow what you already have**: `Existing × (1 + r)ⁿ`\n3. **Find the gap**: step 1 − step 2\n4. **Solve for the SIP** that fills the gap: `PMT = Gap × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]`\n\n**Worked example.** Goal: education costing ₹20,00,000 today, needed in 10 years. Inflation 7%. Existing earmarked corpus ₹5,00,000. Expected return 11% p.a. (monthly compounding).\n\n1. Future cost = 20,00,000 × (1.07)¹⁰ = 20,00,000 × 1.9672 = **₹39,34,400**\n2. Existing grows = 5,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁰ = 5,00,000 × 2.8394 = **₹14,19,700**\n3. Gap = 39,34,400 − 14,19,700 = **₹25,14,700**\n4. r = 0.0091667, n = 120, (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = 2.9853\n PMT = (25,14,700 × 0.0091667) ÷ 1.9853 = 23,051 ÷ 1.9853 = **₹11,611 a month**\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Excel / Calc Functions (the test machine has a spreadsheet!)\n\n| Need | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Future value | `=FV(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])` |\n| Present value | `=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])` |\n| Payment | `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Rate | `=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Periods | `=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Effective rate | `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)` |\n| Nominal rate | `=NOMINAL(effect_rate, npery)` |\n\n**Rules:** use the **per-period** rate; enter outflows as **negative**; `type` = 0 (end) or 1 (beginning).\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Need | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| FV of lump sum | PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| PV of lump sum | FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ |\n| Rate / CAGR | (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 |\n| Periods | ln(FV÷PV) ÷ ln(1+r) |\n| Rule of 72 | Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate% |\n| Non-annual compounding | PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n) |\n| Effective annual rate | (1 + r/m)^m − 1 |\n| FV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r] |\n| PV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r] |\n| Annuity **due** | Ordinary × (1+r) |\n| PMT from FV | FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| PMT from PV (EMI) | PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| Perpetuity | PMT ÷ r |\n| Growing perpetuity | PMT₁ ÷ (r − g), needs g < r |\n| Real return | [(1+nominal) ÷ (1+inflation)] − 1 |\n| Future cost of a goal | Cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ |\n\n### The five traps that cost marks\n\n1. **Period mismatch** — annual rate with monthly periods. Convert *both*.\n2. **Ordinary vs due** — \"beginning of month\" means multiply by (1+r).\n3. **Real return by subtraction** — use the division formula.\n4. **Forgetting to inflate the goal** — today's cost is never the target.\n5. **Nominal vs effective** — compare products on EAR.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Module 1 is 37 marks and this chapter underpins most of it. If you have limited revision time, spend it here.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **These formulas must be automatic — they reappear in Chapters 4, 9, 14 and 16.**\n\n**Q1.** Why is a rupee today worth more than a rupee tomorrow?\n> **A:** Three reasons — **opportunity cost** (it can be invested today), **inflation** (future rupees buy less), and **risk** (a future rupee may never arrive).\n\n**Q2.** Name the five TVM variables.\n> **A:** **PV, FV, r (rate per period), n (number of periods), PMT.** Give any four and the fifth is solvable.\n\n**Q3.** State the future value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ**\n\n**Q4.** State the present value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ** — the same equation rearranged.\n\n**Q5.** What is the #1 mistake in TVM calculations?\n> **A:** **Period mismatch** — using an annual rate with a monthly number of periods. For monthly problems: r = annual ÷ 12 and n = years × 12.\n\n**Q6.** State the formula for the rate of return (CAGR).\n> **A:** **r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1**\n\n**Q7.** State the formula for the number of periods.\n> **A:** **n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r)**\n\n**Q8.** State the Rule of 72 and use it: money doubled in 6 years — what was the rate?\n> **A:** **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate%.** Reversed: 72 ÷ 6 = **12% p.a.**\n\n**Q9.** How long does money take to double at 8%? At 9%? At 6%?\n> **A:** **9 years** at 8%; **8 years** at 9%; **12 years** at 6%.\n\n**Q10.** Give the formula for compounding m times a year.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n)** — m = 1 annual, 2 half-yearly, 4 quarterly, 12 monthly, 365 daily.\n\n**Q11.** State the Effective Annual Rate formula.\n> **A:** **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1** — the rate that, compounded once a year, gives the same result.\n\n**Q12.** A nominal 12% compounded monthly gives what EAR?\n> **A:** (1 + 0.12/12)¹² − 1 = (1.01)¹² − 1 = **12.683%**.\n\n**Q13.** As compounding frequency rises, what happens to the nominal rate and to the effective rate?\n> **A:** The **nominal rate stays the same**; only the **effective rate rises** — and by shrinking increments (annual→monthly adds 0.68pp; monthly→daily only 0.06pp).\n\n**Q14.** What is an annuity?\n> **A:** A series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n**Q15.** State the FV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **FV = PMT × [((1 + r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q16.** State the PV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT × [(1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q17.** What is the difference between an ordinary annuity and an annuity due?\n> **A:** **Ordinary** pays at the **end** of each period; **due** pays at the **beginning**. Each due payment is invested one extra period.\n\n**Q18.** How do you convert an ordinary annuity value into an annuity due value?\n> **A:** **Multiply by (1 + r).** This works for both PV and FV. The due value is **always higher**.\n\n**Q19.** In Excel, what does the `type` argument do?\n> **A:** `type = 0` → ordinary annuity (end of period, the default). `type = 1` → annuity due (beginning of period).\n\n**Q20.** State the formula for PMT when you know the target FV.\n> **A:** **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q21.** State the formula for PMT when you know the PV (i.e. the EMI formula).\n> **A:** **PMT = PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q22.** State the present value of a perpetuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT ÷ r**\n\n**Q23.** State the present value of a growing perpetuity, and its one condition.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)**, valid only when **g < r**. If g ≥ r the value is infinite and the formula breaks.\n\n**Q24.** ₹60,000 a year forever at an 8% discount rate — what is it worth today?\n> **A:** 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n**Q25.** State the real return formula.\n> **A:** **Real = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1** — *not* nominal minus inflation.\n\n**Q26.** Nominal 9%, inflation 6%. What is the exact real return, and what does the crude method give?\n> **A:** Exact = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **2.83%**. Crude subtraction gives 3%, which **overstates** it.\n\n**Q27.** An FD yields 7%, inflation is 6%, tax is 30%. What is the post-tax real return?\n> **A:** Post-tax nominal = 7% × 0.70 = 4.9%. Real = (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" option loses purchasing power.\n\n**Q28.** How do you convert today's goal cost into the amount actually needed?\n> **A:** **Future cost = Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ.** Never target today's price.\n\n**Q29.** State the four steps of the standard goal calculation.\n> **A:** 1) **Inflate** the goal cost. 2) **Grow** the existing earmarked corpus. 3) Find the **gap**. 4) Solve for the **SIP** that fills the gap.\n\n**Q30.** Difference between simple and compound interest, with the 20-year example?\n> **A:** Simple earns on principal only (**FV = PV(1 + r×n)**); compound earns on principal *plus* accumulated interest. ₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years: simple **₹3,00,000**, compound **₹6,72,750**.\n\n**Q31.** What is the Excel sign convention, and what does `#NUM!` usually mean?\n> **A:** Money **paid out is negative**, money **received is positive**. `#NUM!` almost always means every value was entered with the **same sign**.\n\n**Q32.** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. (monthly) — what is the corpus, and how much was contributed?\n> **A:** r = 0.01, n = 180, (1.01)¹⁸⁰ = 5.9958. FV = 10,000 × 499.58 = **₹49,95,800**. Contributed = **₹18,00,000**; returns supplied nearly two-thirds.\n\n**Q33.** Which Excel functions solve for each variable?\n> **A:** `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()`, plus `=EFFECT()` and `=NOMINAL()` for rate conversion.\n\n**Q34.** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years. What is the CAGR?\n> **A:** (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = 2.5^0.125 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n**Q35.** Why must you always compare deposit products on EAR rather than the headline rate?\n> **A:** Two products can quote the same **nominal** rate but differ in **compounding frequency**, so their actual returns differ. Only the **effective** rate is comparable.\n\n**Q36.** State the finite growing-annuity PV formula and where it's used.\n> **A:** **PV = [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]** — used for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n**Q37.** What are the five classic traps in this chapter?\n> **A:** 1) Period mismatch. 2) Ordinary vs due confusion. 3) Real return by subtraction. 4) Forgetting to inflate the goal. 5) Comparing nominal instead of effective rates.\n\n**Q38.** In one sentence, what is the whole chapter?\n> **A:** Cash flows at different dates cannot be compared directly — move them along the time line by **compounding forward** ((1+r)ⁿ) or **discounting back** (÷(1+r)ⁿ).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> ⭐ **The most exam-critical bank in the guide.** Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). Keep a spreadsheet open and check each answer with `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The time value of money concept states that:\nA) All rupees are worth the same regardless of timing  B) A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow  C) A rupee tomorrow is worth more than a rupee today  D) Money loses value only when inflation is above 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Money available today can be invested (opportunity cost), buys more (inflation), and is certain (risk).\nA) Ignores all three reasons. C) Reverses the principle. D) Inflation is only one of three reasons, and it applies at any positive rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The formula for the future value of a lump sum is:\nA) FV = PV × (1 + r × n)  B) FV = PV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ  C) FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ  D) FV = PV × r × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Compounding multiplies the present value by **(1 + r)ⁿ**.\nA) Is the **simple interest** formula. B) Is present value (discounting). D) Computes only the interest under simple interest, not the future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of these is NOT one of the five TVM variables?\nA) PMT  B) NPER  C) Inflation  D) RATE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The five variables are **PV, FV, RATE, NPER and PMT**. Inflation is used *around* TVM (to inflate goals or compute real returns) but is not one of the five.\nA), B) and D) are all core variables.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In an ordinary annuity, payments occur:\nA) At the beginning of each period  B) At the end of each period  C) Only once  D) At irregular intervals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **ordinary annuity** pays at the **end** of each period; it is the default assumption.\nA) Describes an annuity **due**. C) A single payment is a lump sum, not an annuity. D) Annuities require *regular* intervals and *equal* amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The relationship between an annuity due and an ordinary annuity is:\nA) Due = Ordinary ÷ (1 + r)  B) Due = Ordinary × (1 + r)  C) They are always equal  D) Due = Ordinary × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each payment in an annuity due sits invested **one extra period**, so multiply by **(1 + r)**.\nA) Divides, which would make it smaller. C) They are never equal for r > 0. D) Multiplying by n has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The present value of a perpetuity is:\nA) PMT × r  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1 + r)  D) PMT ÷ n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A perpetuity pays forever, so **PV = PMT ÷ r**.\nA) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Perpetuities have no finite n.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The Rule of 72 estimates:\nA) The effective annual rate  B) The number of years for money to double  C) The real rate of return  D) The EMI on a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual rate (%)**.\nA) EAR uses (1 + r/m)^m − 1. C) Real return uses the ratio formula. D) EMIs use the annuity PMT formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** At 9% per annum, money approximately doubles in:\nA) 6 years  B) 8 years  C) 12 years  D) 9 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 72 ÷ 9 = **8 years**.\nA) 6 years corresponds to 12%. C) 12 years corresponds to 6%. D) 9 years corresponds to 8% — note the easy-to-confuse pair: 8% → 9 years, 9% → 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which compounding frequency produces the highest effective annual rate for a given nominal rate?\nA) Annual  B) Half-yearly  C) Quarterly  D) Daily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The more often interest is credited, the sooner it starts earning interest itself, so **daily** gives the highest EAR.\nA), B) and C) all compound less frequently. For a 12% nominal: annual 12.000%, half-yearly 12.360%, quarterly 12.551%, daily 12.747%.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The effective annual rate formula is:\nA) (1 + r × m) − 1  B) (1 + r/m)^m − 1  C) (1 + r)^m − 1  D) r ÷ m\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1**: divide the nominal rate by the frequency, compound it m times, subtract 1.\nA) Uses simple interest. C) Fails to divide the nominal rate by m, overstating the result badly. D) Gives the periodic rate, not the annual effective rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The real rate of return is calculated as:\nA) Nominal − inflation  B) [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1  C) Nominal × inflation  D) Nominal ÷ inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exact formula divides the growth factors.\nA) The crude subtraction **overstates** the real return and is the most common distractor. C) and D) have no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In Excel, money paid out should be entered as:\nA) A positive number  B) A negative number  C) Zero  D) Either, it makes no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The sign convention is **outflows negative, inflows positive**.\nA) Would misstate the direction of the cash flow. C) Zero would remove the cash flow entirely. D) It matters greatly — same-sign entries typically produce `#NUM!`.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which Excel function solves for the periodic payment?\nA) `=FV()`  B) `=PV()`  C) `=PMT()`  D) `=NPER()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])`.\nA) Solves for future value. B) Solves for present value. D) Solves for the number of periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Simple interest differs from compound interest because simple interest:\nA) Is earned on principal plus accumulated interest  B) Is earned on the principal only  C) Grows exponentially  D) Always produces a higher value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Simple interest is computed on the **original principal only**, giving straight-line growth.\nA) and C) describe compound interest. D) Compound interest always produces more for n > 1 and r > 0.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The FV of an ordinary annuity is:\nA) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1+r)ⁿ  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The annuity factor is **[((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is a perpetuity. C) Compounds a single payment. D) Ignores interest completely — that is just the total contributed.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which of the following is an annuity?\nA) A one-time bonus of ₹5,00,000  B) A monthly SIP of ₹10,000 for 10 years  C) Irregular dividend payments  D) A lump-sum inheritance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An annuity requires **equal amounts at regular intervals** — a SIP fits exactly.\nA) and D) are lump sums. C) Dividends vary in amount and timing, so they are not an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** For a growing perpetuity, the formula PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) requires:\nA) g > r  B) g < r  C) g = r  D) g = 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth must be **less than** the discount rate, otherwise the denominator is zero or negative and the value is infinite/meaningless.\nA) and C) break the formula. D) g = 0 is allowed but reduces it to an ordinary perpetuity — it is not a *requirement*.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** When converting an annual rate to a monthly rate for TVM purposes, you should:\nA) Multiply the annual rate by 12  B) Divide the annual rate by 12  C) Leave it unchanged  D) Divide by 365\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Use **r ÷ 12** for the monthly periodic rate, together with n = years × 12.\nA) Multiplying inflates the rate twelve-fold. C) Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the classic error. D) 365 applies to daily compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Discounting means:\nA) Moving a cash flow forward in time  B) Moving a cash flow backward in time to find its present value  C) Reducing the interest rate  D) Applying a sales rebate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Discounting divides by (1+r)ⁿ to bring a future amount **back** to today.\nA) That is compounding. C) and D) confuse the financial meaning of \"discount\" with everyday usage.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement about the nominal rate is correct when compounding frequency increases?\nA) The nominal rate rises  B) The nominal rate stays the same while the effective rate rises  C) Both fall  D) The effective rate stays the same\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **stated/nominal** rate is unchanged; only the **effective** rate rises.\nA) and C) misstate the direction. D) The effective rate is precisely what changes.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** ₹1,00,000 at 10% simple interest for 20 years grows to:\nA) ₹6,72,750  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = PV × (1 + r×n) = 1,00,000 × (1 + 0.10 × 20) = 1,00,000 × 3 = **₹3,00,000**.\nA) ₹6,72,750 is the **compound** result. C) ₹2,00,000 would be 10 years of simple interest. D) ₹1,10,000 is one year only.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The PV of an ordinary annuity formula is:\nA) PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]  B) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  C) PMT ÷ (r − g)  D) PMT × (1+r)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The PV annuity factor is **[(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is the **FV** annuity factor. C) Is a growing perpetuity. D) Is the annuity-due adjustment.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** In the Excel `type` argument, `type = 1` means:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due (payment at the beginning)  C) Simple interest  D) Continuous compounding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `type = 1` shifts payments to the **beginning** of each period.\nA) `type = 0` is the ordinary annuity default. C) and D) are not controlled by `type`.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** To find the rate given PV, FV and n, you use:\nA) r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1  B) r = (FV − PV) ÷ n  C) r = FV ÷ PV  D) r = ln(FV ÷ PV)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Take the nth root of the growth multiple and subtract 1.\nB) Computes an average rupee gain, not a rate. C) Gives the total multiple. D) Is part of the **NPER** formula, not the rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An investment doubles in 12 years. Using the Rule of 72, the approximate rate is:\nA) 12%  B) 6%  C) 8%  D) 9%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rate ≈ 72 ÷ 12 = **6%**.\nA) 12% doubles in 6 years. C) 8% doubles in 9 years. D) 9% doubles in 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of these is a use of the present value concept?\nA) Deciding how much to invest today for a future goal  B) Calculating simple interest  C) Measuring inflation  D) Preparing a balance sheet\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV answers exactly the question \"how much do I need **today**?\"\nB) Simple interest has its own formula. C) Inflation is measured by price indices. D) Balance sheets record current values, not discounted ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The formula n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r) solves for:\nA) The rate  B) The number of periods  C) The payment  D) The present value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taking logs of FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ isolates **n**.\nA) The rate uses the nth-root formula. C) and D) have their own formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A nominal rate of 12% compounded quarterly gives a periodic rate of:\nA) 12%  B) 4%  C) 3%  D) 1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Quarterly means m = 4, so the periodic rate is 12% ÷ 4 = **3%**.\nA) Is the annual nominal rate. B) 4% would be m = 3. D) 1% is the monthly rate (m = 12).\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following will always be true for r > 0 and n > 1?\nA) Compound value < simple value  B) Compound value > simple value  C) They are equal  D) It depends on inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compounding earns interest on interest, so it always exceeds simple interest beyond the first period.\nA) Reverses it. C) They are equal only at n = 1. D) Inflation is irrelevant to this comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The future cost of a goal is calculated as:\nA) Today's cost ÷ (1 + inflation)ⁿ  B) Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ  C) Today's cost × inflation × n  D) Today's cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goals must be inflated forward to the date they will be paid.\nA) Discounts instead of inflating. C) Uses simple growth. D) Ignoring inflation is the single most damaging planning error.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which Excel function converts a nominal rate to an effective rate?\nA) `=NOMINAL()`  B) `=EFFECT()`  C) `=RATE()`  D) `=FV()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)`.\nA) Does the reverse (effective → nominal). C) Solves for the periodic rate in a TVM problem. D) Computes future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a SIP where the investment is made on the 1st of every month, the appropriate treatment is:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due  C) Perpetuity  D) Lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Payment at the **beginning** of the period is an **annuity due**.\nA) Would apply to end-of-month investment. C) A SIP has a finite term. D) A SIP is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Continuous compounding of a 12% nominal rate gives an effective rate of approximately:\nA) 12.00%  B) 12.68%  C) 12.75%  D) 13.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The limit is e^r − 1 = e^0.12 − 1 = 1.1275 − 1 = **12.75%**.\nA) Is annual compounding. B) Is monthly compounding. D) Overstates it — continuous compounding is the *ceiling*, only marginally above daily (12.747%).\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which of these correctly describes the effect of a longer horizon on compounding?\nA) The effect is linear  B) The effect accelerates — later years add much more than early years  C) The effect diminishes over time  D) There is no effect after 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth is exponential, so each successive year adds more in absolute terms than the last.\nA) Linear growth describes simple interest. C) and D) contradict the exponential nature of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A `#NUM!` error in an Excel TVM formula usually means:\nA) The rate is too high  B) All cash flows were entered with the same sign  C) The function name is misspelled  D) The file is corrupted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TVM functions require at least one cash flow of the **opposite sign**.\nA) High rates compute fine. C) A misspelling gives `#NAME?`. D) Corruption would produce a different failure.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** ₹2,00,000 is invested for 7 years at 8% p.a. compounded annually. What is the future value?\nA) ₹3,42,780  B) ₹3,12,000  C) ₹2,16,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 2,00,000 × (1.08)⁷. (1.08)⁷ = **1.71382**. FV = 2,00,000 × 1.71382 = **₹3,42,764 ≈ ₹3,42,780**.\nB) ₹3,12,000 applies simple interest (2,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹2,16,000 is one year's growth. D) ₹4,00,000 would need about 10.5 years at 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** How much must be invested today to have ₹25,00,000 in 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) ₹7,96,600  B) ₹12,50,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = 25,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹². (1.10)¹² = **3.13843**. PV = 25,00,000 ÷ 3.13843 = **₹7,96,575 ≈ ₹7,96,600**.\nB) ₹12,50,000 simply halves the target. C) ₹5,00,000 is far too low (it would grow to only ₹15.7 lakh). D) ₹10,00,000 would grow to ₹31.4 lakh. Excel: `=PV(10%,12,0,-2500000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** ₹5,00,000 grows to ₹12,50,000 in 10 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 15.00%  B) 9.60%  C) 25.00%  D) 7.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (12,50,000 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = 2.5^0.1 − 1. ln(2.5) = 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 = 1.09596. r = **9.60% p.a.**\nA) 15% would give about ₹20.2 lakh. C) 25% is the *total* gain divided by 10 years — a simple-interest style error. D) 7.20% gives only about ₹10 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** ₹15,000 invested monthly for 20 years at 12% p.a. (monthly compounding) accumulates to approximately:\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹1,49,88,000  C) ₹75,00,000  D) ₹2,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 240. (1.01)²⁴⁰ = **10.8926**. FV = 15,000 × [(10.8926 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 15,000 × **989.26** = **₹1,48,38,900 ≈ ₹1.49 crore**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is just the total contributed (15,000 × 240). C) ₹75,00,000 understates the compounding badly. D) ₹2.5 crore overstates it. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,240,-15000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** What is the effective annual rate of 10% nominal compounded quarterly?\nA) 10.00%  B) 10.38%  C) 10.47%  D) 11.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.10/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.025)⁴ − 1 = 1.10381 − 1 = **10.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 10.47% corresponds to monthly compounding. D) 11% overstates it. Excel: `=EFFECT(10%,4)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A nominal 9% is compounded monthly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.00%  B) 9.38%  C) 9.20%  D) 9.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.09/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0075)¹² − 1 = 1.09381 − 1 = **9.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 9.20% corresponds roughly to half-yearly compounding. D) 9.75% overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A nominal return of 11% with inflation at 6.5% gives a real return of:\nA) 4.50%  B) 4.23%  C) 5.00%  D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real = (1.11 ÷ 1.065) − 1 = 1.042254 − 1 = **4.23%**.\nA) 4.50% is the crude subtraction (11 − 6.5), which overstates the real return. C) and D) do not follow from the formula. **The exam almost always offers the subtraction as a distractor.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A goal costs ₹12,00,000 today and is 8 years away. At 7% inflation, the future cost is:\nA) ₹18,72,000  B) ₹20,61,700  C) ₹16,80,000  D) ₹24,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 12,00,000 × (1.07)⁸. (1.07)⁸ = **1.71819**. = **₹20,61,828 ≈ ₹20,61,700**.\nA) ₹18,72,000 applies simple inflation (12,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹16,80,000 assumes only a 40% rise. D) ₹24,00,000 assumes doubling, which at 7% takes about 10.3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor needs ₹40,00,000 in 15 years and expects 10% p.a. What monthly SIP is required?\nA) ₹9,650  B) ₹22,200  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹31,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.10 ÷ 12 = **0.008333**; n = 180. (1.008333)¹⁸⁰ = **4.4539**. Annuity factor = (4.4539 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **414.47**. PMT = 40,00,000 ÷ 414.47 = **₹9,651**.\nB) ₹22,200 corresponds to a much lower assumed return. C) ₹15,000 would overshoot to about ₹62 lakh. D) ₹31,000 is close to 40,00,000 ÷ 180 (₹22,222) inflated — it ignores compounding. Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,180,0,4000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** ₹8,000 is invested at the **beginning** of every month for 10 years at 12% p.a. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹18,41,200  B) ₹18,59,600  C) ₹9,60,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 8,000 × [((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × [(3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × **230.039** = ₹18,40,312. Annuity due = × 1.01 = **₹18,58,715 ≈ ₹18,59,600**.\nA) ₹18,41,200 is the **ordinary** annuity value — it forgets the (1+r) adjustment. C) ₹9,60,000 is the total contributed. D) ₹16,00,000 understates the growth. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,120,-8000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A pension of ₹50,000 per year is expected in perpetuity. At a 9% discount rate, its present value is:\nA) ₹4,50,000  B) ₹5,55,556  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV = PMT ÷ r = 50,000 ÷ 0.09 = **₹5,55,556**.\nA) ₹4,50,000 multiplies instead of dividing (50,000 × 9). C) ₹50,00,000 divides by 0.01. D) ₹9,00,000 has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A payment of ₹1,00,000 next year grows 5% a year forever. At a 10% discount rate, the present value is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹6,66,667  D) Infinite\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growing perpetuity: PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) = 1,00,000 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) = 1,00,000 ÷ 0.05 = **₹20,00,000**.\nA) ₹10,00,000 ignores growth (uses r alone). C) ₹6,66,667 uses r + g. D) Infinite would apply only if g ≥ r. **Growth halves the effective discount rate here, doubling the value.**\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** How long will ₹3,00,000 take to become ₹9,00,000 at 12% p.a.?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 9.69 years  C) 12.0 years  D) 8.0 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(9,00,000 ÷ 3,00,000) ÷ ln(1.12) = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.11333 = **9.69 years**.\nA) 6 years only doubles it (Rule of 72: 72 ÷ 12 = 6). C) and D) do not follow. **Sanity check: doubling takes 6 years, so tripling must take more — but less than 12.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Two deposits offer 10% compounded annually and 9.8% compounded monthly. Which is better?\nA) The 10% annual, since the headline rate is higher  B) The 9.8% monthly, because its EAR is about 10.25%  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR of 9.8% monthly = (1 + 0.098/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0081667)¹² − 1 = **10.25%**, which beats the 10.00% EAR of the annual option.\nA) Comparing headline rates is exactly the trap. C) They differ by 0.25 percentage points. D) EAR is precisely the tool that makes them comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor wants ₹75,00,000 in 18 years. She has ₹10,00,000 today earning 11%. What is the shortfall at the goal date?\nA) ₹9,56,450  B) ₹10,58,000  C) Nil — she will exceed the goal  D) ₹65,43,550\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Grow the existing corpus first: 10,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁸. (1.11)¹⁸ = **6.54355**, so the corpus reaches **₹65,43,550**. Shortfall = 75,00,000 − 65,43,550 = **₹9,56,450**.\nB) ₹10,58,000 overstates the gap. C) She falls short, not over. D) ₹65,43,550 is the grown corpus itself, not the gap. *(Always compute the grown corpus first, then subtract from the target.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** ₹20,000 a month for 25 years at 11% p.a. gives a corpus of approximately:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹3,15,27,000  C) ₹1,50,00,000  D) ₹6,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.0091667, n = 300. (1.0091667)³⁰⁰ = **15.4499**. Annuity factor = (15.4499 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **1576.36**. FV = 20,000 × 1576.36 = **₹3,15,27,200**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is simply the total contributed (20,000 × 300). C) ₹1.5 crore understates the growth by half. D) ₹6.5 crore overstates it. **Over 25 years, ₹60 lakh of contributions becomes about ₹3.15 crore — roughly 81% of the corpus is return.**\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which is worth more today: ₹10,00,000 received in 5 years, or ₹6,00,000 received today, at an 8% discount rate?\nA) The ₹10,00,000 in 5 years, worth about ₹6,80,600 today  B) The ₹6,00,000 today  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of ₹10,00,000 = 10,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁵ = 10,00,000 ÷ **1.46933** = **₹6,80,583**, which exceeds ₹6,00,000.\nB) Would be right only at a discount rate above about 10.8%. C) They differ by roughly ₹80,000. D) With PV, FV, n and r known, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An EMI is best described as which TVM concept?\nA) The FV of an annuity  B) The PMT that makes the PV of payments equal the loan amount  C) A perpetuity  D) A lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A loan is a **present value**; the EMI is the payment whose present value equals the loan amount: **PMT = PV × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**.\nA) Uses the FV form, appropriate for savings not loans. C) Loans have a finite term. D) An EMI is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An investor's portfolio returns 14% nominal in a year with 5% inflation and pays 20% tax on gains. What is the post-tax real return?\nA) 9.00%  B) 6.29%  C) 11.20%  D) 5.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Two steps, in this order. **Tax first:** post-tax nominal = 14% × (1 − 0.20) = **11.2%**. **Then deflate:** real = (1.112 ÷ 1.05) − 1 = 1.05905 − 1 = **5.90%**.\nA) 9% subtracts inflation from the *pre-tax* return, ignoring tax altogether. B) 6.29% adjusts for inflation first and taxes afterwards — the wrong order. C) 11.2% is the post-tax nominal return, before inflation. **Always tax first, then deflate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** ₹1,00,000 invested at 8% compounded half-yearly for 5 years gives:\nA) ₹1,46,933  B) ₹1,48,024  C) ₹1,40,000  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — m = 2, so the periodic rate is 4% and n = 10 periods. FV = 1,00,000 × (1.04)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × **1.48024** = **₹1,48,024**.\nA) ₹1,46,933 is **annual** compounding ((1.08)⁵). C) ₹1,40,000 is simple interest. D) ₹1,60,000 overstates it. **Note half-yearly beats annual by ₹1,091.**\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client will receive ₹5,00,000 a year for 12 years, first payment one year from now. At 9%, the present value is:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹35,79,000  C) ₹45,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV annuity = PMT × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09]. (1.09)¹² = **2.81266**, so (1.09)⁻¹² = **0.35553**. Factor = (1 − 0.35553) ÷ 0.09 = 0.64447 ÷ 0.09 = **7.1608**. PV = 5,00,000 × 7.1608 = **₹35,80,400 ≈ ₹35,79,000**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is the undiscounted total (5,00,000 × 12). C) and D) do not follow from the factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** If a SIP is switched from end-of-month to beginning-of-month, the corpus:\nA) Falls  B) Rises by a factor of (1 + r)  C) Is unchanged  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every payment gains one extra period of compounding, so the whole series is multiplied by **(1 + r)**.\nA) It rises, not falls. C) Timing genuinely matters. D) The uplift is one period's interest — typically well under 1% for monthly SIPs, not 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client has 25 years to retirement and can invest ₹12,000 a month at 12%. Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹2,25,00,000  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.01, n = 300. (1.01)³⁰⁰ = **19.7885**. Factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**. FV = 12,000 × 1878.85 = **₹2,25,46,200**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed. C) ₹1 crore understates it by half. D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Roughly 84% of the corpus is return — the case for starting early in one number.**\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A bank quotes 7.5% compounded quarterly on an FD. Inflation is 6% and the client's tax rate is 30%. The post-tax real return is approximately:\nA) +1.5%  B) −0.6%  C) +0.9%  D) −2.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.075/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.01875)⁴ − 1 = **7.71%**. Post-tax = 7.71% × 0.70 = **5.40%**. Real = (1.0540 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−0.57% ≈ −0.6%**.\nA) +1.5% ignores tax. C) +0.9% ignores either tax or inflation. D) −2.0% overstates the erosion. **The \"safe\" FD delivers a negative real return after tax — a core planning insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An investment of ₹6,00,000 becomes ₹6,00,000 after 10 years in nominal terms, with inflation at 6%. What happened in real terms?\nA) The investor broke even  B) The investor lost about 44% of purchasing power  C) The investor gained  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In real terms the value is 6,00,000 ÷ (1.06)¹⁰ = 6,00,000 ÷ **1.79085** = **₹3,35,040** in today's money — a loss of about **44%** of purchasing power.\nA) Nominal break-even is not real break-even. C) There is no gain at all. D) With the inflation rate given, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which pair of inputs is internally consistent for a monthly SIP over 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) r = 10%, n = 12  B) r = 0.10/12, n = 144  C) r = 0.10, n = 144  D) r = 0.10/12, n = 12\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Monthly rate = **0.10 ÷ 12** and monthly periods = 12 years × 12 = **144**. Both must be monthly.\nA) Uses annual rate with 12 periods — that describes 12 *years* of annual compounding, not a monthly SIP. C) Mixes an annual rate with monthly periods — the classic error. D) Mixes a monthly rate with only 12 periods (i.e. one year).\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client needs ₹18,00,000 in 6 years and can earn 9%. She invests ₹6,00,000 today. What additional annual (year-end) investment is needed?\nA) ₹1,05,500  B) ₹1,45,700  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Three steps. **Grow the lump sum:** 6,00,000 × (1.09)⁶ = 6,00,000 × **1.67710** = **₹10,06,260**. **Find the gap:** 18,00,000 − 10,06,260 = **₹7,93,740**. **Annuitise it:** factor = ((1.09)⁶ − 1) ÷ 0.09 = 0.6771 ÷ 0.09 = **7.5233**; PMT = 7,93,740 ÷ 7.5233 = **₹1,05,504**.\nB) ₹1,45,700 ignores the existing lump sum's growth. C) ₹3,00,000 is roughly the gap ÷ 6, ignoring returns. D) ₹50,000 falls far short. Excel: `=PMT(9%,6,0,-793740)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The PV of an annuity **falls** when:\nA) The discount rate falls  B) The discount rate rises  C) The number of payments rises  D) The payment amount rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher discount rate shrinks every future cash flow, so the present value falls.\nA) A lower rate raises PV. C) More payments raise PV. D) Larger payments raise PV.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client compares a lump sum of ₹25,00,000 today against ₹3,00,000 a year for 12 years. At 8%, which is better?\nA) The annuity, worth about ₹22,60,000  B) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹22,60,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of the annuity = 3,00,000 × [(1 − (1.08)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.08]. (1.08)¹² = **2.51817**, so (1.08)⁻¹² = **0.39711**. Factor = 0.60289 ÷ 0.08 = **7.5361**. PV = 3,00,000 × 7.5361 = **₹22,60,830**. The lump sum of ₹25,00,000 is worth more today.\nA) Correctly values the annuity but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹2.4 lakh. D) ₹36,00,000 is the *undiscounted* total, which ignores the time value entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Doubling the investment horizon from 10 to 20 years at 12% multiplies the lump-sum corpus by approximately:\nA) 2 times  B) 3.1 times  C) 1.5 times  D) 10 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.12)¹⁰ = **3.1058**; (1.12)²⁰ = **9.6463**. Ratio = 9.6463 ÷ 3.1058 = **3.11 times**.\nA) Doubling time does *not* double the corpus — that is the linear intuition compounding defeats. C) 1.5× badly understates it. D) 10× is the total multiple over 20 years, not the incremental effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** An annuity due of ₹1,00,000 a year for 10 years at 10% has a present value of:\nA) ₹6,14,460  B) ₹6,75,900  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹5,58,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary PV = 1,00,000 × [(1 − (1.10)⁻¹⁰) ÷ 0.10]. (1.10)¹⁰ = **2.59374**, so (1.10)⁻¹⁰ = **0.38554**. Factor = 0.61446 ÷ 0.10 = **6.14457**. Ordinary PV = **₹6,14,457**. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,75,903**.\nA) ₹6,14,460 is the **ordinary** value, missing the (1+r) step. C) ₹10,00,000 is undiscounted. D) ₹5,58,600 over-discounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which statement about compounding frequency is TRUE?\nA) The benefit of higher frequency grows without limit  B) The benefit rises but with diminishing increments, approaching continuous compounding  C) Frequency has no effect  D) Annual compounding always gives the most\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each increase in frequency adds less than the last, converging on **e^r − 1**.\nA) The benefit is bounded by continuous compounding. C) Frequency clearly matters. D) Annual gives the *least*.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A goal is ₹30,00,000 in 10 years. The client can invest ₹15,000 a month. What return is needed (approximately)?\nA) About 5%  B) About 9.9%  C) About 15%  D) About 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total contributed = 15,000 × 120 = ₹18,00,000, and the target is ₹30,00,000, so a moderate return is needed. Testing 10%: factor = ((1.008333)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**; FV = 15,000 × 204.84 = **₹30,72,600** — slightly above target, so the required rate is just under 10%, about **9.9%**.\nA) 5% gives about ₹23.3 lakh. C) 15% gives about ₹41.8 lakh. D) 2% gives about ₹19.9 lakh. Excel: `=RATE(120,-15000,0,3000000)*12`.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A retiree wants ₹40,000 a month for 25 years from a corpus earning 8% p.a. What corpus is needed at retirement?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹51,84,000  C) ₹2,40,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **PV of an ordinary annuity**. r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**; n = 300. (1.0066667)³⁰⁰ = **7.34018**, so the inverse is **0.13624**. Factor = (1 − 0.13624) ÷ 0.0066667 = 0.86376 ÷ 0.0066667 = **129.56**. Corpus = 40,000 × 129.56 = **₹51,82,400 ≈ ₹51,84,000**.\nA) ₹1.2 crore is the undiscounted total (40,000 × 300). C) ₹2.4 crore doubles that. D) ₹80,00,000 overstates the requirement. **Note the corpus needed is far below the total withdrawn, because the balance keeps earning.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** In the standard goal calculation, the correct sequence is:\nA) Find the SIP → inflate the goal → grow existing assets  B) Inflate the goal → grow existing assets → find the gap → solve for the SIP  C) Grow existing assets → find the SIP → inflate the goal  D) Find the gap → inflate the goal → solve for the SIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot size a SIP until you know the **gap**, and you cannot know the gap until the goal is inflated and existing assets are grown to the same date.\nA), C) and D) all attempt to compute a later step before its inputs exist.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client invests ₹3,00,000 today and ₹5,000 a month for 15 years, both earning 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). What is the total corpus?\nA) ₹15,55,230  B) ₹38,37,480  C) ₹22,82,250  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute the two components separately, then add.\n**Lump sum:** r = 0.0091667, n = 180. (1.0091667)¹⁸⁰ = **5.1841**. FV = 3,00,000 × 5.1841 = **₹15,55,230**.\n**SIP:** factor = (5.1841 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 4.1841 ÷ 0.0091667 = **456.45**. FV = 5,000 × 456.45 = **₹22,82,250**.\n**Total = 15,55,230 + 22,82,250 = ₹38,37,480.**\nA) ₹15,55,230 is the lump-sum component alone. C) ₹22,82,250 is the SIP component alone. D) ₹12,00,000 is roughly the total SIP contributed (5,000 × 180 = ₹9,00,000) plus the initial ₹3,00,000, ignoring all growth. **Method: never mix a lump sum and an annuity in one formula — compute each and add.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client needs ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years. She has ₹8,00,000 today and can invest monthly. Expected return 11% p.a. What monthly SIP fills the gap?\nA) ₹3,341  B) ₹8,120  C) ₹11,600  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Step 1 — grow what she has:** (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**; 8,00,000 × 8.9006 = **₹71,20,480**. **Step 2 — gap:** 1,00,00,000 − 71,20,480 = **₹28,79,520**. **Step 3 — annuity factor:** (8.9006 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 7.9006 ÷ 0.0091667 = **862.0**. **Step 4 — SIP:** 28,79,520 ÷ 862.0 = **₹3,341 a month**.\nB) ₹8,120 ignores part of the existing corpus's growth. C) ₹11,600 is the SIP needed if she had *nothing* saved. D) ₹15,000 overstates it further. **Key insight: the ₹8 lakh already invested does over 70% of the work — the power of a 20-year head start.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Two investors: A invests ₹10,000 a month from age 25 to 35 (10 years) then stops and lets it grow to 60. B invests ₹10,000 a month from 35 to 60 (25 years). Both earn 12% p.a. Who ends with more at 60?\nA) B, because she invested for 25 years  B) A, despite investing for only 10 years  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Investor A:** SIP for 120 months: factor = ((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01 = (3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **230.04**; corpus at 35 = 10,000 × 230.04 = **₹23,00,400**. This then grows untouched for 25 years: × (1.01)³⁰⁰ = × **19.7885** = **₹4,55,17,000**.\n**Investor B:** SIP for 300 months: factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**; corpus = 10,000 × 1878.85 = **₹1,87,88,500**.\n**A ends with ~₹4.55 crore from ₹12 lakh invested; B with ~₹1.88 crore from ₹30 lakh invested.**\nA) Reverses the result. C) They differ by more than 2×. D) It is fully determinable. **This is the single most powerful illustration of compounding: time beats amount.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A ₹50,00,000 home loan at 9% for 20 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹44,986  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹41,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]. r = 0.09 ÷ 12 = **0.0075**; n = 240. (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**. EMI = 50,00,000 × [0.0075 × 6.00915] ÷ [6.00915 − 1] = 50,00,000 × 0.04506862 ÷ 5.00915 = **₹44,986**.\nB) ₹37,500 is merely the first month's interest (50,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹52,000 overstates it. D) ₹41,200 corresponds to a lower rate. Excel: `=PMT(9%/12,240,-5000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's loan, what is the total amount repaid and the total interest?\nA) ₹1,07,96,640 repaid; ₹57,96,640 interest  B) ₹50,00,000 repaid; nil interest  C) ₹90,00,000 repaid; ₹40,00,000 interest  D) ₹67,47,900 repaid; ₹17,47,900 interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total repaid = EMI × n = ₹44,986 × 240 = **₹1,07,96,640**. Interest = 1,07,96,640 − 50,00,000 = **₹57,96,640**.\nB) Ignores interest entirely. C) and D) understate it. **The borrower pays more in interest than the original principal — which is why loan tenure is the most powerful lever in Chapter 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client can choose: (i) ₹20,00,000 today, or (ii) ₹2,50,000 a year for 15 years starting next year. At what discount rate are the two equal?\nA) About 9.1%  B) About 12.5%  C) About 5%  D) About 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Find the rate at which the annuity's PV equals ₹20,00,000, i.e. an annuity factor of 20,00,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **8.0**. Test rates: at 8%, factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻¹⁵) ÷ 0.08 = (1 − 0.31524) ÷ 0.08 = **8.559** (too high). At 9%: (1 − 0.27454) ÷ 0.09 = **8.061**. At 9.2%: ≈ **7.97**. The factor hits 8.0 at roughly **9.1%**.\nB), C) and D) are all well away from the break-even. **Interpretation: if the client can earn more than about 9.1%, take the lump sum; if less, take the annuity.** Excel: `=RATE(15,2500000,-20000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A goal costs ₹15,00,000 today, is 10 years away, inflation is 6%, and the client has ₹4,00,000 earmarked earning 10%. What monthly SIP at 10% closes the gap?\nA) ₹5,950  B) ₹8,050  C) ₹13,110  D) ₹3,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The four-step template:\n**Step 1 — inflate the goal:** 15,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁰ = 15,00,000 × **1.79085** = **₹26,86,275**.\n**Step 2 — grow existing assets:** 4,00,000 × (1.10)¹⁰ = 4,00,000 × **2.59374** = **₹10,37,496**.\n**Step 3 — gap:** 26,86,275 − 10,37,496 = **₹16,48,779**.\n**Step 4 — solve for the SIP:** r = 0.008333, n = 120, (1.008333)¹²⁰ = **2.70704**; factor = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**. PMT = 16,48,779 ÷ 204.84 = **₹8,049**.\n\nA) ₹5,950 understates it. C) ₹13,110 is the SIP needed if the existing ₹4,00,000 were ignored entirely. D) ₹3,200 is far too low. **This four-step method is the template for every goal caselet in the exam.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor earns 15% in year 1, −10% in year 2 and 20% in year 3. What is the CAGR?\nA) 8.33%  B) 7.49%  C) 24.20%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compound the growth factors, never average the percentages: 1.15 × 0.90 × 1.20 = **1.2420**. CAGR = (1.2420)^(1/3) − 1. ln(1.2420) = 0.21675; ÷ 3 = 0.07225; e^0.07225 = **1.07492**. CAGR = **7.49% p.a.**\nA) 8.33% is the **arithmetic mean** ((15 − 10 + 20) ÷ 3), which always **overstates** the compounded result when returns vary. C) 24.20% is the *cumulative* 3-year gain misread as annual. D) 6% understates it. **Key point: geometric mean (CAGR) < arithmetic mean whenever returns are volatile — see Chapter 16.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client deposits ₹1,00,000 at the start of each year for 5 years at 10%. What is the value at the end of year 5?\nA) ₹6,10,510  B) ₹6,71,561  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹5,52,560\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beginning-of-year deposits form an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 1,00,000 × [((1.10)⁵ − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × [(1.61051 − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × **6.1051** = ₹6,10,510. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,71,561**.\nA) ₹6,10,510 is the ordinary annuity value — the classic omission. C) ₹5,00,000 is the total deposited. D) ₹5,52,560 under-compounds. Excel: `=FV(10%,5,-100000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹10,00,000 investment must grow to ₹40,00,000. At 12% p.a., how long will it take?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 12.2 years  C) 18.0 years  D) 9.7 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(4) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.38629 ÷ 0.113329 = **12.23 years**.\nA) 6 years doubles it (Rule of 72). C) 18 years would take it to about ₹76.9 lakh. D) 9.7 years triples it. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling takes 6 years, and quadrupling is two doublings ≈ 12 years. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client has ₹80,00,000 at retirement, wants ₹60,000 a month, and the corpus earns 7% p.a. How long will the money last?\nA) About 12 years  B) About 21.5 years  C) Indefinitely  D) About 30 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.07 ÷ 12 = **0.0058333**. **First check sustainability:** monthly interest on ₹80,00,000 = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**, which is *less* than the ₹60,000 withdrawal — so the corpus **will** deplete.\n**Then solve for NPER:** n = −ln(1 − (PV × r ÷ PMT)) ÷ ln(1 + r) = −ln(1 − (80,00,000 × 0.0058333 ÷ 60,000)) ÷ ln(1.0058333) = −ln(0.22222) ÷ 0.0058164 = 1.50408 ÷ 0.0058164 = **258.6 months ≈ 21.5 years**.\nA) 12 years understates it. C) It would last indefinitely only if the withdrawal were at or below ₹46,667. D) 30 years overstates it. Excel: `=NPER(7%/12,60000,-8000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Continuing Q81: what monthly withdrawal could the client sustain indefinitely?\nA) ₹46,667  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **perpetuity**: withdraw only the interest earned. Monthly interest = 80,00,000 × (0.07 ÷ 12) = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**.\nB) ₹60,000 exceeds the interest earned and depletes the corpus (as Q81 showed). C) and D) deplete it far faster. **Note this ignores inflation — a truly sustainable *real* withdrawal would be lower still, which is why the 3–4% rule exists.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** ₹5,00,000 is invested at 10% for 3 years compounded annually, then reinvested at 8% for 4 more years. What is the final value?\nA) ₹9,05,300  B) ₹6,65,500  C) ₹8,50,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Phase 1:** 5,00,000 × (1.10)³ = 5,00,000 × **1.331** = **₹6,65,500**. **Phase 2:** 6,65,500 × (1.08)⁴ = 6,65,500 × **1.36049** = **₹9,05,406**.\nB) ₹6,65,500 stops after phase 1. C) ₹8,50,000 understates phase 2. D) ₹10,00,000 overstates it. **Method: chain the phases — never average the two rates.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** In Q83, what single CAGR over the 7 years is equivalent?\nA) 9.00%  B) 8.86%  C) 10.00%  D) 8.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (9,05,406 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/7) − 1 = (1.810812)^(1/7) − 1. ln(1.810812) = 0.59380; ÷ 7 = 0.084829; e^0.084829 = **1.08853**. CAGR = **8.85%**.\nA) 9% is the simple average weighted wrongly. C) and D) are the two individual phase rates. **Note the CAGR (8.85%) sits between 8% and 10%, closer to 8% because the 8% period was longer — a good sanity check.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client wants ₹1,00,000 a month in today's terms at retirement in 20 years. Inflation is 6%. What monthly income will she need in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,20,700  C) ₹1,60,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate: 1,00,000 × (1.06)²⁰ = 1,00,000 × **3.20714** = **₹3,20,714**.\nA) ₹2,00,000 assumes only a doubling — at 6% doubling takes 12 years, so 20 years must give more. C) ₹1,60,000 badly understates it. D) ₹5,00,000 overstates it. **The lesson: retirement income targets must always be stated in future rupees.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: what corpus is needed at retirement to fund ₹3,20,714 a month for 25 years, if the corpus earns 8%?\nA) ₹4,15,50,000  B) ₹9,62,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of an ordinary annuity: r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**, n = 300. From Q69 the factor is **129.56**. Corpus = 3,20,714 × 129.56 = **₹4,15,51,700**.\nB) ₹9.62 crore is the undiscounted total (3,20,714 × 300). C) and D) are far too low. **Combining Q85 and Q86 gives the complete retirement calculation: inflate the income, then discount it back as an annuity.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An investor is offered 8% compounded monthly or 8.2% compounded annually. Which is better and by how much in EAR terms?\nA) 8% monthly, by about 0.10pp  B) 8.2% annual, by about 0.10pp  C) They are identical  D) 8% monthly, by about 1pp\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EAR of 8% monthly = (1 + 0.08/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0066667)¹² − 1 = **8.30%**. The 8.2% annual option has an EAR of exactly **8.20%**. So 8% monthly wins by **0.10 percentage points**.\nB) Reverses the winner. C) They differ by 0.10pp. D) Overstates the gap tenfold. **Never compare headline rates — always convert to EAR.**\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client saves ₹20,000 a month for 5 years, then ₹40,000 a month for the next 10 years, all at 11% p.a. What is the total corpus after 15 years?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹1,34,05,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹85,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Split into two phases and value each.\n**Phase 1 (₹20,000 × 60 months):** (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**; factor = (1.72804 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **79.42**; value at year 5 = 20,000 × 79.42 = **₹15,88,400**. This then grows untouched for 10 more years: × (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = × **2.98529** = **₹47,42,000**.\n**Phase 2 (₹40,000 × 120 months):** factor = (2.98529 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **216.58**; value = 40,000 × 216.58 = **₹86,63,200**.\n**Total = 47,42,000 + 86,63,200 = ₹1,34,05,200.**\nA) ₹1,20,00,000 understates it. C) ₹60,00,000 is the total contributed (₹12L + ₹48L). D) ₹85,00,000 omits phase 1's carry-forward growth. **Method: value phase 1 at its end date, carry it forward, then add phase 2 — never annuitise a changing payment in one step.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A ₹30,00,000 loan at 10% for 15 years. If the client prepays ₹5,00,000 at the end of year 5, what happens?\nA) The EMI must rise  B) Either the EMI falls or the tenure shortens — the borrower usually chooses  C) Nothing changes  D) The interest rate falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A prepayment reduces the outstanding principal. The lender then offers either a **lower EMI** for the same remaining tenure, or the **same EMI with a shorter tenure**.\nA) A prepayment never forces a higher EMI. C) Reducing principal always reduces total interest. D) The contractual rate is unaffected. **Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure saves far more interest — the key Chapter 4 insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investment grows from ₹6,00,000 to ₹7,50,000 in 18 months. What is the annualised return?\nA) 25.00%  B) 16.05%  C) 16.67%  D) 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding-period return is 7,50,000 ÷ 6,00,000 = **1.25** over **1.5 years**. Annualised = 1.25^(1/1.5) − 1 = 1.25^0.6667 − 1. ln(1.25) = 0.22314; × 0.6667 = 0.14876; e^0.14876 = **1.16040**. Annualised = **16.04%**.\nA) 25% is the total (un-annualised) return. C) 16.67% divides 25% by 1.5 — a simple-interest shortcut that overstates it. D) 12.5% halves the total return. **Annualising requires compounding, not division.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which sequence of ₹1,00,000 invested annually for 3 years at 10% correctly shows the year-3 value (ordinary annuity)?\nA) 1,00,000×(1.10)³ + 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10)  B) 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10) + 1,00,000  C) 1,00,000×3×1.10  D) 1,00,000×(1.10)³\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In an **ordinary** annuity the last payment arrives *at* the end of year 3 and earns nothing. The year-1 payment compounds for 2 years, year-2's for 1 year, year-3's for 0. Total = 1,21,000 + 1,10,000 + 1,00,000 = **₹3,31,000**.\nA) Describes an **annuity due** (each payment gets one extra year). C) Ignores compounding. D) Compounds only one payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client's SIP of ₹25,000 a month for 12 years returns 10% p.a. She then stops and lets it grow for 8 more years at 8%. Final value?\nA) ₹1,25,00,000  B) ₹1,28,09,000  C) ₹69,00,000  D) ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two phases.\n**Accumulation:** r = 0.008333, n = 144. (1.008333)¹⁴⁴ = **3.30692**; factor = (3.30692 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **276.83**; corpus = 25,000 × 276.83 = **₹69,20,750**.\n**Growth (no further contribution):** 69,20,750 × (1.08)⁸ = 69,20,750 × **1.85093** = **₹1,28,09,300**.\nA) ₹1,25,00,000 is close but understates it. C) ₹69,00,000 stops after the accumulation phase. D) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed (25,000 × 144). **Note the corpus nearly doubles in 8 years with no further contribution — compounding on an accumulated base.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Inflation is 6% and a client's salary rises 8% a year. What is the real growth in purchasing power?\nA) 2.00%  B) 1.89%  C) 14.00%  D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real growth = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = **1.89%**.\nA) 2% is the crude subtraction, which overstates it. C) 14% adds them. D) 0.75% understates it. **The same ratio formula that gives real *returns* gives real *income growth*.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client can invest ₹10,00,000 today at 9% or receive ₹1,30,000 a year for 12 years. Which is better?\nA) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹9,30,000  B) The annuity, since its PV is about ₹9,30,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹15,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of the annuity = 1,30,000 × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09] = 1,30,000 × **7.1607** = **₹9,30,891**, which is *less* than ₹10,00,000. So investing the lump sum is better.\nB) Values it correctly but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹69,000. D) ₹15,60,000 is the undiscounted total (1,30,000 × 12), which ignores the time value of money.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A ₹12,00,000 car loan at 11% for 5 years. What is the EMI and total interest?\nA) EMI ₹26,092; interest ₹3,65,520  B) EMI ₹20,000; interest ₹1,00,000  C) EMI ₹30,000; interest ₹6,00,000  D) EMI ₹22,000; interest ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 60. (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**. EMI = 12,00,000 × [0.0091667 × 1.72804] ÷ [1.72804 − 1] = 12,00,000 × 0.01583999 ÷ 0.72804 = **₹26,102**. Total repaid = 26,102 × 60 = ₹15,66,120; interest = **₹3,66,120**.\nB) ₹20,000 is roughly the principal ÷ 60, ignoring interest. C) Overstates both. D) Understates both. Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,60,-1200000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An investor's ₹20,00,000 portfolio must double in real terms in 12 years. Inflation is 6%. What nominal return is required?\nA) 6.0%  B) 12.25%  C) 18.0%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Doubling in **real** terms in 12 years needs a real return of 2^(1/12) − 1 = **5.946%**. The required **nominal** return = (1 + real) × (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.05946 × 1.06 − 1 = 1.12303 − 1 = **12.30%**.\nA) 6% is inflation alone, giving zero real growth. C) 18% overstates it. D) 8% gives only about 1.9% real. **The formula runs both ways: nominal = (1 + real)(1 + inflation) − 1.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client invests ₹50,000 at the end of each quarter for 8 years at 10% p.a. compounded quarterly. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹16,00,000  B) ₹24,03,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Quarterly rate = 0.10 ÷ 4 = **0.025**; n = 8 × 4 = **32**. (1.025)³² = **2.20376**. Factor = (2.20376 − 1) ÷ 0.025 = **48.15**. FV = 50,000 × 48.15 = **₹24,07,500 ≈ ₹24,03,000**.\nA) ₹16,00,000 is the total contributed (50,000 × 32). C) and D) do not follow. **Note the period conversion: quarterly means divide the rate by 4 and multiply the years by 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A retiree needs an income stream rising 5% a year to keep pace with inflation, starting at ₹6,00,000 next year, discounted at 9%, for 20 years. Which formula applies?\nA) PMT ÷ r  B) The growing annuity formula: [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]  C) PMT × (1+r)  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **finite** stream that **grows** at a constant rate needs the growing-annuity formula. Here PV = [6,00,000 ÷ 0.04] × [1 − (1.05/1.09)²⁰] = 1,50,00,000 × [1 − 0.47615] = 1,50,00,000 × 0.52385 = **₹78,57,750**.\nA) Is a level perpetuity (infinite, no growth). C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Ignores discounting and growth entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client is told \"your investment grew 100% in 6 years, that's 16.7% a year.\" This is:\nA) Correct  B) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 12.25%  C) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 20%  D) Correct only if compounding is annual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividing the total gain by the number of years is a **simple-interest** error. The correct CAGR = 2^(1/6) − 1 = **12.25%**.\nA) 16.7% (100 ÷ 6) ignores compounding. C) 20% overstates it. D) The error exists regardless of frequency. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling in 6 years implies about 72 ÷ 6 = 12% — matching 12.25%. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which combination of errors would most inflate a client's projected retirement corpus?\nA) Using an annuity due instead of ordinary, and inflating the goal  B) Ignoring inflation on the goal, using the arithmetic mean return instead of CAGR, and ignoring tax  C) Using CAGR and post-tax returns  D) Using monthly compounding correctly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three errors push the same way: **ignoring inflation** understates what is needed, the **arithmetic mean** overstates the return achieved, and **ignoring tax** overstates what the client keeps. Together they can overstate readiness by a wide margin.\nA) Inflating the goal is *correct* practice, and the annuity-due difference is small. C) Both are the correct, conservative choices. D) Correct method, no distortion. **The professional habit: inflate the goal, use geometric (compound) returns, and project post-tax.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Use a spreadsheet — this is exactly how the 2-mark caselets are set.\n\n### Case: Planning Kavita's Retirement\n\nKavita is **34 years old** and plans to retire at **60**. Her current annual household expenses are **₹9,00,000**. She expects to live until **85**.\n\nShe has already accumulated **₹22,00,000** in an EPF and equity mutual fund portfolio.\n\n**Assumptions:**\n- Inflation before *and* after retirement: **6% p.a.**\n- Return on the portfolio before retirement: **11% p.a.** (monthly compounding for SIPs)\n- Return on the corpus after retirement: **8% p.a.**\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** How many years will Kavita spend in accumulation, and how many in retirement?\nA) 26 years accumulating, 25 years retired  B) 34 years accumulating, 26 years retired  C) 26 years accumulating, 51 years retired  D) 25 years accumulating, 25 years retired\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Accumulation** = retirement age − current age = 60 − 34 = **26 years**. **Retirement** = life expectancy − retirement age = 85 − 60 = **25 years**.\nB) 34 is her current age, not a period. C) 51 is her total remaining life expectancy (85 − 34), which spans *both* phases. D) Miscounts the accumulation phase. *(Always establish these two horizons first — every later step depends on them.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What will Kavita's annual expenses be in her first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹40,93,000  C) ₹27,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate for **26 years** at 6%: 9,00,000 × (1.06)²⁶. (1.06)²⁶ = **4.54938**. = **₹40,94,442 ≈ ₹40,93,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes a mere doubling — but at 6% money doubles in 12 years, so 26 years gives more than 4×. C) ₹27,00,000 assumes 3×. D) ₹9,00,000 ignores inflation entirely. **Rule of 72 check: 72 ÷ 6 = 12 years per doubling; 26 years ≈ 2.17 doublings ≈ 4.5×. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Ignoring post-retirement inflation for simplicity, what corpus does Kavita need at 60 to fund ₹40,94,442 a year for 25 years at 8%?\nA) ₹10,23,61,050  B) ₹4,37,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹40,94,442\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of a 25-year ordinary annuity at 8%: factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻²⁵) ÷ 0.08. (1.08)²⁵ = **6.84848**, so the inverse is **0.146018**. Factor = (1 − 0.146018) ÷ 0.08 = 0.853982 ÷ 0.08 = **10.6748**. Corpus = 40,94,442 × 10.6748 = **₹4,37,07,000**.\nA) ₹10.24 crore is the **undiscounted** total (40,94,442 × 25) — it ignores that the corpus keeps earning 8% throughout retirement. C) ₹2 crore is far too low. D) Is one year's expense. **Note the corpus needed (₹4.37 cr) is well under half the total withdrawn (₹10.24 cr) — the balance does the rest.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Kavita's existing ₹22,00,000 grows at 11% for 26 years. What will it be worth at 60?\nA) ₹3,23,70,000  B) ₹1,50,00,000  C) ₹57,20,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 22,00,000 × (1.11)²⁶. (1.11)²⁶ = **14.7138**. FV = **₹3,23,70,360**.\nB) ₹1.5 crore understates it badly. C) ₹57,20,000 applies a mere 2.6× (roughly simple interest). D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Rule of 72 check: at 11%, money doubles roughly every 6.5 years; 26 years ≈ 4 doublings ≈ 16×. ✓ The head start does most of the heavy lifting.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given the corpus needed from QC3 and the existing portfolio's growth from QC4, what monthly SIP at 11% would close the remaining gap?\nA) About ₹6,900  B) About ₹25,000  C) No SIP is needed — the existing corpus is nearly sufficient  D) About ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Gap** = required corpus − grown existing corpus = ₹4,37,07,000 − ₹3,23,70,000 = **₹1,13,37,000**.\n**SIP:** r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 26 × 12 = **312**. (1.0091667)³¹² = **16.9846**. Annuity factor = (16.9846 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 15.9846 ÷ 0.0091667 = **1743.8**. PMT = 1,13,37,000 ÷ 1743.8 = **₹6,502 a month**.\n\nSo roughly **₹6,500–6,900 a month** closes the gap — option **A)**.\nB) and D) massively overstate the requirement. C) Is tempting but wrong: the existing corpus reaches ₹3.24 crore against a ₹4.37 crore requirement, a real shortfall of over ₹1.1 crore.\n\n> ⚠️ **Important caveat the exam may probe:** this calculation **ignored post-retirement inflation**. Kavita's expenses will keep rising after 60, so the true corpus requirement is materially higher and the real SIP would be several times ₹6,500. Using the **growing annuity** formula (g = 6%, r = 8%) for the retirement phase is the technically correct approach.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "03", "title": "Cash Flow Management and Budgeting", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **The ratios in section 3.9 are guaranteed exam marks.** There are ten of them, they are simple to compute, and caselets love them. Learn each formula *and* what a \"good\" value looks like.\n\n---\n\n## 3.1 Why Cash Flow Matters\n\n**Cash flow is the engine of a financial plan.** Investments, insurance premiums and loan EMIs are all funded from one place: the **surplus** between what comes in and what goes out.\n\n$$\\textbf{Surplus (Savings)} = \\textbf{Total Inflows} - \\textbf{Total Outflows}$$\n\n- **No surplus → no plan.** You cannot allocate money you never generated.\n- A client with a ₹5 lakh monthly income and ₹5 lakh of expenses is in a worse position than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000.\n- Cash flow problems are usually **behavioural**, not arithmetic — which is why budgeting is a planning discipline, not an accounting chore.\n\n### The two statements (don't confuse them)\n\n| | **Cash Flow Statement** | **Personal Balance Sheet** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Shows** | Inflows and outflows | Assets and liabilities |\n| **Covers** | A **period** (month/year) | A **point in time** |\n| **Key output** | **Surplus / deficit** | **Net worth** |\n| **Type** | A **flow** | A **stock** |\n\n> 🧠 The two link together: this period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, raising **net worth**.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Cash Inflows and Outflows\n\n### Inflows\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Regular / active** | Salary, professional fees, business income |\n| **Portfolio / passive** | Interest, dividends, rent |\n| **Irregular** | Bonus, incentives, gifts, maturity proceeds, capital gains |\n\n### Outflows\n\n| Type | Examples | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fixed / committed** | EMIs, rent, insurance premiums, school fees | Contractual — hard to cut quickly |\n| **Variable / discretionary** | Groceries, fuel, dining, travel, entertainment | Controllable |\n| **Periodic / lumpy** | Annual insurance, festivals, vacations, car servicing | Predictable but infrequent — the ones people forget |\n| **Savings / investment** | SIPs, PPF, RD | Should be treated as a *committed* outflow |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most common budgeting failure** is ignoring **periodic outflows**. A budget that balances every month collapses in the month the annual insurance premium falls due. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that aside monthly.\n\n### 3.3.1 Cash management\nEnsuring enough liquidity to meet obligations **as they fall due**, without holding so much idle cash that returns are sacrificed. Two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing or asset sales) and too much (opportunity cost).\n\n### 3.3.2 Income and Expenditure Statement\nThe formal record: list all inflows, list all outflows, and derive the surplus. Best prepared **monthly** with an **annualised** view alongside so lumpy items are visible.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 & 3.7 Preparing a Household Budget\n\nA **budget** is a *forward-looking* plan for income and spending; the income–expenditure statement is the *backward-looking* record of what actually happened.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Record** actual income and spending for 2–3 months (most people badly underestimate discretionary spending).\n2. **Categorise** into fixed, variable, periodic and savings.\n3. **Set targets** for each category.\n4. **Build in savings first** — see \"pay yourself first\" below.\n5. **Monitor** actual vs budget each month.\n6. **Revise** as circumstances change.\n\n### 🔑 \"Pay yourself first\"\n\nThe critical principle. Two orderings:\n\n| Approach | Order | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| ❌ Common | Income → Expenses → *whatever is left* is saved | Savings become the residual — usually near zero |\n| ✅ Correct | Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest | Savings are protected; spending adapts |\n\nAutomate it: schedule SIPs and recurring deposits for the **day after salary credit**.\n\n### The role of forecasting (3.4)\n\nBudgeting looks ahead — anticipating salary increments, a new EMI starting, school fees rising, a child's arrival, a planned car purchase. Forecasting lets the client see a shortfall **before** it becomes a crisis.\n\n### 3.5 Monitoring and provision for savings\n\nMonitoring answers: *where did the plan leak?* Variance analysis (budget vs actual, per category) locates it. Persistent overspending in one category means either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n---\n\n## 3.8 Contingency Planning\n\nThe **contingency (emergency) fund** protects the plan from income shocks and unplanned expenses.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Job loss, medical emergency, urgent repairs, income gap |\n| **Size** | **3–6 months of expenses** (go higher for irregular income, single-earner households, or many dependants) |\n| **Where** | Savings account, sweep-in FD, **liquid/overnight mutual funds** |\n| **Requirements** | **Immediate availability + certainty of value.** Return is secondary |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the emergency fund is sized against **expenses**, not income — because expenses are what must be paid. And it must **never** be parked in equity: the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Beyond cash:** adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** are part of contingency planning, because they stop a single event from consuming the entire fund.\n\n---\n\n## 3.6 Personal Balance Sheet and Net Worth\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n**Assets** (at current market value):\n- **Liquid** — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs\n- **Financial (invested)** — deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, PPF/EPF, insurance cash values\n- **Physical/real** — property, land, gold\n- **Personal-use** — car, appliances, jewellery for use\n\n**Liabilities** (at outstanding principal):\n- **Short-term** — credit cards, overdrafts, dues within a year\n- **Long-term** — home, education, vehicle loans\n\n> ⚠️ Record loans at the **outstanding principal**, never the original amount and never the total of remaining EMIs (that would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n---\n\n## 3.9 ⭐ Evaluating the Financial Position — The Ten Ratios\n\n**Learn every formula and its healthy range. These are the most reliably examinable items in Module 1.**\n\n### 1. Savings Ratio\n$$\\text{Savings Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Savings (Surplus)}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: 20%+ (higher for late starters).** Measures the plan's fuel supply.\n\n### 2. Expenses Ratio\n$$\\text{Expenses Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Expenses}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**The complement:** Savings Ratio + Expenses Ratio = **100%**. A great self-check in the exam.\n\n### 3. Total Assets\nThe sum of all assets at current market value. Not a ratio, but the base for several below.\n\n### 4. Total Liabilities\nThe sum of all outstanding debts at principal value.\n\n### 5. Leverage Ratio (Debt-to-Assets)\n$$\\text{Leverage Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Liabilities}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Lower is better.** Above ~0.5 means more than half the asset base is financed by debt.\n\n### 6. Net Worth\n$$\\text{Net Worth} = \\text{Total Assets} - \\text{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### 7. Solvency Ratio\n$$\\text{Solvency Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Net Worth}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better.** Shows the share of assets genuinely owned.\n\n> 🔑 **Solvency Ratio + Leverage Ratio = 1.** Always. Use it to check your arithmetic.\n\n### 8. Liquid Assets\nAssets convertible to cash quickly with no material loss — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs. **Equity and PPF do not qualify** (volatile / locked in).\n\n### 9. Liquidity Ratio\n$$\\text{Liquidity Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Liquid Assets}}{\\text{Monthly Expenses}}$$\n**Answer is in *months*. Healthy: 3–6 months.**\n\n### 10. Financial Assets Ratio\n$$\\text{Financial Assets Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Financial Assets}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better for goal funding.** A low ratio signals the \"asset-rich, cash-poor\" trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that cannot be partly sold or spent.\n\n### 11. Debt-to-Income Ratio\n$$\\text{Debt-to-Income Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total EMIs / Debt Servicing}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: below ~35–40%.** Lenders use this to decide eligibility. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n---\n\n### 📋 Ratio Cheat Sheet\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Healthy | Reads as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Savings | Savings ÷ Income | ≥ 20% | % |\n| Expenses | Expenses ÷ Income | ≤ 80% | % |\n| Leverage | Liabilities ÷ Assets | < 0.5 | decimal |\n| Solvency | Net worth ÷ Assets | > 0.5 | decimal |\n| Liquidity | Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses | 3–6 | **months** |\n| Financial assets | Financial assets ÷ Total assets | higher | decimal/% |\n| Debt-to-income | EMIs ÷ Income | < 35–40% | % |\n\n**Three self-checks:**\n1. Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%**\n2. Solvency ratio + leverage ratio = **1**\n3. Liquidity ratio is in **months** — if your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Worked Example\n\n**Ravi's data:** Monthly income ₹1,80,000. Monthly expenses ₹1,26,000 (including EMIs of ₹54,000).\n**Assets:** savings ₹2,40,000; liquid fund ₹1,60,000; mutual funds ₹18,00,000; EPF ₹14,00,000; house ₹80,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹5,00,000.\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹44,00,000; car loan ₹4,00,000.\n\n| Step | Working | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Savings | 1,80,000 − 1,26,000 | **₹54,000** |\n| Savings ratio | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n| Expenses ratio | 1,26,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **70%** (30 + 70 = 100 ✓) |\n| Total assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 + 80 + 9 + 5 (₹ lakh) | **₹1,30,00,000** |\n| Total liabilities | 44 + 4 (₹ lakh) | **₹48,00,000** |\n| Net worth | 1,30,00,000 − 48,00,000 | **₹82,00,000** |\n| Solvency ratio | 82,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.63** ✅ |\n| Leverage ratio | 48,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.37** (0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓) |\n| Liquid assets | 2,40,000 + 1,60,000 | **₹4,00,000** |\n| Liquidity ratio | 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 | **3.17 months** ⚠️ thin |\n| Financial assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 (₹ lakh) | **₹36,00,000** |\n| Financial assets ratio | 36,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **27.7%** |\n| Debt-to-income | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n\n**Verdict:** Ravi saves well (30%) and his debt burden is manageable (30%), but his **liquidity is thin at 3.17 months** and **68% of his assets are physical** (house + car + gold). Priorities: raise the emergency fund toward 6 months (≈₹7.6 lakh) and direct future surplus into financial assets.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Surplus | Inflows − Outflows |\n| Cash flow statement vs balance sheet | Flow over a **period** vs stock at a **point** |\n| The link between them | This period's surplus adds to next period's assets → net worth rises |\n| Four outflow types | Fixed, variable, **periodic**, savings |\n| Most-forgotten outflow | **Periodic** (annual premiums, festivals) — divide by 12 and set aside |\n| Pay yourself first | Save **before** spending, automated on salary day |\n| Emergency fund size | **3–6 months of expenses** (not income) |\n| Emergency fund location | Savings / sweep-in FD / liquid funds — **never equity** |\n| Savings + expenses ratios | Sum to **100%** |\n| Solvency + leverage ratios | Sum to **1** |\n| Liquidity ratio units | **Months** |\n| Liquid assets exclude | Equity, PPF, property, personal-use assets |\n| Debt-to-income ceiling | ~**35–40%** |\n| Low financial assets ratio signals | **Asset-rich, cash-poor** — wealth locked in property |\n\n> **Exam tip:** in every caselet, compute **total assets and total liabilities first**, then derive every ratio from those two numbers. The distractors are almost always the two subtotals themselves, or the complement ratio.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The ten ratios are near-certain exam marks — drill them until instant.**\n\n**Q1.** What is the formula for surplus?\n> **A:** **Surplus (Savings) = Total Inflows − Total Outflows.** It is the engine of the whole plan — no surplus, no plan.\n\n**Q2.** What is the difference between the cash flow statement and the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** Cash flow statement = **flows over a period**, output is the **surplus**. Balance sheet = **stock at a point in time**, output is **net worth**.\n\n**Q3.** How do the two statements connect?\n> **A:** This period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, which raises **net worth**.\n\n**Q4.** Name the four types of cash outflow.\n> **A:** **Fixed/committed** (EMIs, rent, premiums), **variable/discretionary** (groceries, dining), **periodic/lumpy** (annual premiums, festivals, vacations), and **savings/investment**.\n\n**Q5.** What is the most common budgeting failure?\n> **A:** Ignoring **periodic (lumpy) outflows**. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that amount aside every month.\n\n**Q6.** Explain \"pay yourself first.\"\n> **A:** Save **before** you spend: Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest. The wrong order treats savings as the residual, which ends up near zero.\n\n**Q7.** What is a budget, versus an income and expenditure statement?\n> **A:** A **budget is forward-looking** (a plan); the **income and expenditure statement is backward-looking** (the record of what actually happened).\n\n**Q8.** How large should a contingency fund be, and measured against what?\n> **A:** **3–6 months of EXPENSES** — not income, because expenses are what must be paid. Go higher for irregular income, single-earner households or many dependants.\n\n**Q9.** Where should an emergency fund be held, and why not equity?\n> **A:** Savings account, sweep-in FD or **liquid/overnight funds**. Never equity — the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Q10.** State the savings ratio formula and a healthy level.\n> **A:** **Savings ÷ Gross Income**; healthy is **20% or more**.\n\n**Q11.** State the expenses ratio, and its relationship to the savings ratio.\n> **A:** **Expenses ÷ Gross Income.** Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%** — a useful arithmetic self-check.\n\n**Q12.** State the leverage ratio.\n> **A:** **Total Liabilities ÷ Total Assets.** Lower is better; above ~0.5 means over half the asset base is debt-financed.\n\n**Q13.** State the solvency ratio and its relationship to leverage.\n> **A:** **Net Worth ÷ Total Assets.** **Solvency + Leverage = 1**, always.\n\n**Q14.** State the liquidity ratio — and what unit is the answer in?\n> **A:** **Liquid Assets ÷ Monthly Expenses.** The answer is in **months**; healthy is **3–6**.\n\n**Q15.** Which assets count as \"liquid\", and which commonly-mistaken ones do not?\n> **A:** Count cash, savings balance, liquid/overnight funds, sweep-in FDs. **Do NOT count** equity (volatile), PPF/EPF (locked), property or personal-use assets.\n\n**Q16.** State the financial assets ratio and what a low value signals.\n> **A:** **Financial Assets ÷ Total Assets.** A low value signals the **asset-rich, cash-poor** trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that can't be partly sold.\n\n**Q17.** State the debt-to-income ratio and its ceiling.\n> **A:** **Total EMIs ÷ Gross Income**; keep it below about **35–40%**. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n**Q18.** Name the three arithmetic self-checks for the ratio questions.\n> **A:** 1) Savings % + expenses % = **100**. 2) Solvency + leverage = **1**. 3) Liquidity ratio is in **months** — a percentage-looking answer means you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n**Q19.** At what value are liabilities recorded on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** — never the original loan amount, never the total of remaining EMIs (which would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n**Q20.** At what value are assets recorded?\n> **A:** **Current market value**, so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\n\n**Q21.** Name the three categories of cash inflow.\n> **A:** **Regular/active** (salary, fees), **portfolio/passive** (interest, dividends, rent), and **irregular** (bonus, gifts, maturity proceeds).\n\n**Q22.** What are the two failure modes of cash management?\n> **A:** **Too little** liquidity (forced borrowing or asset sales) and **too much** idle cash (opportunity cost of foregone returns).\n\n**Q23.** What are the six steps in preparing a budget?\n> **A:** **Record** actuals → **categorise** → **set targets** → **build in savings first** → **monitor** actual vs budget → **revise**.\n\n**Q24.** What does variance analysis tell you?\n> **A:** *Where the plan leaked.* Comparing budget vs actual by category locates persistent overspending — meaning either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n**Q25.** Is a client earning ₹5 lakh a month and spending ₹5 lakh better off than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000?\n> **A:** **No.** The first has a **zero surplus** and therefore no capacity to fund any goal; the second saves ₹30,000 a month. Income level is not financial health — surplus is.\n\n**Q26.** Besides cash, what else belongs to contingency planning?\n> **A:** Adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** — they stop a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\n\n**Q27.** Why should savings be treated as a committed outflow rather than a residual?\n> **A:** Because discretionary spending expands to fill available income. Committing (and automating) savings on salary day protects the plan's fuel supply.\n\n**Q28.** A client has ₹4,00,000 liquid and spends ₹1,26,000 a month. What is the liquidity ratio and the verdict?\n> **A:** 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 = **3.17 months** — just inside the 3–6 month norm, but **thin**; building toward 6 months is advisable.\n\n**Q29.** What is the very first thing to compute in any ratio caselet?\n> **A:** **Total assets and total liabilities.** Almost every other ratio derives from those two figures — and the distractors are usually the subtotals themselves.\n\n**Q30.** Total assets ₹1,30,00,000; total liabilities ₹48,00,000. Give net worth, solvency and leverage.\n> **A:** Net worth = **₹82,00,000**. Solvency = 82 ÷ 130 = **0.63**. Leverage = 48 ÷ 130 = **0.37**. Check: 0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓\n", "questions": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The ten ratios dominate this chapter and the exam.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Surplus (savings) is calculated as:\nA) Total assets − total liabilities  B) Total inflows − total outflows  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Income × savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **cash flow** concept: what comes in minus what goes out over a period.\nA) Is net worth, a balance sheet concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Is circular — the savings rate is derived *from* the surplus.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The savings ratio is:\nA) Savings ÷ Total assets  B) Savings ÷ Gross income  C) Savings ÷ Expenses  D) Income ÷ Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Savings ÷ Gross Income**, expressed as a percentage.\nA) Divides a flow by a stock — meaningless. C) Would exceed 100% for good savers and is not the standard definition. D) Is the reciprocal.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The liquidity ratio is expressed in:\nA) Percentage  B) Rupees  C) Months  D) A decimal between 0 and 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Liquid assets ÷ **monthly** expenses answers \"how many months could I survive?\" — so the unit is **months**.\nA), B) and D) all indicate a wrong denominator. If your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by income or assets instead of monthly expenses.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The solvency ratio is:\nA) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  B) Net worth ÷ Total assets  C) Net worth ÷ Total liabilities  D) Total assets ÷ Net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Net worth ÷ Total assets** — the share of the asset base genuinely owned.\nA) Is the **leverage** ratio. C) and D) are not standard ratios in this syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Solvency ratio + leverage ratio equals:\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since net worth + liabilities = assets, dividing through by assets gives solvency + leverage = **1**. A reliable arithmetic self-check.\nA), B) and D) do not follow from the identity.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A contingency fund should typically cover:\nA) 3–6 months of income  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 1 month of expenses  D) 24 months of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sized against **expenses**, because expenses are what must actually be paid during an income gap.\nA) Using income overstates the requirement for high savers. C) One month is too thin for a job loss. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of these qualifies as a liquid asset?\nA) Public Provident Fund balance  B) Equity mutual fund units  C) Sweep-in fixed deposit  D) Residential property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A sweep-in FD converts to cash instantly with no loss of value.\nA) PPF is locked in for a long term. B) Equity is volatile — the value is not certain when needed. D) Property takes months to sell.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** \"Pay yourself first\" means:\nA) Pay your EMIs before anything else  B) Save before spending, ideally automated  C) Withdraw your salary in cash  D) Repay the smallest loan first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Route savings out **before** discretionary spending, automated on or just after salary day.\nA) EMIs are contractual outflows, not savings. C) Has nothing to do with the principle. D) Describes the debt \"snowball\" method (Chapter 4).\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a periodic (lumpy) outflow?\nA) Monthly rent  B) The annual motor insurance premium  C) The monthly SIP  D) Daily commuting cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic outflows are **predictable but infrequent** — annual premiums, festivals, vacations, servicing.\nA), C) and D) all recur monthly or daily and are easy to budget.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The leverage ratio is:\nA) Net worth ÷ Total assets  B) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  C) EMIs ÷ Income  D) Liquid assets ÷ Expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total liabilities ÷ Total assets**; lower is better.\nA) Is solvency. C) Is debt-to-income. D) Is liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Savings ratio plus expenses ratio equals:\nA) 50%  B) 100%  C) 1 month  D) It varies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since savings + expenses = income, dividing by income gives **100%**.\nA) and C) do not follow. D) The identity always holds when both are measured against the same gross income.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The debt-to-income ratio is generally considered healthy below:\nA) 10%  B) 35–40%  C) 60%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lenders and planners generally regard **under 35–40%** as manageable.\nA) 10% is unnecessarily strict and few borrowers would qualify for a home loan. C) and D) indicate debt stress.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A budget differs from an income and expenditure statement because a budget is:\nA) Backward-looking  B) Forward-looking  C) Prepared only by companies  D) The same thing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **budget plans ahead**; the income and expenditure statement **records what happened**.\nA) Describes the income and expenditure statement. C) Households prepare budgets too. D) They serve different purposes.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The financial assets ratio is:\nA) Financial assets ÷ Total assets  B) Financial assets ÷ Income  C) Total assets ÷ Financial assets  D) Financial assets ÷ Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It measures what share of wealth is in **financial** (as opposed to physical/personal-use) form.\nB), C) and D) are not standard definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which statement about the personal balance sheet is correct?\nA) It covers a period of time  B) It is a snapshot at a point in time  C) It shows the monthly surplus  D) It records only liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot**; the cash flow statement covers a period.\nA) and C) describe the cash flow statement. D) It records both sides.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Liabilities on the personal balance sheet are recorded at:\nA) The original loan sanctioned  B) The outstanding principal  C) The sum of all remaining EMIs  D) The market value of the asset financed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is currently owed.\nA) Ignores repayments made. C) Includes unaccrued future interest, which is not yet a liability. D) That is the asset side.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Which of these is a fixed (committed) outflow?\nA) Dining out  B) A home loan EMI  C) A weekend trip  D) Impulse shopping\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EMIs are contractual and cannot be cut at short notice.\nA), C) and D) are all discretionary/variable and are the first place to look when a budget needs fixing.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which best describes cash management?\nA) Holding as much cash as possible  B) Meeting obligations as they fall due without holding excessive idle cash  C) Investing everything in equity  D) Avoiding all bank accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance the two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing) and too much (opportunity cost).\nA) Excessive cash sacrifices returns. C) Equity cannot meet near-term obligations reliably. D) Impractical.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is a portfolio (passive) inflow?\nA) Monthly salary  B) Rental income from a let-out property  C) Professional consulting fees  D) A performance bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Portfolio inflows arise from **assets** — interest, dividends and rent.\nA) and C) are active/earned income. D) Is an irregular inflow tied to employment.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A client with zero surplus:\nA) Has an excellent financial position  B) Cannot fund any goal until cash flow is fixed  C) Should immediately buy equity  D) Has a high savings ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With no surplus there is nothing to allocate; **cash flow management must precede investment planning**.\nA) Zero surplus is the weakest possible position regardless of income. C) There is nothing to invest. D) The savings ratio is 0%.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Net worth is:\nA) Income − Expenses  B) Total assets − Total liabilities  C) Liquid assets − Short-term debt  D) Assets + Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The balance sheet identity.\nA) Is the surplus. C) Is a partial measure, not net worth. D) Adds instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of these should NOT be counted as a liquid asset?\nA) Balance in a savings account  B) An overnight mutual fund  C) EPF balance  D) Cash in hand\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **EPF is locked in** until specified events, so it cannot serve an emergency.\nA), B) and D) are all immediately available at certain value.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Monitoring a budget primarily involves:\nA) Comparing budgeted with actual figures by category  B) Increasing income  C) Cancelling all insurance  D) Buying more assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Variance analysis** — budget vs actual, per category — locates where the plan leaked.\nB), C) and D) are not monitoring activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which is the correct order under \"pay yourself first\"?\nA) Income → Expenses → Savings  B) Income → Savings → Expenses  C) Savings → Income → Expenses  D) Expenses → Income → Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings are routed out **immediately after income is received**, and spending comes from the remainder.\nA) Is the common (failing) order that makes savings a residual. C) and D) are not feasible sequences.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A household with irregular income should hold a contingency fund at:\nA) The lower end of the 3–6 month range  B) The upper end of the range or beyond  C) Zero, since income is variable  D) Exactly one month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Greater income uncertainty means a longer possible gap, so the buffer should be **larger**.\nA) Understates the need. C) and D) leave the household badly exposed.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The expenses ratio measures:\nA) Expenses ÷ Total assets  B) Expenses ÷ Gross income  C) Expenses ÷ Savings  D) Fixed ÷ Variable expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Expenses ÷ Gross income**, the complement of the savings ratio.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A let-out flat  B) The family car  C) A corporate bond  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Personal-use assets are held for use, typically depreciate and produce no income.\nA) Generates rent, so it is an investment asset. C) and D) are financial assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A savings ratio of 30% means:\nA) 30% of assets are savings  B) 30% of gross income is saved  C) 30 months of expenses are covered  D) Debt is 30% of income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The savings ratio measures the share of **income** saved.\nA) Would be an asset-composition measure. C) Is liquidity. D) Is debt-to-income.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The main reason to categorise expenses is to:\nA) Increase total spending  B) Identify which expenses are controllable  C) Reduce income  D) Satisfy tax authorities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Separating **fixed** from **variable** shows where cuts are actually possible.\nA) and C) are not objectives. D) Personal budgets are not filed with tax authorities.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which best describes the link between the two statements?\nA) They are unrelated  B) This period's surplus adds to assets, raising net worth  C) Net worth determines income  D) The balance sheet determines expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The surplus generated in a period flows onto the balance sheet as additional assets.\nA) They are tightly linked. C) and D) reverse the causation.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A leverage ratio of 0.65 indicates:\nA) 65% of assets are financed by debt  B) 65% of income goes to EMIs  C) 65 months of liquidity  D) A 65% savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets, so **65% of the asset base is debt-financed** — high, and above the ~0.5 comfort level.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Contingency planning includes which of the following besides a cash buffer?\nA) Buying more equity  B) Adequate health and life insurance  C) Taking a larger home loan  D) Cancelling the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance stops a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\nA) Equity is not a contingency asset. C) More debt increases fragility. D) Removes the buffer entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of these forecasting events should a budget anticipate?\nA) A school fee increase next April  B) Yesterday's grocery bill  C) Last year's bonus  D) The 2019 tax rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Forecasting is **forward-looking**: increments, new EMIs starting, fee rises, a child's arrival.\nB) and C) are historical records. D) Is obsolete data.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A low financial assets ratio typically signals:\nA) Excellent goal-funding capacity  B) Wealth locked in physical assets — \"asset-rich, cash-poor\"  C) High liquidity  D) Zero debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most wealth sits in a house or other physical assets that cannot be partly sold or spent.\nA) Reverses it. C) Physical assets are illiquid. D) The ratio says nothing about debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Assets on the personal balance sheet should be recorded at:\nA) Original cost  B) Current market value  C) Insured value  D) Zero until sold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Current market value** keeps the picture realistic.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Insured value reflects replacement, not market worth. D) Unsold assets are still assets.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Monthly income ₹1,50,000, monthly expenses ₹1,08,000. What are the savings and expenses ratios?\nA) 28% and 72%  B) 72% and 28%  C) 38.9% and 61.1%  D) 30% and 70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,08,000 = **₹42,000**. Savings ratio = 42,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **28%**. Expenses ratio = 1,08,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **72%**. Check: 28 + 72 = 100 ✓\nB) Reverses the two. C) 38.9% wrongly divides savings by expenses (42,000 ÷ 1,08,000). D) Does not match the figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Total assets ₹90,00,000; total liabilities ₹36,00,000. What are net worth, solvency and leverage?\nA) ₹54,00,000; 0.60; 0.40  B) ₹1,26,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  C) ₹54,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  D) ₹54,00,000; 1.50; 0.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 90 − 36 = **₹54,00,000**. Solvency = 54 ÷ 90 = **0.60**. Leverage = 36 ÷ 90 = **0.40**. Check: 0.60 + 0.40 = 1 ✓\nB) Adds instead of subtracting for net worth. C) Swaps solvency and leverage. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client holds cash ₹60,000, savings balance ₹2,40,000, liquid fund ₹3,00,000, equity funds ₹15,00,000 and PPF ₹9,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹1,50,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 2.0 months  C) 18.0 months  D) 1.6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquid assets = 60,000 + 2,40,000 + 3,00,000 = **₹6,00,000**. Ratio = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 2.0 counts only the savings balance. C) 18.0 wrongly includes equity and PPF — neither is liquid. D) 1.6 counts only cash plus part of the savings balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A household's monthly EMIs total ₹68,000 against a gross monthly income of ₹1,70,000. The debt-to-income ratio is:\nA) 25%  B) 40%  C) 250%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 68,000 ÷ 1,70,000 = **40%** — right at the upper limit of the healthy range, so no further borrowing capacity.\nA) 25% understates it. C) 250% is the reciprocal. D) 60% does not match.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A budget balances every month yet the client repeatedly runs a deficit in March and October. The most likely cause is:\nA) The savings ratio is too high  B) Periodic (lumpy) expenses were not provisioned monthly  C) Income is too high  D) The liquidity ratio is excessive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual premiums, school fees and festival spending cluster in particular months. **Divide each annual amount by 12 and set it aside monthly.**\nA) A high savings ratio is desirable and would not cause seasonal deficits. C) and D) are not causes of a deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client is in the stronger position?\nA) Income ₹6,00,000/month, expenses ₹6,00,000/month  B) Income ₹1,20,000/month, expenses ₹84,000/month  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — B saves **₹36,000 a month (30%)**; A saves **nothing (0%)**. Financial health is measured by **surplus**, not income level.\nA) A high income with zero surplus funds no goals. C) They are very different. D) The surplus makes them directly comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client's liquidity ratio is 1.2 months and she is the sole earner with two dependants. The adviser should:\nA) Recommend investing the buffer in mid-caps  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months before other investing  C) Take on a personal loan  D) Do nothing — 1.2 months is fine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1.2 months is far below the 3–6 month norm**, and a sole earner with dependants sits at the *upper* end of the requirement.\nA) Defeats the purpose of an emergency fund. C) Debt is not a substitute for savings. D) The household is one income shock from crisis.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Total assets ₹1,20,00,000, of which the self-occupied house is ₹92,00,000 and the car ₹8,00,000. What is the financial assets ratio?\nA) 83.3%  B) 16.7%  C) 76.7%  D) 23.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Physical + personal-use = 92,00,000 + 8,00,000 = ₹1,00,00,000. Financial assets = 1,20,00,000 − 1,00,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**. Ratio = 20 ÷ 120 = **16.7%**.\nA) 83.3% is the *physical* asset share. C) and D) do not follow. **A 16.7% financial assets ratio is the classic asset-rich, cash-poor signature.**\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which action most directly improves the savings ratio?\nA) Buying a larger house  B) Cutting discretionary spending and automating a SIP on salary day  C) Increasing the credit card limit  D) Revaluing assets upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reducing outflows and committing savings first both raise the surplus.\nA) Adds an EMI, lowering the surplus. C) Raises borrowing capacity, not savings. D) Changes the balance sheet, not the cash flow.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client's solvency ratio is 0.25. This means:\nA) Only 25% of the asset base is genuinely owned  B) 25% of income goes to EMIs  C) The client has 25 months of liquidity  D) The client saves 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets, so **75% of the asset base is financed by debt** (leverage = 0.75) — a highly leveraged, fragile position.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which pair of expenses should be attacked first when a client must cut spending?\nA) Home loan EMI and insurance premium  B) Dining out and subscription services  C) School fees and utilities  D) SIP and PPF contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Variable/discretionary** expenses are the controllable ones.\nA) EMIs and premiums are contractual — cutting insurance also destroys protection. C) Largely essential and committed. D) Cutting savings defeats the purpose of the exercise.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client's monthly surplus is ₹40,000 but the liquidity ratio is 0.8 months. What is the correct sequencing?\nA) Start an equity SIP with the entire surplus  B) Direct the surplus to the emergency fund until 3–6 months is reached, then invest  C) Prepay the home loan entirely  D) Buy a second property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **less than one month** of buffer, any shock forces debt or a distressed asset sale. Build the buffer first, then invest.\nA) Leaves the household exposed. C) Locks money into an illiquid asset while the buffer is empty. D) Worsens both liquidity and leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Gross monthly income ₹2,00,000; expenses ₹1,40,000 including ₹60,000 of EMIs. What are the savings ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 30% and 30%  B) 30% and 43%  C) 40% and 30%  D) 70% and 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,00,000 − 1,40,000 = ₹60,000; savings ratio = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) Miscomputes savings. D) 70% is the expenses ratio. **Both ratios are healthy here.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Why is the emergency fund measured against expenses rather than income?\nA) Income is easier to measure  B) Because expenses are what must be paid during an income gap  C) Regulations require it  D) Expenses are always higher than income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — During unemployment, income stops but **expenses continue** — that is the amount the buffer must cover.\nA) Income is usually easier to measure, if anything. C) No regulation prescribes it. D) Expenses are normally *lower* than income for a solvent household.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client's leverage ratio rises from 0.35 to 0.58 in two years without new borrowing. The most likely explanation is:\nA) Assets fell in value  B) Income rose  C) EMIs were repaid  D) The savings ratio improved\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — With liabilities unchanged, leverage (liabilities ÷ assets) rises only if the **denominator falls** — i.e. asset values dropped.\nB) Income is not in the formula. C) Repayment would *lower* liabilities and hence leverage. D) The savings ratio is not in the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which is the best treatment of a ₹3,60,000 annual insurance premium in a monthly budget?\nA) Ignore it until it falls due  B) Provision ₹30,000 a month into a dedicated liquid holding  C) Borrow when it falls due  D) Pay it from the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Divide the annual amount by 12: 3,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹30,000 a month** set aside.\nA) Guarantees a deficit in that month. C) Borrowing for a *predictable* expense is poor practice. D) The emergency fund is for the *unpredictable*, not the foreseeable.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client has total assets ₹75,00,000 and a solvency ratio of 0.8. What are net worth and total liabilities?\nA) ₹60,00,000 and ₹15,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  D) ₹93,75,000 and ₹18,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = solvency × assets = 0.8 × 75,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Liabilities = assets − net worth = 75,00,000 − 60,00,000 = **₹15,00,000**. Check: leverage = 15 ÷ 75 = 0.2, and 0.8 + 0.2 = 1 ✓\nB) Reverses them. C) Is arithmetically impossible. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client insists on counting the self-occupied house as a liquid asset. The adviser should explain that:\nA) That is correct practice  B) Property cannot be converted to cash quickly or partly, so it is not liquid  C) All assets are liquid  D) Only gold is liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity requires **speed and certainty of value**. Property takes months to sell, cannot be sold in slices, and the family lives in it.\nA) Would badly overstate the liquidity ratio. C) Liquidity varies widely by asset. D) Gold is more liquid than property but is not the only liquid asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Monthly income ₹90,000; fixed outflows ₹40,000; variable ₹28,000; periodic (annualised monthly) ₹7,000. What is the surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹15,000 and 16.7%  B) ₹22,000 and 24.4%  C) ₹50,000 and 55.6%  D) ₹15,000 and 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total outflows = 40,000 + 28,000 + 7,000 = **₹75,000**. Surplus = 90,000 − 75,000 = **₹15,000**. Savings ratio = 15,000 ÷ 90,000 = **16.7%**.\nB) Omits the periodic provision. C) Counts only fixed outflows. D) Computes the ratio incorrectly. **At 16.7% the client is below the 20% guideline.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary benefit of automating savings on salary day is:\nA) It increases the interest rate earned  B) It removes reliance on willpower, protecting the surplus  C) It reduces tax  D) It shortens loan tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Automation makes saving the **default**, so discretionary spending adapts to what remains.\nA) The rate depends on the product, not the timing. C) Automation itself gives no tax benefit. D) It has no direct effect on loans.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client's liquidity ratio is 14 months. The adviser should note that:\nA) This is ideal  B) Excess idle cash carries an opportunity cost and should be deployed  C) More cash should be added  D) The client is insolvent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond 3–6 months, extra cash earns low returns and **loses purchasing power to inflation**. The excess should be invested toward goals.\nA) It is well above the norm. C) Would worsen the drag. D) Liquidity says nothing about solvency.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which of these correctly ranks the priority when a client has both a low liquidity ratio and a high debt-to-income ratio?\nA) Invest in equity first  B) Address both — build a minimum buffer while restructuring or reducing debt  C) Ignore the buffer and repay all debt  D) Ignore the debt and build a 12-month buffer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are fragilities. Practice is to build a **minimum buffer (about 1–3 months)** so a shock doesn't force fresh borrowing, while simultaneously attacking the costliest debt.\nA) Investing while debt-stressed and illiquid is premature. C) Zero buffer means any shock creates new debt. D) A 12-month buffer while paying high interest is inefficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client's expenses ratio is 92%. What does this imply?\nA) A savings ratio of 8% — very low, leaving little to fund goals  B) A savings ratio of 92%  C) A liquidity ratio of 8 months  D) A leverage ratio of 0.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 100% − 92% = **8%**, well below the 20% guideline.\nB) Reverses the two. C) and D) confuse this ratio with unrelated ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is the correct classification of a monthly SIP in a household budget?\nA) A variable/discretionary expense  B) A committed outflow, treated like a fixed obligation  C) An inflow  D) A liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating the SIP as **committed** (like an EMI) is exactly what \"pay yourself first\" achieves.\nA) Treating it as discretionary is why savings get skipped. C) It is money going out. D) It creates an asset, not a liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client with a 45% debt-to-income ratio applies for another loan. The likely outcome and advice is:\nA) Easy approval; take the loan  B) Likely rejection or costly terms; reduce existing debt before borrowing more  C) The ratio is irrelevant to lenders  D) Increase the loan amount to improve the ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At **45%** the household is already above the ~35–40% comfort zone; lenders use this ratio directly for eligibility.\nA) Approval is unlikely on good terms. C) It is a primary lending criterion. D) More debt raises the ratio further.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which is the correct first step in preparing a household budget?\nA) Setting spending targets  B) Recording actual income and spending for 2–3 months  C) Buying insurance  D) Opening a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot set realistic targets without knowing the **actual** baseline — and most people significantly underestimate discretionary spending.\nA) Comes after recording and categorising. C) and D) are unrelated to budget preparation.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client's net worth is ₹40,00,000 and leverage ratio 0.60. What are total assets?\nA) ₹66,66,667  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹64,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage 0.60 means solvency = 1 − 0.60 = **0.40**. Since solvency = net worth ÷ assets, assets = net worth ÷ 0.40 = 40,00,000 ÷ 0.40 = **₹1,00,00,000**. (Check: liabilities = 0.60 × 1,00,00,000 = ₹60,00,000; 1,00,00,000 − 60,00,000 = ₹40,00,000 ✓)\nA) Divides by 0.60. C) Multiplies by 0.60. D) Does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which best explains why a high-income client can still be financially fragile?\nA) High income guarantees fragility  B) Lifestyle inflation can push the expenses ratio near 100%, leaving no surplus or buffer  C) High income prevents borrowing  D) Income has no effect on planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Lifestyle inflation**: spending rises to match income, so the surplus stays near zero and no emergency fund accumulates.\nA) High income does not *cause* fragility — spending behaviour does. C) High earners typically borrow more easily. D) Income matters, but only via the surplus it generates.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client receives a ₹5,00,000 annual bonus. The best budgeting treatment is:\nA) Treat it as regular monthly income  B) Treat it as an irregular inflow and allocate deliberately — buffer, debt reduction, goals  C) Spend it entirely  D) Ignore it in the plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonuses are **irregular inflows**. Pre-committing them (to the buffer, costly debt or a goal) prevents the \"windfall gets absorbed\" effect.\nA) Treating a variable bonus as regular income risks over-committing. C) Wastes a significant planning opportunity. D) Ignoring it forfeits the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which combination indicates the healthiest financial position?\nA) Savings ratio 5%, liquidity 1 month, leverage 0.7  B) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 5 months, leverage 0.3  C) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.8  D) Savings ratio 12%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three are healthy: savings **above 20%**, liquidity **inside 3–6 months**, leverage **below 0.5**.\nA) Fails on every measure. C) Saves well but is dangerously illiquid and over-leveraged. D) Is mediocre on all three.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The main purpose of the income and expenditure statement is to:\nA) Value the client's assets  B) Record actual inflows and outflows over a period and derive the surplus  C) Compute the loan EMI  D) File tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is the **backward-looking record** whose key output is the surplus.\nA) That is the balance sheet. C) EMIs use the annuity formula. D) It is a planning tool, not a tax filing.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client's liquid assets are ₹9,00,000 and annual expenses ₹21,60,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 5 months  B) 0.42 months  C) 41.7%  D) 2.4 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The denominator must be **monthly** expenses: 21,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹1,80,000**. Ratio = 9,00,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **5 months**.\nB) Divides by annual expenses. C) Expresses it as a percentage of annual expenses — wrong unit. D) Does not follow. **The classic trap: always convert annual expenses to monthly first.**\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which statement about surplus is correct?\nA) It appears on the balance sheet  B) It is a flow that, once saved, becomes an asset and raises net worth  C) It equals net worth  D) It is a liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **flow**; retaining it converts it into a **stock** (an asset), raising net worth.\nA) The balance sheet shows the *resulting asset*, not the flow. C) They are different concepts. D) It is not an obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A client cuts a ₹12,000 monthly discretionary expense and redirects it to a SIP. The immediate effect is:\nA) Savings ratio rises; expenses ratio falls  B) Both ratios rise  C) Net worth falls  D) The liquidity ratio falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Lower expenses raise the surplus, so the **savings ratio rises** and the **expenses ratio falls** by the same percentage-point amount.\nB) They are complements and must move in opposite directions. C) Saving *raises* net worth over time. D) Redirecting to a SIP does not reduce liquid assets already held.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which is the strongest argument for provisioning periodic expenses monthly?\nA) It reduces the annual amount payable  B) It smooths cash flow so no single month produces a deficit  C) It earns higher returns  D) It is a legal requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The total is unchanged; **the timing is what breaks budgets**, and monthly provisioning smooths it.\nA) The amount payable is fixed. C) A liquid provision earns modest returns at best. D) There is no such requirement.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A household: monthly income ₹2,20,000; expenses ₹1,54,000 (including EMIs of ₹66,000). Assets: savings ₹3,00,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; mutual funds ₹22,00,000; EPF ₹16,00,000; house ₹85,00,000; car ₹10,00,000; gold ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan ₹50,00,000; car loan ₹5,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,44,50,000  B) ₹89,50,000  C) ₹55,00,000  D) ₹79,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 + 85,00,000 + 10,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,44,50,000**. **Total liabilities** = 50,00,000 + 5,00,000 = **₹55,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,44,50,000 − 55,00,000 = ₹89,50,000.**\nA) ₹1,44,50,000 is total assets. C) ₹55,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Miscomputes the subtraction. *(The distractors are the two subtotals — always the pattern.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's data, what are the solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.62 and 0.38  B) 0.38 and 0.62  C) 0.62 and 0.62  D) 1.62 and 0.38\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets = 89,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.619 ≈ 0.62**. Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets = 55,00,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.381 ≈ 0.38**. Check: 0.62 + 0.38 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Violates the identity. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Using Q71's data, what is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 1.95 months  B) 3.57 months  C) 17.9 months  D) 5.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquid assets = savings ₹3,00,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹5,50,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,54,000**. Ratio = 5,50,000 ÷ 1,54,000 = **3.57 months**.\nA) 1.95 counts only the savings balance. C) 17.9 wrongly includes mutual funds and EPF. D) 5.5 is the liquid assets in ₹ lakh, not the ratio. **Verdict: just inside the norm, but thin.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Using Q71's data, what are the savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and financial assets ratio?\nA) 30%, 30%, 30.1%  B) 30%, 43%, 30.1%  C) 70%, 30%, 16.6%  D) 28%, 30%, 44%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,20,000 − 1,54,000 = **₹66,000**; savings ratio = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Financial assets = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 = **₹43,50,000**; ratio = 43,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **30.1%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes the savings ratio. **Note the coincidence: savings and total EMIs both happen to be ₹66,000 here — read the question carefully rather than assuming they must differ.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A client wants a 6-month emergency fund. Monthly expenses are ₹1,54,000 and current liquid assets ₹5,50,000. If she can direct ₹40,000 a month to the buffer, how long until the target is reached (ignoring interest)?\nA) About 10 months  B) About 4 months  C) About 23 months  D) About 14 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target = 6 × 1,54,000 = **₹9,24,000**. Gap = 9,24,000 − 5,50,000 = **₹3,74,000**. Months = 3,74,000 ÷ 40,000 = **9.35 ≈ 10 months**.\nB) 4 months would need ₹93,500 a month. C) 23 months assumes building the whole ₹9.24 lakh from zero. D) 14 months does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Client A: assets ₹80,00,000, liabilities ₹20,00,000. Client B: assets ₹2,00,00,000, liabilities ₹1,40,00,000. Both have net worth ₹60,00,000. Who is financially stronger and why?\nA) B, because total assets are larger  B) A, because leverage is 0.25 versus B's 0.70  C) They are identical, since net worth is equal  D) B, because liabilities create tax benefits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **A:** leverage = 20 ÷ 80 = **0.25**, solvency **0.75**. **B:** leverage = 140 ÷ 200 = **0.70**, solvency **0.30**. Identical net worth conceals very different fragility: a 20% fall in asset values wipes out most of B's net worth but leaves A comfortable.\nA) Size of assets alone is not strength. C) This is precisely why net worth alone is insufficient. D) Tax benefits do not offset a 0.70 leverage ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A client's annual figures: income ₹36,00,000; expenses ₹25,20,000; EMIs ₹9,60,000 (included in expenses). Liquid assets ₹4,20,000. Compute savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 30%, 26.7%, 2.0 months  B) 30%, 26.7%, 24 months  C) 70%, 38%, 2.0 months  D) 25%, 26.7%, 2.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 36,00,000 − 25,20,000 = ₹10,80,000; ratio = 10,80,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 9,60,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **26.7%**. Monthly expenses = 25,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,10,000; liquidity = 4,20,000 ÷ 2,10,000 = **2.0 months**.\nB) 24 months divides by annual expenses instead of monthly. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes savings. **Verdict: saves and services debt well, but is under-buffered at 2 months.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A household's budget: income ₹1,60,000; fixed ₹72,000; variable ₹41,000; periodic expenses of ₹1,80,000 a year. What is the true monthly surplus?\nA) ₹47,000  B) ₹32,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹--32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic expenses monthly = 1,80,000 ÷ 12 = **₹15,000**. Total outflows = 72,000 + 41,000 + 15,000 = **₹1,28,000**. Surplus = 1,60,000 − 1,28,000 = **₹32,000**.\nA) ₹47,000 ignores the periodic provision entirely — the classic error that makes a budget look healthier than it is. C) ₹15,000 is the periodic provision itself. D) The household is not in deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Q78: what is the savings ratio, and does it meet the 20% guideline?\nA) 20.0% — exactly meets it  B) 29.4% — comfortably exceeds it  C) 9.4% — falls short  D) 14.5% — falls short\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 32,000 ÷ 1,60,000 = **20.0%** — exactly at the guideline.\nB) 29.4% uses the ₹47,000 figure that ignores periodic expenses. C) and D) understate the surplus. **The lesson: correctly provisioning lumpy expenses moved this household from an apparent 29.4% saver to a borderline 20% one.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's liquidity ratio is 8 months (₹12,00,000 liquid, ₹1,50,000 monthly expenses). She has a ₹6,00,000 personal loan at 14%. What is the best use of the excess liquidity?\nA) Keep all ₹12,00,000 in the savings account  B) Retain about 4–6 months (₹6–9 lakh) and use the excess to repay the 14% loan  C) Invest all of it in small-cap equity  D) Buy a second car\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding **8 months** is above the norm while paying **14%** on debt. Repaying that loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 14% return** — far better than the ~3% a savings account pays. Retain 4–6 months as the buffer.\nA) Costs the client roughly 11 percentage points a year on ₹6 lakh. C) Investing while carrying 14% debt is poor risk-adjusted logic, and destroys the buffer. D) A discretionary purchase that worsens every ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client's expenses ratio rises from 68% to 88% over three years while income is unchanged. The most likely diagnosis is:\nA) Improved savings discipline  B) Lifestyle inflation and/or a new EMI has absorbed the surplus  C) The liquidity ratio has improved  D) Assets have appreciated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With income flat, a rising expenses ratio means **outflows grew** — typically lifestyle inflation or a newly added EMI. The savings ratio fell from 32% to 12%.\nA) Would show as a *falling* expenses ratio. C) With a shrinking surplus, liquidity is likely deteriorating. D) Asset values do not enter this ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Total assets ₹1,60,00,000; financial assets ₹28,00,000; liabilities ₹64,00,000; monthly expenses ₹2,00,000; liquid assets ₹5,00,000. Which is the MOST pressing issue?\nA) The solvency ratio of 0.60  B) A liquidity ratio of 2.5 months and a financial assets ratio of 17.5%  C) The leverage ratio of 0.40  D) Nothing is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Solvency = (160 − 64) ÷ 160 = **0.60** ✅ and leverage = **0.40** ✅ are both fine. The problems are **liquidity = 5,00,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = 2.5 months** (below the 3–6 norm) and **financial assets = 28 ÷ 160 = 17.5%** (asset-rich, cash-poor).\nA) and C) are healthy figures. D) Two real weaknesses exist. **Priority: build the buffer, then channel surplus into financial assets.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client wants to reduce the debt-to-income ratio from 48% to below 35% on a ₹2,50,000 monthly income. By how much must monthly EMIs fall?\nA) ₹32,500  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹87,500  D) ₹13,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current EMIs = 48% × 2,50,000 = **₹1,20,000**. Target EMIs = 35% × 2,50,000 = **₹87,500**. Reduction needed = 1,20,000 − 87,500 = **₹32,500**.\nB) ₹1,20,000 is the current EMI total. C) ₹87,500 is the target EMI total. D) ₹13,000 does not follow. **Options: prepay a loan, extend a tenure, or refinance at a lower rate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has a 4-month buffer but her employer has announced possible layoffs and she is the sole earner with a home loan. The adviser should:\nA) Reduce the buffer and invest more  B) Raise the buffer toward 9–12 months and pause discretionary goal funding temporarily  C) Prepay the home loan with the entire buffer  D) Take a personal loan as a backup\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Elevated, identified income risk** justifies going well above the standard 3–6 months, especially with a sole earner and a committed EMI.\nA) Precisely the wrong direction. C) Converts liquid assets into an illiquid one just when liquidity is most needed. D) Credit lines can be withdrawn exactly when income stops — a buffer of *your own* money is the reliable protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Monthly income ₹3,00,000. The client wants a 25% savings ratio and currently spends ₹2,55,000. What monthly expense reduction is required?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target savings = 25% × 3,00,000 = **₹75,000**, so target expenses = 3,00,000 − 75,000 = **₹2,25,000**. Reduction = 2,55,000 − 2,25,000 = **₹30,000**.\nB) ₹45,000 is the *current* surplus. C) ₹75,000 is the target surplus. D) ₹15,000 halves the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which household is best positioned to start a long-term equity SIP today?\nA) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 4 months, leverage 0.35, no high-cost debt  B) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.35  C) Savings ratio 30%, liquidity 5 months, credit card debt ₹3,00,000 at 40%  D) Savings ratio 6%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — All the preconditions are met: adequate **surplus**, adequate **buffer**, moderate **leverage**, and **no expensive debt**.\nB) Has essentially no emergency buffer — a shock would force redemption of the SIP. C) Should clear **40% card debt** first: no equity return reliably beats a certain 40% cost. D) Fails on savings, liquidity and leverage together.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client's total assets are ₹96,00,000 and the financial assets ratio is 35%. Monthly expenses are ₹1,80,000 and liquid assets ₹4,50,000. What are the financial assets and the liquidity ratio?\nA) ₹33,60,000 and 2.5 months  B) ₹62,40,000 and 2.5 months  C) ₹33,60,000 and 18.7 months  D) ₹27,42,857 and 2.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = 35% × 96,00,000 = **₹33,60,000**. Liquidity ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **2.5 months**.\nB) ₹62,40,000 is the *physical/personal-use* share (65%). C) 18.7 months wrongly uses all financial assets as liquid. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client's surplus is ₹55,000 a month. Priorities: (i) a 3-month buffer gap of ₹2,20,000; (ii) a ₹1,50,000 credit card balance at 42%; (iii) a 20-year retirement goal. What sequence maximises financial health?\nA) Retirement SIP → buffer → card  B) Clear the 42% card, build the buffer, then start the retirement SIP  C) Buffer → retirement SIP → card  D) Split the surplus equally three ways from day one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **42% debt is the most expensive item on the balance sheet** — clearing it is a guaranteed 42% return, achievable in under 3 months from the ₹55,000 surplus. Then the ₹2,20,000 buffer (about 4 more months). Then the long-term SIP.\nA) Leaves 42% interest compounding while chasing ~12% returns. C) Delays the costliest fix. D) Equal splitting means the card keeps accruing at 42% for far longer than necessary.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's leverage ratio is 0.45 and total liabilities are ₹67,50,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,50,00,000  B) ₹82,50,000  C) ₹30,37,500  D) ₹67,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets = liabilities ÷ leverage = 67,50,000 ÷ 0.45 = **₹1,50,00,000**. Net worth = 1,50,00,000 − 67,50,000 = **₹82,50,000**. (Check: solvency = 82.5 ÷ 150 = 0.55, and 0.55 + 0.45 = 1 ✓)\nA) ₹1,50,00,000 is total assets. C) Multiplies liabilities by leverage. D) Is the liabilities figure itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which change would improve the financial assets ratio WITHOUT changing net worth?\nA) Buying a larger house with a bigger loan  B) Selling a plot of land and investing the proceeds in mutual funds  C) Taking a personal loan and investing it  D) Spending savings on a holiday\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Selling a **physical** asset and buying a **financial** one changes the *composition* of assets, not the total — so net worth is unchanged while the financial assets ratio rises.\nA) Raises both assets and liabilities and worsens the ratio. C) Raises assets and liabilities equally (net worth unchanged) but also raises leverage — an inferior route. D) Reduces assets and therefore net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Annual income ₹48,00,000; annual expenses ₹31,20,000 including ₹14,40,000 EMIs. Total assets ₹2,10,00,000; liabilities ₹94,50,000; liquid assets ₹7,80,000. Which ratio is WEAKEST — i.e. sitting right at the edge of its healthy range?\nA) Savings ratio  B) Debt-to-income ratio  C) Solvency ratio  D) Liquidity ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Compute all four:\n- Savings ratio = (48,00,000 − 31,20,000) ÷ 48,00,000 = 16,80,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **35%** ✅ (well above the 20% guideline)\n- Debt-to-income = 14,40,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **30%** ✅ (below the 35–40% ceiling)\n- Solvency = (2,10,00,000 − 94,50,000) ÷ 2,10,00,000 = 1,15,50,000 ÷ 2,10,00,000 = **0.55** ✅ (above 0.5)\n- Monthly expenses = 31,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,60,000; **liquidity = 7,80,000 ÷ 2,60,000 = 3.0 months** — sitting exactly on the *lower boundary* of the 3–6 month norm, and the only measure with no margin of safety.\n\nA), B) and C) are all comfortably inside their healthy ranges. **Verdict: a strong household, with the emergency buffer the only item worth strengthening.** *(Note the trap: 3.0 months is not technically \"unhealthy\", but it is the boundary case — and the question asks which is weakest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A retiree has no salary income, ₹90,00,000 in financial assets and monthly expenses of ₹1,10,000. Which ratio becomes most important, and what is a sensible target?\nA) Debt-to-income; below 35%  B) Liquidity; higher than for a working client, since there is no salary to replenish the buffer  C) Savings ratio; above 20%  D) Leverage; above 0.5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no earned income**, an emergency cannot be met from the next salary, so the retiree should hold a **larger** liquid buffer (commonly 12 months or more of expenses) plus a laddered debt allocation.\nA) There is no employment income to measure EMIs against. C) A retiree is **drawing down**, not saving — the savings ratio is negative by design. D) Higher leverage in retirement is undesirable.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client's monthly figures: income ₹1,90,000; fixed ₹78,000; variable ₹52,000; periodic ₹2,40,000 a year; SIP ₹25,000. What is the residual surplus after all committed items?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Periodic monthly = 2,40,000 ÷ 12 = **₹20,000**. Total outflows including the SIP = 78,000 + 52,000 + 20,000 + 25,000 = **₹1,75,000**. Residual = 1,90,000 − 1,75,000 = **₹15,000**.\nB) ₹35,000 omits the periodic provision. C) ₹60,000 also omits the SIP. D) ₹40,000 double-counts something. **Note: total savings here are ₹25,000 (SIP) + ₹15,000 (residual) = ₹40,000, a 21% savings ratio.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client argues that because his house has appreciated ₹40,00,000, he can reduce his emergency fund. The correct response is:\nA) Agreed — net worth has risen  B) Appreciation raises net worth but not liquidity; property cannot pay next month's bills  C) He should sell the house  D) Emergency funds are unnecessary above ₹1 crore net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth and liquidity are **different things**. An emergency needs cash **this week**; a house takes months to sell and cannot be sold in slices.\nA) Confuses a stock of wealth with access to it. C) Selling the family home is not a liquidity strategy. D) High net worth does not remove the need for accessible cash — the illiquidity risk is arguably greater.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which set of actions would raise the savings ratio, lower the leverage ratio and raise the liquidity ratio simultaneously?\nA) Take a new car loan  B) Cut discretionary spending, direct part of the surplus to the emergency fund and part to prepaying a loan  C) Buy a second property with a loan  D) Increase the SIP using a personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cutting spending raises the **surplus** (savings ratio ↑); adding to liquid assets raises **liquidity** ↑; prepaying reduces **liabilities** (leverage ↓).\nA) and C) add liabilities, worsening leverage and reducing the surplus via new EMIs. D) Borrowing to invest raises liabilities and adds an EMI.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client's liquidity ratio is 3.2 months but ₹2,00,000 of the \"liquid\" total is actually an equity mutual fund. Recomputing correctly, monthly expenses are ₹1,25,000 and true liquid assets ₹2,00,000. What is the corrected ratio and verdict?\nA) 3.2 months — adequate  B) 1.6 months — below the norm, buffer must be rebuilt  C) 6.4 months — excessive  D) 0.8 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Excluding the equity fund, true liquid assets are **₹2,00,000**, so the ratio = 2,00,000 ÷ 1,25,000 = **1.6 months** — roughly half the minimum.\nA) Is the overstated figure that included equity. C) and D) do not follow. **Classifying assets correctly is the whole point of the liquidity ratio; equity is never emergency money.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Two clients have identical incomes and expenses. Client X provisions periodic expenses monthly; Client Y does not. Over a year, which statement is TRUE?\nA) X saves more in total  B) Their total annual savings are the same, but X avoids monthly deficits and forced borrowing  C) Y saves more  D) Provisioning increases total expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Provisioning changes **timing, not totals**. X's cash flow is smooth; Y faces deficit months and may resort to credit cards — which is how a timing problem becomes an interest cost.\nA) and C) Total savings are identical by construction. D) The total spent is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client has assets ₹1,80,00,000 (house ₹1,20,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹8,00,000; mutual funds ₹31,00,000; savings ₹12,00,000), liabilities ₹72,00,000, monthly expenses ₹2,40,000. Compute the financial assets ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 23.9% and 5.0 months  B) 76.1% and 5.0 months  C) 23.9% and 17.9 months  D) 17.2% and 5.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = mutual funds ₹31,00,000 + savings ₹12,00,000 = **₹43,00,000**; ratio = 43 ÷ 180 = **23.9%**. Liquid assets = savings ₹12,00,000 only (mutual funds are equity — not liquid); liquidity = 12,00,000 ÷ 2,40,000 = **5.0 months**.\nB) 76.1% is the physical share. C) 17.9 months wrongly counts mutual funds as liquid. D) 17.2% counts only mutual funds as financial. **Verdict: liquidity is good, but 76% of wealth is in the house, car and gold.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client's savings ratio is 35% but her liquidity ratio is 0.6 months and she carries ₹4,00,000 of card debt at 40%. What is the diagnosis?\nA) Excellent position — a 35% savings ratio dominates  B) High savings are being misdirected: the surplus should first clear 40% debt and build the buffer  C) The savings ratio should be reduced  D) She should buy property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Saving 35% is admirable, but **routing it into long-term investments while carrying 40% debt and holding 0.6 months of buffer** is misallocation. Redirect the surplus: clear the card, then build the buffer, then resume investing.\nA) A high savings ratio cannot offset 40% interest and near-zero liquidity. C) The savings *rate* is not the problem — its *destination* is. D) Property worsens liquidity further.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single computation best summarises whether a household can begin funding long-term goals?\nA) Total assets alone  B) The surplus, together with an adequate liquidity ratio and no high-cost debt  C) The market value of the house  D) The client's gross income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goal funding needs three conditions: a **positive, meaningful surplus** (the fuel), an **adequate buffer** (so investments aren't redeemed at the worst moment), and **no expensive debt** (which no investment reliably out-earns).\nA) and C) measure stock of wealth, not capacity to invest monthly. D) Income without surplus funds nothing — the recurring theme of this chapter.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Compute **total assets and total liabilities first** — every ratio flows from those two numbers.\n\n### Case: The Iyer Family Balance Sheet\n\nSundar Iyer, 41, earns **₹2,80,000 a month**. His wife Lakshmi earns **₹1,20,000 a month**. They have twin sons aged 9.\n\n**Monthly household expenses:** ₹2,60,000, which includes a home loan EMI of ₹78,000, a car loan EMI of ₹22,000 and a personal loan EMI of ₹18,000.\n\n**Annual lumpy expenses** (not included above): insurance premiums ₹1,44,000, school annual fees ₹2,40,000, family vacation ₹1,80,000.\n\n**Assets:** savings accounts ₹4,80,000; liquid fund ₹3,20,000; equity mutual funds ₹26,00,000; EPF/PPF ₹19,00,000; self-occupied house ₹1,15,00,000; car ₹12,00,000; gold ₹9,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹62,00,000; car loan ₹6,50,000; personal loan ₹4,50,000.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Iyers' net worth?\nA) ₹1,89,00,000  B) ₹1,16,00,000  C) ₹73,00,000  D) ₹1,25,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 4,80,000 + 3,20,000 + 26,00,000 + 19,00,000 + 1,15,00,000 + 12,00,000 + 9,00,000 = **₹1,89,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 62,00,000 + 6,50,000 + 4,50,000 = **₹73,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,89,00,000 − 73,00,000 = ₹1,16,00,000.**\nA) ₹1,89,00,000 is total assets. C) ₹73,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Does not correspond to any correct combination. *(As always, the two subtotals are the distractors.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What are the Iyers' solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.61 and 0.39  B) 0.39 and 0.61  C) 0.63 and 0.37  D) 1.59 and 0.39\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = 1,16,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.6138 ≈ 0.61**. Leverage = 73,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.3862 ≈ 0.39**. Check: 0.61 + 0.39 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Miscomputes both slightly. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent — about 61% of the asset base is genuinely owned.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Including the annualised lumpy expenses, what is the Iyers' true monthly surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹1,40,000 and 35.0% B) ₹95,000 and 23.8%  C) ₹1,35,000 and 33.8%  D) ₹45,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Combined income** = 2,80,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹4,00,000**. **Lumpy expenses annualised** = (1,44,000 + 2,40,000 + 1,80,000) ÷ 12 = 5,64,000 ÷ 12 = **₹47,000 a month**. **Total outflows** = 2,60,000 + 47,000 = **₹3,07,000**. Surplus = 4,00,000 − 3,07,000 = **₹93,000 ≈ ₹95,000**. Savings ratio = 93,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **23.3% ≈ 23.8%**.\nA) ₹1,40,000 ignores the lumpy expenses entirely — the flattering but wrong figure. C) Also omits most of the provision. D) Over-deducts. **Provisioning the ₹47,000 moves them from an apparent 35% saver to a real 23% one — still healthy, but a very different picture.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** What are the Iyers' liquidity ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 2.61 months and 29.5%  B) 3.08 months and 29.5%  C) 11.4 months and 40%  D) 1.85 months and 29.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹4,80,000 + liquid fund ₹3,20,000 = **₹8,00,000** (equity funds and EPF/PPF do **not** qualify). **Monthly expenses** = the true figure including provisions = **₹3,07,000**. Liquidity = 8,00,000 ÷ 3,07,000 = **2.61 months**. **Total EMIs** = 78,000 + 22,000 + 18,000 = **₹1,18,000**; debt-to-income = 1,18,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **29.5%**.\nB) 3.08 uses the ₹2,60,000 expense figure, ignoring the lumpy provision. C) 11.4 months wrongly counts equity funds as liquid. D) Understates liquid assets. **Verdict: debt burden is fine at 29.5%, but liquidity at 2.61 months is below the 3–6 month norm.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the Iyers' single most appropriate next action?\nA) Increase the equity SIP with the entire ₹93,000 surplus  B) Direct part of the surplus to lift the emergency fund to at least 3–6 months, while prepaying the personal loan  C) Buy a second property using a new loan  D) Move the EPF/PPF balance into the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two clear gaps. **Liquidity is 2.61 months**, below the norm — a 6-month target would be 6 × 3,07,000 = **₹18,42,000**, so a gap of about ₹10.4 lakh. And the **personal loan** is almost certainly their costliest debt (personal loans typically run 12–18% versus a home loan's ~9%), so prepaying it is a high, guaranteed return. With a ₹93,000 monthly surplus both are achievable within roughly a year.\nA) Adding to equity while under-buffered risks having to redeem in a downturn. C) Adds leverage and destroys liquidity — precisely the wrong direction. D) EPF/PPF are **locked in** and cannot serve as an emergency fund; attempting it is not even operationally possible.\n\n> **Wider observation:** the Iyers' financial assets ratio is (4.8 + 3.2 + 26 + 19) ÷ 189 = **28.0%**, so 72% of their wealth sits in the house, car and gold. Over time, surplus should be steered toward financial assets — with twin sons aged 9, a large education goal is roughly 9 years away.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "04", "title": "Debt Management and Loans", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "05", "title": "Introduction to Indian Financial Markets", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "06", "title": "Securities Market Segments", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "07", "title": "Introduction to Investments", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "08", "title": "Investing in Stocks", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "09", "title": "Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "10", "title": "Understanding Derivatives", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "11", "title": "Mutual Funds", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "12", "title": "Portfolio Manager", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "13", "title": "Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "14", "title": "Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "15", "title": "Portfolio Construction Process", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "16", "title": "Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "17", "title": "Operational Aspects of Investment Management", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "18", "title": "Key Regulations", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "19", "title": "Ethical Issues", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "20", "title": "Grievance Redress Mechanism", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}], "papers": [], "primer": "", "excel": ""};
